window.ENGLISH_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"comprehension","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Reading comprehension: finding answers in the passage","body":"READING COMPREHENSION — UNDERSTANDING WHAT A PASSAGE ACTUALLY SAYS\n\nINTRODUCTION\nComprehension is the ability to read a passage, identify the writer's message, and answer questions using evidence from the passage. In UTME Use of English, the correct answer is not necessarily the statement you agree with in everyday life. It is the answer supported by the particular text. A passage may be narrative (telling a story), descriptive (painting a picture), expository (explaining a subject) or argumentative (defending a position). Recognising the purpose helps you interpret details.\n\n1. THE MAIN IDEA AND SUPPORTING DETAILS\nTopic, main idea and detail:\nTopic: School gardens.\nMain idea: A school garden helps pupils learn practical agriculture.\nSupporting detail (i): Pupils learn how to prepare nursery beds.\nSupporting detail (ii): They observe how crops respond to watering.\nSupporting detail (iii): They learn to identify pests.\nNotice that 'school gardens' names a subject, while the main idea says something important about that subject.\n\nExample (i)\nPassage: 'The village clinic received new equipment in January. Nurses were trained to use it in February. By March, patients no longer had to travel to another town for routine tests.'\nQuestion: Which statement best summarises the passage?\nA. The clinic opened in February.\nB. Better equipment and training improved access to local medical tests.\nC. Patients dislike travelling.\nD. Nurses work only in March.\nSolution: The three sentences show equipment, training and the resulting improvement in access. B covers all three. A invents an opening date; C and D are not supported.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nThe main idea is the central point the writer wants you to understand. Supporting details are examples, reasons, descriptions, statistics or incidents that develop that point. A topic is only the subject area; a main idea makes a statement about it. For example, 'public transport' is a topic, while 'reliable public transport reduces the cost of getting to work' is a main idea.\n\nHOW TO FIND THE MAIN IDEA\nRead the first and last sentences of each paragraph, but do not assume that the main idea must appear there. Ask: 'What claim connects most of these sentences?' Check whether the other sentences explain or illustrate your proposed answer. In a narrative, the central message may emerge through the characters' actions rather than a topic sentence.\n\nExample (ii)\nPassage: 'Every morning, commuters waited for buses that arrived without a timetable. Workers lost wages because they reached their offices late. When the town introduced a reliable schedule, lateness declined.'\nQuestion: What is the main idea?\nSolution: The first two sentences describe the problem, and the last sentence supplies the improvement. The passage mainly explains how dependable bus schedules reduce lateness, not how buses are manufactured.\n\n2. EXPLICIT AND IMPLIED INFORMATION\nA useful distinction is between a safe inference and a possible story. If a passage says, \"The school gate was locked when Chika arrived\", we know the gate was locked. We do not know whether Chika was late, whether the school was closed or who locked it. Several explanations are possible; none becomes an exam answer without supporting evidence.\n\nExample (iii)\nPassage: \"The baker increased production after customers began queuing before sunrise.\"\nQuestion: Why did production increase?\nSolution: The text links the increase to greater demand, shown by early queues. It does not say the baker bought a new oven or employed more staff.\n\nExample (iv)\nPassage: \"The bus stopped twice because its engine overheated, but it eventually reached the terminal.\"\nQuestion: Which conclusion is justified?\nSolution: The journey was interrupted by an engine problem, but the bus reached its destination. It is wrong to infer that passengers changed buses or that the engine was replaced.\n\nWhat is stated and what is inferred:\nSentence: 'Tunde wiped the wet bench before sitting down.'\nExplicit fact: The bench was wet.\nReasonable inference: Tunde wanted a dry place to sit.\nUnsupported guess: It rained all night. The bench might have become wet in another way.\n\nExample (v)\nPassage: 'Despite the heavy traffic, Zainab arrived before the interview began.'\nQuestion: Which statement is supported?\nA. Zainab missed the interview.\nB. There was no traffic.\nC. Zainab arrived on time despite a delay on the road.\nD. The interview was cancelled.\nSolution: 'Despite' introduces a difficulty that did not prevent her arrival. C follows from the text. We cannot assume how she travelled or exactly when she left.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExplicit information is stated directly. An inference is a conclusion reasonably drawn from clues in the text. An inference must not contradict the passage or depend on assumptions that the writer did not supply.\n\nExample (vi)\nPassage: 'Amaka checked the sky, carried an umbrella and covered her books with plastic before leaving.'\nQuestion: What can reasonably be inferred?\nSolution: Amaka expected rain or wanted to protect her books from possible rain. We cannot conclude that it had already started raining; the passage does not say so.\n\n3. MEANING OF WORDS IN CONTEXT\nOne word, two meanings:\n'The bank approved the loan.' Here 'bank' means a financial institution.\n'The children sat on the river bank.' Here 'bank' means land beside a river.\nThe surrounding words decide the meaning. This is why choosing a dictionary meaning without reading the sentence is unsafe.\n\nExample (vii)\n'After several failed attempts, the engineer devised a practical solution.'\nA. rejected B. invented C. copied D. forgot\nSolution: 'Devised' means thought up or created a plan or solution. 'Invented' is the closest option. Correct answer: B.\n\nA word can have several dictionary meanings. Select the meaning that fits its sentence. 'The principal addressed the assembly' means spoke to the assembly, not wrote an address on an envelope. Use the surrounding clause, the writer's attitude and the relationship between ideas to decide. Be careful with figurative language: 'The news was a bitter pill' refers to unpleasant news, not medicine.\n\nExample (viii)\nPassage: 'Although the task was arduous, the volunteers completed it before sunset.'\nQuestion: 'Arduous' most nearly means what?\nSolution: The word 'although' introduces a contrast between difficulty and successful completion. 'Arduous' means difficult or demanding, not enjoyable or short.\n\n4. WRITER'S PURPOSE, TONE AND ATTITUDE\nDo not confuse subject matter with attitude. Two writers can discuss the same school policy: one may welcome it, while another may question its fairness. Look at adjectives, contrasts, rhetorical questions and the final recommendation. The writer's purpose is the action the text is trying to perform; the tone is the feeling conveyed while doing it.\n\nExample (ix)\n\"The new clinic is a welcome improvement, especially for elderly residents who previously travelled for hours.\"\nQuestion: What is the writer's attitude?\nSolution: \"Welcome improvement\" and the benefit to elderly residents express approval, not neutrality or suspicion.\n\nExample (x)\n\"Must we wait for another accident before the authorities repair this crossing?\"\nQuestion: What is the purpose of the question?\nSolution: It is rhetorical: the writer criticises delay and urges action. It is not mainly a request for a timetable.\n\nSame topic, different tones:\nNeutral: 'The bridge was closed on Monday.' The writer reports a fact.\nConcerned: 'The bridge has remained unsafe for weeks; residents need protection.' The writer expresses worry.\nSarcastic: 'What a wonderful idea—to repair the bridge only after it collapses!' The words appear approving, but the intended meaning is critical.\n\nExample (xi)\n'If we continue to dump refuse into the drains, the next rainfall may flood our homes. We must act now.'\nQuestion: The writer's main purpose is to\nA. praise the drains B. warn and persuade residents C. entertain with a joke D. describe a wedding.\nSolution: The predicted danger and 'must act now' combine a warning with a call to action. Correct answer: B.\n\nPurpose asks why the writer wrote: to inform, persuade, entertain, warn, criticise, describe or explain. Tone describes the attitude communicated by the language: approving, sarcastic, concerned, optimistic, humorous, doubtful or critical. Do not label a serious warning 'humorous' simply because it contains an unusual image. Look for evaluative words and the passage's overall direction.\n\nExample (xii)\nPassage: 'The factory promised clean water, yet the river grew darker each month. Residents deserve an explanation.'\nQuestion: What is the writer's attitude?\nSolution: 'Yet' signals a contrast between the promise and the outcome. The demand for an explanation expresses criticism and concern.\n\n5. REFERENCE WORDS AND TEXT ORGANISATION\nWhen two nouns are possible antecedents, check number, meaning and the action described. A plural pronoun such as \"they\" generally refers to a plural noun or group; a singular \"it\" normally refers to one thing or idea. Sometimes \"this\" refers to a whole previous event rather than a single noun.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"The principal cancelled the trip because the weather was dangerous. This disappointed the pupils.\"\nQuestion: What does \"This\" refer to?\nSolution: It refers to the cancellation of the trip, not simply to \"the weather\". Substituting \"The cancellation disappointed the pupils\" preserves the meaning.\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"Farmers requested seeds and fertiliser; the latter arrived first.\"\nQuestion: Which item arrived first?\nSolution: Fertiliser. The former is the first item (seeds), while the latter is the second (fertiliser).\n\nReplace the reference word:\n'Bola bought a notebook and a pencil. She lost the latter.'\nFirst item = notebook. Second item = pencil. 'The latter' means the pencil.\nTest: 'Bola lost the pencil.' The sentence still makes sense.\n\nExample (xv)\n'The coach spoke to the players after they had lost the match.'\nQuestion: What does 'they' refer to?\nA. the coach B. the players C. the match D. the spectators\nSolution: Replace 'they' with each option. 'The players had lost the match' is grammatical and sensible. Correct answer: B.\n\nCONNECTIVE GUIDE\n'Because' → reason; 'therefore' → result; 'however' → contrast; 'furthermore' → addition; 'for instance' → example. These signals help you follow the writer's argument.\n\nPronouns and expressions such as 'this', 'those', 'the former' and 'the latter' refer to earlier ideas. The former means the first of two things mentioned; the latter means the second. A pronoun must agree sensibly with its referent. Connectives reveal structure: 'however' signals contrast, 'therefore' a conclusion, 'because' a cause, 'for example' an illustration and 'meanwhile' simultaneous activity.\n\nExample (xvi)\nPassage: 'The farmers requested irrigation equipment, but the council offered fertiliser. The former would help during droughts.'\nQuestion: What does 'the former' refer to?\nSolution: Of the two items, irrigation equipment is mentioned first. Therefore, 'the former' refers to irrigation equipment, not fertiliser.\n\n6. FACT, OPINION AND AUTHOR'S ASSUMPTION\nDistinguish three types of statement:\nFact claim: 'The debate began at 10 a.m.' It can be checked against a record.\nOpinion: 'It was the most exciting debate ever.' 'Most exciting' is a judgement.\nAssumption: 'More pupils will attend if the debate is moved to Saturday.' The speaker assumes Saturday is more convenient for the pupils, but has not shown evidence.\n\nExample (xvii)\n'The town should plant more trees because shade makes public spaces more comfortable.'\nQuestion: Which unstated assumption supports the recommendation?\nA. Trees can provide shade in public spaces.\nB. Every tree grows overnight.\nC. All towns already have forests.\nD. People never use public spaces.\nSolution: The argument depends on trees providing useful shade. A is the supporting assumption. The other claims are unnecessary or unreasonable. Correct answer: A.\n\nA fact is a statement that can in principle be checked. An opinion expresses a judgement. 'The school opened in 1998' is a checkable claim; 'It is the finest school in the country' is a judgement unless a clear measure is given. An assumption is an unstated idea on which the writer's argument depends. Questions may ask you to identify what the writer takes for granted.\n\n7. SUMMARY AND SUITABLE TITLE\nFor a multi-paragraph passage, write a five-word note for each paragraph, then look for the idea linking those notes. A good summary removes minor names, dates and illustrations unless they are essential to the argument. An answer that mentions only one striking incident may be accurate but too narrow.\n\nExample (xviii)\nPassage outline: Paragraph 1 explains how flooding disrupts transport; paragraph 2 describes damaged markets; paragraph 3 recommends better drainage.\nQuestion: Which title is best: \"A Market\", \"The Effects of Flooding and the Need for Drainage\", or \"Transport Fares\"?\nSolution: The second title covers the problems and proposed remedy across all three paragraphs. The others capture only one detail.\n\nBroad, narrow and suitable titles:\nPassage: 'A school introduced a reading hour, trained librarians and opened a book-lending service. Within a term, more pupils were borrowing books.'\nToo broad: 'Education in Africa'. This could refer to countless unrelated matters.\nToo narrow: 'Training Librarians'. This names only one detail.\nSuitable: 'How a School Encouraged Reading'. This covers the measures and their purpose.\n\nExample (xix)\nQuestion: Which is the best one-sentence summary of the passage?\nSolution: 'The school improved reading participation by combining a reading hour, librarian training and a lending service.' This includes the central action and result without copying every detail.\n\nA summary preserves the important meaning without minor details, repetition or personal comments. A good title is neither so broad that it could describe hundreds of unrelated passages nor so narrow that it names only one example. Prefer a title that covers the whole passage.\n\nHOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Read the questions first, then the passage, so you know what to look for.\n2. For each question, find the sentence that answers it. The right option usually rewords that sentence.\n3. Title questions: choose the option that covers the whole passage, not one paragraph.\n4. \"Refers to\" questions (what does \"those\" or \"it\" mean?): put your answer in place of the word and re-read the sentence. It should still make sense.\n5. \"Inferred / deduced\" means supported by the passage, even if not said word for word. It does not mean your opinion.\n\n8. A RELIABLE UTME ANSWERING METHOD\nFirst read the question to know what evidence to seek. Read the relevant paragraph carefully. Underline or mentally note the exact supporting phrase. Predict an answer in your own words before looking at options. Eliminate options that contradict the passage, exaggerate its claim ('always', 'never', 'only'), import outside knowledge or confuse cause with effect. Finally, reread the sentence in context before choosing.\n\nCOMMON EXAM TRAPS\nA familiar fact from the real world may still be unsupported by the passage. An answer may repeat several words from the text but change their relationship. Another may describe one paragraph correctly but fail to capture the passage as a whole. Do not confuse what the author reports someone saying with what the author personally believes.\n\nPRACTICE PASSAGE\n'The community library had few visitors until volunteers introduced weekend reading circles. Children came first, followed by parents who wanted to borrow books. Within six months, the library extended its opening hours.'\nCheck your understanding:\n1. What change initially attracted visitors?\n2. Who visited first?\n3. What happened after six months?\n4. What can you infer about community interest?\n5. Suggest a suitable title.\nAnswers and explanations: (1) Weekend reading circles; this is explicitly stated. (2) Children; 'came first' identifies them. (3) Opening hours were extended. (4) Interest grew, supported by the arrival of parents and extended hours; do not infer exact visitor numbers. (5) 'How Reading Circles Revived a Community Library' covers the central development.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nUse 'Practice JAMB Past Questions' below to apply these skills to actual comprehension passages. When an answer depends on a longer passage, read the whole passage before selecting an option."},"old":{"title":"Reading comprehension: finding answers in the passage","body":"JAMB comprehension questions test whether you can find and interpret what the passage actually says, not what you already believe.\n\nHow to answer:\n1. Read the questions first, then the passage, so you know what to look for.\n2. For each question, find the sentence that answers it. The right option usually rewords that sentence.\n3. Title questions: choose the option that covers the whole passage, not one paragraph.\n4. \"Refers to\" questions (what does \"those\" or \"it\" mean?): put your answer in place of the word and re-read the sentence. It should still make sense.\n5. \"Inferred / deduced\" means supported by the passage, even if not said word for word. It does not mean your opinion.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Options that copy passage words but change the meaning.\n• Options that reverse cause and effect.\n• Options that are true in real life but are not in the passage.\n• Options that go too far (\"all\", \"never\", \"no longer\" when the passage says \"many\").\n\nQuick check: The passage says \"many Nigerians stopped eating chicken.\" Can you choose \"Nigerians no longer eat chicken\"? Only if no better option exists, because \"many\" is not \"all\"."}},{"id":"cloze","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Cloze passages: filling gaps in a passage","body":"CLOZE PASSAGES — CHOOSING WORDS THAT FIT THE WHOLE TEXT\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA cloze passage is a continuous piece of writing with missing words or expressions. You choose the option that completes each gap most accurately. Unlike an isolated grammar question, a cloze test examines how words work together across sentences. It can test vocabulary, grammar, logical relationships, register, prepositions and fixed expressions.\n\n1. READ THE PASSAGE BEFORE FILLING ANY GAP\nThe surrounding sentence changes the answer:\n'Yesterday, Musa ___ to school.' The time marker 'yesterday' favours 'went'.\n'Every day, Musa ___ to school.' The habitual time marker favours 'goes'.\n'By the time the bell rang, Musa ___ to school.' The earlier completed action can be expressed as 'had gone'.\nA cloze gap is therefore not solved by memorising one word; you must read the whole context.\n\nExample (i)\n'The farmers waited for rain. ___, the clouds cleared and the day remained dry.'\nA. Fortunately B. However C. Therefore D. Similarly\nSolution: The second sentence contrasts their expectation with the dry weather. 'However' marks that contrast. Correct answer: B.\n\nRead the passage once to identify the subject, time frame and style. Is it about a past event, a scientific process or a formal announcement? Then read the sentence containing the blank and the sentences before and after it. A word that fits grammatically may still be wrong because it does not fit the writer's meaning.\n\n2. GRAMMATICAL AGREEMENT\nTo identify the subject, temporarily remove descriptive phrases. In \"The chairman, together with the members, is present\", remove \"together with the members\" and the sentence becomes \"The chairman is present\". By contrast, \"The chairman and the members are present\" has a compound subject. Also watch indefinite pronouns: \"Everyone is ready\"; \"Several are absent\".\n\nExample (ii)\n\"Neither the teacher nor the pupils ___ late.\"\nA. was B. were C. is D. has\nSolution: With \"neither ... nor\", the plural noun \"pupils\" nearest the verb calls for \"were\" in this past-time example. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (iii)\n\"The number of registered candidates ___ increasing.\"\nA. are B. is C. have D. were\nSolution: \"The number\" is singular; contrast \"A number of candidates are waiting.\" Correct answer: B.\n\nFind the true subject:\n'The box of pencils is missing.' The subject is 'box' (singular), not 'pencils'.\n'The boxes of pencils are missing.' The subject is 'boxes' (plural).\n'Neither of the two answers is acceptable.' In standard formal examination usage, 'neither' takes a singular verb.\n'The teacher and the principal are present.' Two distinct people form a plural subject.\n\nExample (iv)\n'The quality of these oranges ___ excellent.'\nA. are B. were C. is D. have\nSolution: The head noun is 'quality', which is singular. The phrase 'of these oranges' does not make it plural. Correct answer: C.\n\nCheck subject–verb concord: 'The list of names is ready' because the head of the subject is singular 'list', not plural 'names'. With 'each', 'every', 'either' and 'neither' used as singular subjects, standard examination English usually requires a singular verb. Watch pronoun reference and number.\n\nExample (v)\n'Each of the applicants ___ required to sign.'\nA. are  B. is  C. were  D. have\nSolution: 'Each' is the singular head of the subject, and the sentence describes a present requirement. The correct answer is B, 'is'.\n\n3. TENSE AND SEQUENCE OF EVENTS\nDistinguish an action completed before another past event from an action happening at that past moment. \"Had eaten\" places the meal earlier; \"was eating\" places it in progress. For events continuing until now, \"has lived here for three years\" is appropriate. \"Since\" introduces a starting point; \"for\" introduces a duration.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"She ___ in Abuja since 2022.\"\nA. lives B. has lived C. had live D. living\nSolution: \"Since 2022\" indicates a period beginning in the past and continuing to the present. The present perfect \"has lived\" fits. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (vii)\n\"The children ___ when the lights went out.\"\nA. studied B. were studying C. have study D. will studied\nSolution: The past continuous describes an ongoing action interrupted by a past event. Correct answer: B.\n\nA simple time line:\nEarlier past: The train had departed.\nLater past: We reached the station.\nCombined: 'The train had departed before we reached the station.'\nPresent continuing situation: 'She has lived here since 2020' means her residence began in 2020 and continues now.\n\nExample (viii)\n'When I called Ada, she ___ her homework.'\nA. does B. was doing C. has do D. will doing\nSolution: 'When I called' gives a past moment; 'was doing' describes an activity in progress at that moment. Correct answer: B.\n\nTime expressions help determine tense. 'Yesterday' normally points to past time, 'since 2020' with a continuing situation often requires the present perfect, and 'by the time' can establish which event happened first. Do not choose a tense based only on the nearest verb.\n\nExample (ix)\n'By the time the lecturer arrived, the students ___ their seats.'\nA. take  B. had taken  C. are taking  D. will take\nSolution: The students sat down before the lecturer arrived. The past perfect 'had taken' marks the earlier past action. Correct answer: B.\n\n4. COLLOCATIONS AND FIXED EXPRESSIONS\nNatural word partners:\nCorrect: make a mistake, take a break, draw a conclusion, keep a promise, raise an objection.\nIncorrect in standard usage: do a mistake, make a break, pull a conclusion, hold a promise (when the intended meaning is 'keep').\nA collocation is a usual partnership between words; a literal translation from another language may produce an unnatural choice.\n\nExample (x)\n'The witness was asked to ___ an account of the event.'\nA. give B. make C. do D. put\nSolution: We 'give an account' of an event. Correct answer: A.\n\nCollocations are combinations that English speakers normally use together: 'make a decision', 'take responsibility', 'pay attention', 'strong evidence' and 'heavy rain'. The individual meanings may not reveal the correct combination. A fixed expression must be learned as a unit: 'in spite of', 'as a result of', 'on behalf of'.\n\nExample (xi)\n'The committee will ___ a decision tomorrow.'\nA. do  B. make  C. perform  D. create\nSolution: Standard English uses 'make a decision'. Correct answer: B.\n\n5. PREPOSITIONS AND PHRASAL VERBS\nThe preposition can change the meaning:\n'Look after the child' = care for the child.\n'Look into the matter' = investigate the matter.\n'Look up the word' = search for the word in a dictionary.\n'Look down on someone' = consider the person inferior.\nThe same verb combines with different particles to produce different meanings.\n\nExample (xii)\n'The match was put ___ because of the storm.'\nA. on B. off C. in D. over\nSolution: 'Put off' means postponed. Correct answer: B.\n\nSome words require particular prepositions: 'interested in', 'depend on', 'accused of', 'responsible for', 'comply with'. A phrasal verb can have a meaning different from the verb alone: 'put off' means postpone; 'look into' means investigate. Choose by context, not by guessing from a single word.\n\nExample (xiii)\n'The officer promised to look ___ the complaint.'\nA. over  B. into  C. up  D. across\nSolution: To investigate a complaint is to 'look into' it. Correct answer: B.\n\n6. CONNECTIVES AND LOGICAL RELATIONSHIPS\nA connective must match both the logic and the grammatical structure. \"Although\" introduces a full clause (\"Although it rained\"); \"despite\" normally takes a noun phrase or -ing form (\"Despite the rain\"; \"Despite raining\" is less natural here). \"Because\" introduces a clause; \"because of\" introduces a noun phrase.\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"___ his illness, he attended the ceremony.\"\nA. Although B. Despite C. Because D. Unless\nSolution: \"His illness\" is a noun phrase, so \"despite\" fits. \"Although\" would need \"he was ill\". Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xv)\n\"The roads were blocked; ___, the delivery arrived late.\"\nA. therefore B. nevertheless C. however D. although\nSolution: The late arrival is the result of blocked roads. \"Therefore\" expresses the consequence. Correct answer: A.\n\nChoose by logic, not sound:\nCause: 'The road was closed because the bridge was damaged.'\nResult: 'The bridge was damaged; therefore, the road was closed.'\nContrast: 'Although the bridge was damaged, pedestrians crossed carefully.'\nCondition: 'Unless the bridge is repaired, the road will remain closed.'\nThe four sentences describe different relationships. The missing connective must match the writer's intended relationship.\n\nExample (xvi)\n'The team trained hard; ___, it lost the final.'\nA. consequently B. nevertheless C. because D. for example\nSolution: Training hard might suggest success, but the result was defeat. 'Nevertheless' expresses contrast. Correct answer: B.\n\nConnectives show addition, contrast, cause, result, purpose or condition. 'Although' introduces concession; 'therefore' introduces a conclusion; 'unless' means except if. Test whether the completed sentence makes sense.\n\nExample (xvii)\n'___ the road was flooded, the driver continued carefully.'\nA. Because of  B. Although  C. Unless  D. Therefore\nSolution: The sentence contrasts the flood with the decision to continue. 'Although' correctly introduces a clause. 'Because of' would require a noun phrase ('because of the flood'), not the full clause here. Correct answer: B.\n\n7. REGISTER AND MEANING\nChoose the word that fits the setting:\nHospital: A doctor makes a diagnosis.\nCourt: A witness gives testimony.\nBank: A customer makes a deposit.\nSchool: A candidate sits an examination.\nA word can be grammatically correct but unsuitable to the field or situation.\n\nExample (xviii)\n'The doctor examined the patient before making a ___.'\nA. verdict B. diagnosis C. sentence D. deposit\nSolution: 'Diagnosis' names the identification of a medical condition. Correct answer: B.\n\nRegister means the style appropriate to a situation. A medical passage may require 'diagnosis', not 'guess'; an official notice may use 'submit', not 'hand in', depending on the options. However, do not choose a difficult word merely because it sounds impressive. The correct option must express the intended meaning accurately.\n\n8. ARTICLES, QUANTIFIERS AND COUNTABILITY\nAn article depends on pronunciation, not spelling: \"an honest man\" begins with a vowel sound because h is silent; \"a European country\" begins with a /j/ sound. \"Few\" and \"a few\" are not identical: \"few books\" emphasises shortage, while \"a few books\" means some books are available. Likewise, \"little water\" suggests scarcity and \"a little water\" means a small but available amount.\n\nExample (xix)\n\"She is ___ honest officer.\"\nA. a B. an C. the D. some\nSolution: \"Honest\" starts with a vowel sound because the h is silent. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xx)\n\"We still have ___ time to revise before the test.\"\nA. a few B. many C. a little D. fewer\nSolution: \"Time\" is uncountable in this meaning; \"a little\" means a small amount remains. Correct answer: C.\n\nListen to the sound, not the first letter:\n'an hour' because the h is silent; 'a university' because the initial sound is /j/ ('y').\nCountable: many chairs, fewer books, a few pupils.\nUncountable: much water, less sugar, a little patience.\n'A few pupils attended' means some attended; 'few pupils attended' emphasises that the number was small.\n\nExample (xxi)\n'There is ___ information in this report than in the previous one.'\nA. fewer B. less C. many D. a few\nSolution: 'Information' is uncountable, so the comparative is 'less'. Correct answer: B.\n\nUse 'a' before a consonant sound and 'an' before a vowel sound: 'a university', 'an hour'. Use 'many' with countable plural nouns and 'much' with uncountable nouns. 'Fewer' normally compares countable items; 'less' normally compares uncountable amounts. 'A few' suggests some are available; 'few' emphasises scarcity.\n\n9. PRONOUNS AND PARALLEL STRUCTURE\nKeep the pattern balanced:\nCorrect: 'She enjoys swimming, reading and singing.'\nUnbalanced: 'She enjoys swimming, to read and singing.'\nCorrect: 'The programme aims not only to teach skills but also to create jobs.'\nThe two actions after 'not only' and 'but also' use matching infinitive forms.\n\nExample (xxii)\n'The manager asked us to be punctual, polite and ___.'\nA. honesty B. honestly C. honest D. an honesty\nSolution: 'Punctual' and 'polite' are adjectives, so the third item should also be an adjective: 'honest'. Correct answer: C.\n\nA pronoun should refer clearly to a noun and match it in number. Parallel structures use comparable grammatical forms: 'She enjoys reading, writing and debating', not 'reading, to write and debates'. With 'not only ... but also', check that the paired elements are grammatically balanced.\n\nHOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Read the complete passage once before choosing any option.\n2. Check the words before and after each gap; they often reveal the required tense, preposition or expression.\n3. Put each option into the full sentence, not just the blank.\n4. Read the sentence after the gap to check whether your choice fits the writer's meaning.\n5. Return to difficult gaps after completing easier ones; earlier answers may reveal the pattern.\n\n10. A STEP-BY-STEP CLOZE METHOD\nRead the whole passage. Identify the grammar role of the missing word. Predict a likely answer. Check all options for grammar and meaning. Read the completed sentence aloud in your mind. Check the surrounding sentences for consistency. Return to uncertain gaps after filling easier ones.\n\nPRACTICE CLOZE\n'Last year, the pupils (1) ___ a reading club. Their teacher advised them to (2) ___ attention to unfamiliar words. Although the library was small, they (3) ___ to meet every Friday. By December, the club (4) ___ more than fifty books. The pupils were proud (5) ___ their progress.'\n1. A form B formed C forming D forms\n2. A do B make C pay D give\n3. A continue B continuing C continued D continues\n4. A reads B has read C had read D reading\n5. A in B of C for D with\nSolutions: (1) B, 'formed', because 'last year' places the event in the past. (2) C, 'pay attention' is the collocation. (3) C, 'continued', matching the past narrative. (4) C, 'had read', marking completed reading by a past reference point; 'read' could work in a different sentence, but is not offered here. (5) B, 'proud of' is the correct preposition.\n\nCOMMON EXAM TRAPS\nDo not select an option simply because it is grammatically possible in isolation. Watch negative expressions, nearby plural nouns that are not the subject, and prepositions that depend on the preceding verb. Avoid changing the writer's tense without a clear reason.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nThe linked JAMB questions below test cloze choices in real passages. Read all the sentences around each gap, not just the line containing the blank."},"old":{"title":"Cloze passages: filling gaps in a passage","body":"In a cloze test, each gap in a passage has four options. The right one must fit the grammar of the sentence and the meaning of the whole passage.\n\nHow to answer:\n1. Read the whole passage once before choosing anything, so you know the topic and tone.\n2. Read a few words before and after the gap. The answer often depends on what comes after.\n3. Check grammar first: does the gap need a noun, verb, adjective or preposition? Singular or plural? Which tense?\n4. Then check meaning and collocation: which word naturally goes with its neighbours? (You \"conduct\" a survey, \"draw\" conclusions, \"pay\" respects.)\n5. Watch linking words: \"these\", \"such\" and \"however\" point back to earlier sentences.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Words that fit the sentence but not the passage's topic.\n• Look-alike words (laid/lain, defuse/diffuse).\n• Words that are almost right but need a different preposition.\n\nQuick check: \"A survey ___ to find out the traffic.\" Classified, precoded, conducted or experimented? Conducted: surveys are conducted."}},{"id":"synonyms","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Words nearest in meaning (synonyms)","body":"SYNONYMS — CHOOSING THE WORD NEAREST IN MEANING\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA synonym is a word or expression with the same or a closely related meaning as another. In UTME Use of English, 'nearest in meaning' does not always mean that two words are interchangeable in every sentence. You must choose the option that best preserves the meaning of the highlighted word as it is used in that sentence.\n\n1. CONTEXT COMES FIRST\nThe same word in different sentences:\n'The children enjoyed a light meal.' Here 'light' means not heavy or substantial.\n'She carried a light bag.' Here 'light' means not weighing much.\n'The room was filled with light.' Here 'light' refers to illumination.\nThe nearest meaning depends on the sentence, not the word in isolation.\n\nExample (i)\n'The committee reached a unanimous decision.'\nA. delayed B. shared by everyone C. controversial D. secret\nSolution: 'Unanimous' means all members agreed. Correct answer: B.\n\nMany words have several meanings. 'Bright' can describe strong light, intelligence or cheerful colours. In 'The bright pupil solved the puzzle', the nearest meaning is 'intelligent', not 'shining'. Read the full sentence and identify what the word is doing.\n\nExample (ii)\n'The witness gave a candid account of the incident.'\nA. lengthy B. frank C. confused D. hostile\nSolution: 'Candid' describes openness and honesty. 'Frank' is nearest in meaning. Correct answer: B.\n\n2. DENOTATION AND CONNOTATION\nAsk whether the writer is praising, criticising or simply describing. \"Curious\" can mean eager to learn, whereas \"nosy\" implies an unwelcome interest in private affairs. \"Determined\" can be complimentary; \"obstinate\" suggests unreasonable refusal to change. An option may share the basic idea but alter the attitude.\n\nExample (iii)\n\"The child was INQUISITIVE and asked thoughtful questions.\"\nA. curious B. intrusive C. hostile D. silent\nSolution: \"Thoughtful questions\" gives a positive learning context, so \"curious\" is nearest. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"The official's ARROGANT response angered the visitors.\"\nA. humble B. proud and overbearing C. polite D. uncertain\nSolution: \"Arrogant\" is excessive pride that treats others as inferior. The second option preserves the negative judgement. Correct answer: B.\n\nSame basic idea, different feeling:\n'Persistent' can praise someone who continues despite difficulty.\n'Stubborn' can criticise someone who refuses to change an unreasonable position.\n'Confident' suggests assurance; 'arrogant' suggests excessive pride.\nChoose a replacement that preserves the speaker's attitude as well as the core meaning.\n\nExample (v)\n'The coach admired the player's determination.'\nA. laziness B. resolve C. confusion D. pride\nSolution: 'Determination' here is a praised quality: firm resolve to succeed. Correct answer: B.\n\nDenotation is a word's basic meaning. Connotation is the feeling or attitude associated with it. 'Slim' and 'skinny' can both describe a thin person, but 'skinny' may sound critical while 'slim' is often approving. In a sentence that praises someone's appearance, 'slim' is more suitable.\n\n3. DEGREE OR INTENSITY\nA near-synonym should be close in strength as well as meaning. \"Exhausted\" means extremely tired, so \"slightly weary\" is too weak. \"Devastated\" means deeply distressed, so \"disappointed\" may understate the feeling. Always compare the whole scale, not just whether two words are related.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"The climbers were EXHAUSTED after the journey.\"\nA. slightly tired B. extremely tired C. excited D. refreshed\nSolution: \"Exhausted\" expresses severe tiredness. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (vii)\n\"Her performance was OUTSTANDING.\"\nA. average B. exceptionally good C. acceptable D. ordinary\nSolution: \"Outstanding\" expresses unusually high quality. \"Acceptable\" is too weak. Correct answer: B.\n\nA scale of strength:\nConcerned → worried → alarmed → terrified.\nWarm → hot → scorching.\nDisappointed → upset → devastated.\nWords on the same scale are related, but not equally strong. Choose the closest level.\n\nExample (viii)\n'The sudden explosion left the crowd petrified.'\nA. slightly concerned B. extremely frightened C. mildly surprised D. amused\nSolution: 'Petrified' means extremely frightened. Correct answer: B.\n\nSome words are related but differ in strength. 'Annoyed' is not as strong as 'furious'; 'warm' is not as strong as 'scorching'. If the original sentence says 'The crowd was furious', an option meaning 'very angry' is better than one meaning 'slightly displeased'.\n\nExample (ix)\n'The villagers were apprehensive about the approaching storm.'\nA. excited B. anxious C. indifferent D. certain\nSolution: 'Apprehensive' means worried or anxious about something that may happen. Correct answer: B.\n\n4. PART OF SPEECH\nThe replacement must fit grammatically:\n'Her response was prompt.' 'Prompt' is an adjective; 'quick' can replace it.\n'She responded promptly.' 'Promptly' is an adverb; 'quickly' can replace it.\n'He gave a prompt.' Here 'prompt' is a noun meaning a cue; 'quick' does not fit.\n\nExample (x)\n'The pupils listened attentively.'\nA. careful B. carefully C. care D. caring\nSolution: 'Attentively' describes how they listened and is an adverb. 'Carefully' is the closest grammatically suitable choice. Correct answer: B.\n\nA synonym should perform the same grammatical function in the sentence. 'Rapid' is an adjective; 'rapidly' is an adverb. In 'A rapid response saved the child', 'swift' is a suitable adjective. In 'She responded rapidly', 'swiftly' is an appropriate adverb.\n\n5. FORMALITY AND REGISTER\nMeaning and style:\nFormal: 'The meeting will commence at noon.'\nNeutral: 'The meeting will begin at noon.'\nInformal: 'The meeting will kick off at noon.'\nAll can refer to a start, but a formal examination notice may favour 'commence' or 'begin'. Choose by context and the options supplied.\n\nExample (xi)\n'The company intends to terminate the contract.'\nA. extend B. end C. sign D. discuss\nSolution: In this context 'terminate' means bring to an end. Correct answer: B.\n\n'Commence' and 'begin' have similar meanings, but 'commence' is more formal. 'Purchase' and 'buy' also differ in register. Examination options often include a word that is related but unsuitable to the situation. Focus on the actual sentence, not memorised pairs alone.\n\n6. POLYSEMY: ONE WORD, SEVERAL MEANINGS\nUse the neighbouring nouns and verbs to identify which sense is active. \"A sharp knife\" is physically keen; \"a sharp reply\" may be harsh; \"a sharp student\" is quick to understand. Memorising \"sharp = pointed\" would fail in the other contexts.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"The principal gave a STERN warning.\"\nA. strict B. cheerful C. lengthy D. confusing\nSolution: \"Stern\" describes seriousness and firmness, so \"strict\" is nearest. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"She gave a BRILLIANT explanation of the problem.\"\nA. luminous B. excellent C. noisy D. lengthy\nSolution: The word praises the quality of an explanation, not physical brightness. Correct answer: B.\n\nMeaning changes with use:\n'The scientist gave a sound explanation.' Sound = reasonable or well-founded.\n'The alarm made a loud sound.' Sound = something heard.\n'The diver crossed the sound.' A sound can be a narrow stretch of sea.\nOnly the first meaning fits the first sentence.\n\nExample (xiv)\n'The witness's story was inconsistent.'\nA. contradictory B. detailed C. lengthy D. interesting\nSolution: An inconsistent account contains statements that do not agree with one another. 'Contradictory' is closest. Correct answer: A.\n\n'Reluctant' means unwilling or hesitant; 'resolute' means firmly determined. 'Obscure' can mean not well known, difficult to understand or hidden from view. The correct synonym depends on which meaning is activated by context.\n\nExample (xv)\n'The explanation was obscure to the younger students.'\nA. famous B. unclear C. visible D. brief\nSolution: The students could not understand the explanation. 'Unclear' is the nearest meaning. Correct answer: B.\n\n7. IDIOMATIC OR FIGURATIVE MEANING\nThe whole phrase has a meaning:\n'She broke the ice' may mean she eased an awkward social situation, not that she smashed frozen water.\n'The plan fell through' means it failed to happen, not that it dropped through a hole.\n'He kept his head' means he remained calm, not that he physically held his head.\n\nExample (xvi)\n'When the discussion became heated, the chairperson kept her cool.'\nA. became angry B. remained calm C. left the room D. changed the subject\nSolution: 'Kept her cool' means stayed calm under pressure. Correct answer: B.\n\nA word may be used figuratively. 'The proposal received a cold reception' does not refer to temperature; it means an unfriendly or unenthusiastic response. When a word is part of an idiom, replace the meaning of the entire expression rather than its literal pieces.\n\nHOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Read the whole sentence and explain the highlighted word in simple language.\n2. Check whether the word is literal or figurative in that sentence.\n3. Substitute each likely option into the sentence and compare the meaning.\n4. Reject options with the wrong intensity, attitude or grammatical role.\n5. Choose the nearest meaning in context, not a memorised synonym that fits another situation.\n\n8. ELIMINATION TECHNIQUE\nRead the sentence without the options. Replace the target word with your own simple explanation. Remove any option with the opposite meaning, the wrong intensity or the wrong grammatical role. Substitute each remaining option into the sentence. Choose the one that changes the original meaning least.\n\nExample (xvii)\n'The judge was impartial in handling the dispute.'\nA. careless B. unbiased C. impatient D. severe\nSolution: An impartial judge does not favour either side. 'Unbiased' preserves that meaning. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xviii)\n'The athlete's recovery was remarkable.'\nA. ordinary B. gradual C. extraordinary D. painful\nSolution: 'Remarkable' means worthy of notice because it is unusual or impressive. 'Extraordinary' is nearest among the options. Correct answer: C.\n\n9. COMMON CONFUSIONS\nA quick test is to write a short sentence using each confusing word. \"The evidence is credible\" means believable; \"The witness is credulous\" means too ready to believe. \"An economical car\" uses little fuel; \"an economic policy\" concerns the economy. They look related but answer different questions.\n\nExample (xix)\n\"The proposal was PRACTICABLE.\"\nA. possible to carry out B. already completed C. theoretical only D. expensive\nSolution: \"Practicable\" means feasible or capable of being done. \"Practical\" often means useful in real situations; the closest offered choice is A.\n\nLook-alike words:\n'Ingenious' = clever and inventive; 'ingenuous' = innocent or unsuspecting.\n'Complement' = complete or go well with; 'compliment' = praise.\n'Imply' = suggest without stating directly; 'infer' = draw a conclusion from evidence.\n'Practical' = useful in real situations; 'practicable' = possible to carry out.\nA question may include a look-alike option to tempt a student who recognises the spelling but not the meaning.\n\nExample (xx)\n'Her solution was ingenious.'\nA. inventive B. innocent C. ordinary D. careless\nSolution: 'Ingenious' praises clever inventiveness. 'Ingenuous' would mean innocent or straightforward, not the intended sense. Correct answer: A.\n\n'Credible' means believable; 'credulous' means too ready to believe. 'Economic' relates to the economy or efficient use of resources; 'economical' often means avoiding waste. 'Disinterested' can mean impartial, whereas 'uninterested' means lacking interest. Distinguish these through sentences rather than rote lists.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. In 'The instructions were explicit', does 'explicit' mean clear or secret? Answer: Clear and directly stated.\n2. In 'She was reluctant to speak', does 'reluctant' mean unwilling or eager? Answer: Unwilling.\n3. In 'The evidence was compelling', does 'compelling' mean persuasive or irrelevant? Answer: Persuasive.\n4. In 'The manager was meticulous', does 'meticulous' mean careless or very careful? Answer: Very careful.\n5. In 'The argument was plausible', does 'plausible' mean believable or impossible? Answer: Believable, though not necessarily proven true.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nUse the mapped JAMB synonym questions below. The target word's sentence controls the answer, even when another option is a synonym in a different context."},"old":{"title":"Words nearest in meaning (synonyms)","body":"These questions give a word in capitals inside a sentence and ask for the option closest in meaning.\n\nHow to answer:\n1. Read the whole sentence. The context tells you which meaning of the word is used.\n2. Decide the meaning in your own words before looking at the options.\n3. Replace the capitalised word with each option. The right one keeps the sentence's meaning.\n4. Use word parts: \"bene-\" = good, \"mal-\" = bad, \"-phobia\" = fear, \"in-/un-\" = not.\n\nCommon traps:\n• The opposite of the word. Examiners almost always include it.\n• A word that is related but not equal (a thief is a criminal, but not every criminal is a thief).\n• A word that matches another meaning of the capitalised word, not the one in this sentence.\n\nBuild your vocabulary: learn words in pairs of opposites (verbose/laconic, sanguine/pessimistic, tardy/prompt). JAMB reuses many of them.\n\nQuick check: \"The men were TARDY in offering help.\" Quick, slow, brave or generous? Slow. \"Quick\" is the opposite trap."}},{"id":"antonyms","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Words opposite in meaning (antonyms)","body":"These questions ask for the option opposite in meaning to the word or phrase in capitals.\n\nHow to answer:\n1. Work out what the word means in the sentence.\n2. Cross out any synonyms first. Examiners almost always include one or two as traps.\n3. Of what's left, choose the true opposite. It should be about the same quality (size, mood, quantity), just reversed.\n4. Test it: put the option into the sentence. It should make the sentence mean the reverse.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A synonym (the most common trap).\n• A word that is negative but not opposite (for \"harmless\", \"unpleasant\" is negative, but the opposite is \"pernicious\": harmful).\n• A word about a different quality (for \"plethora\", which is about amount, \"complexity\" is about difficulty).\n\nClassic JAMB pairs: covert/overt, verbose/laconic, conceited/unassuming, munificent/niggardly, apathy/enthusiasm, aversion/predilection, transient/permanent.\n\nQuick check: opposite of \"The lecture seemed INTERMINABLE\": brief, boring, interesting or unending? Brief. \"Unending\" is the synonym trap."},"new":{"title":"Words opposite in meaning (antonyms)","body":"ANTONYMS — FINDING THE OPPOSITE MEANING IN CONTEXT\n\nINTRODUCTION\nAn antonym is a word or expression whose meaning is opposite to another word or expression. In JAMB Use of English, the instruction may say \"Choose the word opposite in meaning to the word in capitals.\" The correct option must reverse the particular meaning used in the sentence. It is not enough to remember a word pair from a vocabulary list.\n\n1. FIRST IDENTIFY THE WORD'S MEANING IN THE SENTENCE\nA word can have several meanings, so it can have different opposites in different contexts. \"Light luggage\" contrasts with \"heavy luggage\"; \"a light room\" contrasts with \"a dark room\"; \"a light punishment\" contrasts with \"a severe punishment\". The same printed word does not have one opposite for every situation.\n\nContext changes the answer:\nSentence A: \"The soup was BLAND.\" Opposite: spicy or strongly flavoured.\nSentence B: \"His BLAND speech bored the audience.\" Opposite: lively or engaging.\nSentence C: \"The witness gave a VAGUE answer.\" Opposite: clear or precise.\nFirst explain the capitalised word in your own words. Then find a choice that reverses that meaning.\n\nExample (i)\n\"The chairman's remarks were CANDID.\"\nA. frank  B. evasive  C. truthful  D. direct\nSolution: \"Candid\" means open and frank. The opposite is \"evasive\", meaning avoiding a direct answer. A, C and D are near-synonyms, so they are traps. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\n\"The witness gave a CONCISE statement.\"\nA. brief  B. accurate  C. lengthy  D. relevant\nSolution: \"Concise\" means short while containing the necessary information. \"Lengthy\" reverses the idea of brevity. Correct answer: C.\n\n2. TYPES OF OPPOSITES\nIn gradable pairs, the context determines the useful opposite. \"An expensive meal\" may contrast with \"an inexpensive meal\", not necessarily a free meal. Converse pairs reverse roles: if a shopkeeper sells an item, a customer buys it. Do not mistake \"sell\" for \"lose\": the two events are not opposites.\n\nExample (iii)\n\"The merchant SOLD the goods.\"\nA. bought B. lost C. stored D. displayed\nSolution: \"Bought\" reverses the commercial role of selling. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"The answer was OPTIONAL.\"\nA. voluntary B. compulsory C. suggested D. possible\nSolution: Optional means not required. Compulsory means required. Correct answer: B.\n\nGradable antonyms describe opposite ends of a scale: hot/cold, rich/poor, generous/stingy. A person may be neither rich nor poor, so these are not always absolute alternatives.\nComplementary opposites are commonly treated as mutually exclusive in the relevant context: present/absent, legal/illegal, alive/dead. Be aware that everyday usage can be more nuanced.\nRelational or converse opposites describe the same relationship from opposite sides: buy/sell, lend/borrow, employer/employee, parent/child. They are not opposites in the same way as hot/cold; they reverse the roles.\n\nThree patterns:\nScale: cold ← cool ← warm ← hot. The strongest opposite of \"scorching\" is nearer \"freezing\" than \"mild\".\nEither/or: an attendee is \"present\" or \"absent\" at roll call.\nReversed relationship: If Ada lends a book to Emeka, Emeka borrows it from Ada.\n\nExample (v)\n\"Her attitude towards the visitors was HOSTILE.\"\nA. unfriendly  B. aggressive  C. welcoming  D. suspicious\nSolution: \"Hostile\" describes unfriendly or antagonistic behaviour. \"Welcoming\" reverses that attitude. Correct answer: C.\n\n3. NEGATIVE PREFIXES ARE USEFUL BUT NOT AUTOMATIC\nA negative-looking prefix does not always create the meaning you expect. \"Invaluable\" means extremely useful or precious; it does not mean \"without value\". \"Infamous\" means notorious, not merely unknown. Check the actual meaning before relying on the spelling.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"The volunteer's contribution was INVALUABLE.\"\nA. priceless B. worthless C. important D. helpful\nSolution: Invaluable means exceptionally valuable. The opposite among the options is \"worthless\". Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (vii)\n\"The account was ACCURATE.\"\nA. precise B. reliable C. erroneous D. detailed\nSolution: Accurate means correct. Erroneous means containing errors. Correct answer: C.\n\nPrefixes such as un-, in-, im-, ir-, il-, dis- and non- can form opposites: happy/unhappy, possible/impossible, regular/irregular, legal/illegal, honest/dishonest. But you cannot attach any prefix to any word and assume it is correct. \"Valuable\" contrasts with \"worthless\" in many contexts; \"unvaluable\" is not the standard antonym. \"Inflammable\" means easily set on fire, not unable to burn.\n\nPrefix and meaning:\nPatient → impatient; logical → illogical; responsible → irresponsible; accurate → inaccurate.\nHowever: famous → obscure or unknown, not necessarily \"infamous\". \"Infamous\" means notorious for a bad reason, not simply unknown.\n\nExample (viii)\n\"The instructions were AMBIGUOUS.\"\nA. uncertain  B. confusing  C. clear  D. doubtful\nSolution: \"Ambiguous\" means open to more than one interpretation. \"Clear\" is the best opposite among the choices. Correct answer: C.\n\n4. DEGREE, INTENSITY AND EMOTIONAL COLOUR\n\"Furious\" is more intense than \"annoyed\"; \"delighted\" is more intense than \"pleased\". An antonym question may offer an option on the same side of the scale but with a different strength. You must select the choice that actually reverses the meaning. Emotional colouring also matters: \"frugal\" suggests careful spending, while \"miserly\" suggests excessive unwillingness to spend.\n\nScale:\nVery fearful ← fearful ← calm → confident → fearless.\nVery pessimistic ← pessimistic ← neutral → optimistic.\n\"Optimistic\" and \"pessimistic\" contrast in outlook; \"happy\" is not necessarily the best antonym of \"pessimistic\".\n\nExample (ix)\n\"The villagers were OPTIMISTIC about the harvest.\"\nA. hopeful  B. cheerful  C. pessimistic  D. confident\nSolution: \"Optimistic\" means expecting a favourable outcome. \"Pessimistic\" means expecting an unfavourable outcome. Correct answer: C.\n\n5. PART OF SPEECH AND GRAMMATICAL FIT\nSubstitute the opposite into the original sentence. \"She acted CAREFULLY\" needs an adverb such as \"carelessly\", not the adjective \"careless\". \"The response was COURTEOUS\" needs an adjective such as \"rude\". Grammatical fit is often enough to eliminate two distractors before checking meaning.\n\nExample (x)\n\"He accepted the offer WILLINGLY.\"\nA. eagerly B. reluctantly C. willing D. pleasure\nSolution: Willingly means readily or without resistance. Reluctantly means with hesitation or unwillingness and is also an adverb. Correct answer: B.\n\nIf the target word is an adjective, the best option is generally an adjective that can replace it. \"She responded RELUCTANTLY\" is an adverb; \"eagerly\" is the corresponding adverbial opposite. \"Reluctant\" is an adjective; \"eager\" is its opposite. Some distractors are related in meaning but have the wrong grammatical form.\n\nExample (xi)\n\"The officer spoke POLITELY.\"\nA. rudely  B. hostile  C. angry  D. discourtesy\nSolution: The opposite of the adverb \"politely\" is the adverb \"rudely\". The other options do not match the grammatical role. Correct answer: A.\n\n6. WORDS THAT LOOK LIKE OPPOSITES BUT ARE NOT\n\"Disinterested\" may mean impartial; \"uninterested\" means lacking interest. \"Invaluable\" means extremely valuable, not worthless. \"Credulous\" means too ready to believe; \"incredulous\" means unwilling or unable to believe something. \"Famous\" and \"infamous\" differ in reputation, not simply recognition.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"The referee was IMPARTIAL.\"\nA. unbiased  B. fair  C. biased  D. objective\nSolution: \"Impartial\" means not favouring either side. \"Biased\" means showing favouritism. Correct answer: C.\n\n7. FIGURATIVE AND IDIOMATIC OPPOSITES\nA phrase may express a quality indirectly. \"He gave a COLD reception\" means unfriendly reception, not low temperature. The opposite is \"warm\" or \"friendly\". \"Her explanation SHED LIGHT on the matter\" means clarified it; an opposite would be to obscure or confuse it.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"The announcement put the workers AT EASE.\"\nA. relieved  B. reassured  C. anxious  D. comfortable\nSolution: \"At ease\" means relaxed or free from worry. \"Anxious\" reverses that state. Correct answer: C.\n\n8. HOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Read the entire sentence and explain the capitalised word as used there.\n2. Think of a simple opposite before looking at the options.\n3. Eliminate synonyms: JAMB may offer one or two words that mean almost the same as the target.\n4. Check degree, attitude and part of speech.\n5. Put the remaining option into the sentence: does it reverse the intended meaning?\n\nElimination:\n\"Her explanation was LUCID.\"\nA. clear  B. coherent  C. obscure  D. understandable\n\"Lucid\" means clear and easy to understand. A, B and D support that meaning. C, \"obscure\", reverses it.\n\n9. EXTRA WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (xiv)\n\"The judge was LENIENT with the first offender.\"\nA. merciful  B. strict  C. forgiving  D. tolerant\nSolution: A lenient judge is not severe in punishment. \"Strict\" is the best opposite. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xv)\n\"The agreement was TEMPORARY.\"\nA. brief  B. provisional  C. permanent  D. short-lived\nSolution: Temporary means lasting for a limited period. Permanent means intended to last indefinitely. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"The student was METICULOUS in checking her answers.\"\nA. careful  B. precise  C. careless  D. thorough\nSolution: Meticulous means extremely careful and attentive to detail. Careless reverses that quality. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"The proposal was FEASIBLE.\"\nA. practical  B. workable  C. impossible  D. realistic\nSolution: Feasible means possible to carry out. Impossible is the clearest opposite. Correct answer: C.\n\n10. COMMON EXAM TRAPS\nA synonym is the most common distractor. Another distractor may be unrelated rather than opposite: \"large\" is not the opposite of \"honest\". Some answers are opposites in a different context, such as \"dark\" for \"light punishment\". Beware of a prefix that makes a word look negative without giving the correct meaning.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — PRACTISE BEFORE REVEALING\n(i) The child was TIMID. Opposite: bold or courageous.\n(ii) The evidence was INSUFFICIENT. Opposite: sufficient or adequate.\n(iii) The officer was CORRUPT. Opposite: honest or upright.\n(iv) The instructions were EXPLICIT. Opposite: vague or unclear.\n(v) The response was SPONTANEOUS. Opposite: planned or deliberate.\nFor each, explain why the opposite fits the original sentence.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nThe past-question button below opens JAMB antonym questions mapped to this lesson. For each one, first identify the meaning of the capitalised word in its sentence, then test the opposite."}},{"id":"lexis","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Choosing the right word in a sentence (lexis)","body":"Many gap questions test whether you know which word fits the meaning and goes naturally with the words around it (collocation).\n\nHow to answer:\n1. Find the clue in the sentence. \"Tried to ___ all memory\" (forget: obliterate), \"reconciling with her ___ husband\" (they were apart: estranged).\n2. Check the word's exact meaning, not just its general feel.\n3. Check collocation, the words English naturally puts together: conduct a survey, defuse a bomb, reconcile views, a wealth of experience.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sound-alikes: defuse (make a bomb safe) vs diffuse (spread); precocious vs premature.\n• Nigerian English forms that standard English rejects: \"was not opportuned\" (use \"did not have the opportunity\"), \"cannot be able to\" (use \"cannot\" or \"is not able to\").\n• A word that is roughly right but doesn't collocate (\"experiment a survey\").\n\nQuick check: \"The police came early enough to ___ the bomb.\" Detonate, diffuse, insulate or defuse? Defuse."},"new":{"title":"Choosing the right word in a sentence (lexis)","body":"LEXIS — CHOOSING THE EXACT WORD THAT FITS\n\nINTRODUCTION\nLexis means the vocabulary of a language: the words we use and the meanings they carry. JAMB lexical-choice questions often present a sentence with a blank and ask you to choose the most suitable word. A good answer must satisfy meaning, grammar, word partnership and the situation. A word may sound impressive but still be wrong.\n\n1. MEANING IN CONTEXT\nStart by reading the entire sentence. Identify the clue that tells you what the missing word must mean. In \"The committee decided to ___ the meeting until Friday\", the time expression \"until Friday\" suggests postponement. \"Adjourn\" or \"postpone\" may fit, depending on the options; \"cancel\" would mean the meeting will not take place at all.\n\nClue → meaning → word:\n\"The witness refused to ___ the secret.\" Clue: a secret is being kept. Possible verb: reveal or disclose.\n\"The patient began to ___ after treatment.\" Clue: health improves. Possible verb: recover.\n\"The referee had to ___ the dispute.\" Clue: two sides disagree. Possible verb: settle or resolve.\nThe surrounding words narrow the possibilities.\n\nExample (i)\n\"The officer was asked to ___ the allegations.\"\nA. investigate  B. celebrate  C. decorate  D. imitate\nSolution: Allegations are claims requiring examination. \"Investigate\" is the appropriate verb. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (ii)\n\"The new policy is intended to ___ waste.\"\nA. encourage  B. reduce  C. multiply  D. conceal\nSolution: A policy aimed at controlling waste would reduce it. The context supports B. Correct answer: B.\n\n2. COLLOCATION — WORDS THAT NATURALLY GO TOGETHER\nCollocations are familiar combinations of words. We \"make a decision\", \"take a risk\", \"pay attention\", \"commit a crime\", \"conduct an experiment\", \"reach a conclusion\" and \"raise an objection\". The wrong option may have a related dictionary meaning but not combine naturally with the noun.\n\nCommon partners:\nVerb + noun: lodge a complaint; draw a conclusion; keep a promise; meet a deadline; deliver a speech.\nAdjective + noun: heavy rain; strong evidence; bitter disappointment; keen interest; serious concern.\nNoun + preposition: access to; reason for; solution to; interest in; demand for.\nVerb + preposition: depend on; insist on; comply with; object to; suffer from.\n\nExample (iii)\n\"The scientist will ___ an experiment tomorrow.\"\nA. do out  B. conduct  C. manufacture  D. perform into\nSolution: \"Conduct an experiment\" is a standard collocation. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"The witness was told to ___ a complaint at the station.\"\nA. lodge  B. lay down  C. create  D. construct\nSolution: A person \"lodges a complaint\" formally. Correct answer: A.\n\n3. NEAR-SYNONYMS WITH DIFFERENT USES\nDo not treat near-synonyms as identical. \"Refuse\" can mean decline to do something; \"deny\" can mean say a claim is untrue. \"Borrow\" means receive something temporarily; \"lend\" means give it temporarily. \"Remember\" means retain or recall information; \"remind\" means cause another person to remember.\n\nExample (v)\n\"Please ___ me to submit the form tomorrow.\"\nA. remember B. remind C. recall D. recollect\nSolution: Another person is being asked to prompt the speaker's memory. \"Remind\" is the correct verb. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"The suspect ___ that he had entered the building.\"\nA. denied B. refused C. rejected to D. declined that\nSolution: \"Denied that\" introduces a claim the suspect says is untrue. \"Refused\" would normally take an infinitive such as \"refused to enter\". Correct answer: A.\n\nWords may overlap without being interchangeable. \"Persuade\" means convince someone to do or believe something; \"dissuade\" means persuade someone not to do something. \"Assure\" commonly takes a person as object (\"assure her\"); \"ensure\" means make certain something happens (\"ensure safety\"); \"insure\" means arrange insurance against risk.\n\nThe difference matters:\n\"Please ___ me that the parcel has arrived.\" → assure.\n\"Please ___ that the parcel arrives.\" → ensure.\n\"We will ___ the parcel against loss.\" → insure.\nThe choice is determined by the object and the intended meaning.\n\nExample (vii)\n\"The teacher tried to ___ the pupils from cheating.\"\nA. persuade  B. dissuade  C. encourage  D. invite\nSolution: \"Dissuade someone from doing something\" means persuade the person not to do it. Correct answer: B.\n\n4. CONFUSABLE WORDS\nSimilar spellings often hide unrelated meanings. \"Elicit\" means draw out a response; \"illicit\" means illegal. \"Allude\" means refer indirectly; \"elude\" means escape or avoid capture. \"Council\" is an assembly; \"counsel\" is advice or a legal adviser. Learn each in a complete sentence.\n\nExample (viii)\n\"The investigator tried to ___ a confession.\"\nA. illicit B. elicit C. elude D. allude\nSolution: \"Elicit\" means draw out information or a response. \"Illicit\" is an adjective meaning unlawful. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ix)\n\"The thief managed to ___ the police.\"\nA. allude B. elude C. include D. conclude\nSolution: \"Elude\" means escape from or avoid capture. Correct answer: B.\n\n\"Adapt\" = adjust to a situation; \"adopt\" = take up a practice or legally take someone as a child.\n\"Affect\" is commonly a verb meaning influence; \"effect\" is commonly a noun meaning result, though it can also be a formal verb meaning bring about.\n\"Compliment\" = praise; \"complement\" = complete or go well with.\n\"Principal\" = main or head of a school; \"principle\" = rule or belief.\n\"Stationary\" = not moving; \"stationery\" = writing materials.\n\nExample (x)\n\"The company had to ___ to the new regulations.\"\nA. adopt  B. adapt  C. adept  D. addition\nSolution: \"Adapt to\" means adjust to changed conditions. \"Adopt\" would mean take up something, not adjust to it. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xi)\n\"The manager praised her for working according to sound ___.\"\nA. principals  B. principles  C. principal  D. principled\nSolution: The sentence refers to rules or standards, so \"principles\" is required. Correct answer: B.\n\n5. REGISTER — WORD CHOICE FOR A PARTICULAR FIELD\nRegister is language suited to a profession or situation. Medical writing uses terms such as diagnosis, prescription and symptoms. Law uses evidence, testimony, verdict and appeal. Banking uses deposits, withdrawals, balances and transactions. Agriculture uses germination, irrigation and harvest. Choose the word that fits the field and the sentence.\n\nSame general idea, different context:\nDoctor: \"The doctor prescribed medication.\"\nJudge: \"The judge delivered a verdict.\"\nBanker: \"The cashier processed a withdrawal.\"\nTeacher: \"The examiner marked the scripts.\"\nUsing \"verdict\" for a doctor's diagnosis or \"prescription\" for a court judgement would confuse the registers.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"The jury returned a ___ after considering the evidence.\"\nA. diagnosis  B. verdict  C. harvest  D. deposit\nSolution: A jury reaches or returns a verdict in a legal proceeding. Correct answer: B.\n\n6. PREPOSITIONS AND FIXED CONSTRUCTIONS\nA preposition can change the meaning of a verb: \"agree with a person\", \"agree to a proposal\", \"agree on a date\". With \"accuse\", use \"accuse someone of something\"; with \"congratulate\", use \"congratulate someone on an achievement\". Notice the grammatical pattern, not only the preposition.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"The students agreed ___ a date for the excursion.\"\nA. with B. on C. for D. by\nSolution: They reached a shared decision about the date: \"agreed on\". Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"She congratulated him ___ winning the scholarship.\"\nA. for B. at C. on D. with\nSolution: The fixed construction is \"congratulate someone on doing something\". Correct answer: C.\n\nSome adjectives and verbs normally require specific prepositions: \"capable of\", \"accustomed to\", \"different from\", \"responsible for\", \"consist of\", \"participate in\", \"congratulate someone on\". Others require a particular verb pattern: \"prevent someone from doing\"; \"insist on doing\"; \"prefer X to Y\".\n\nPattern, not guesswork:\nCorrect: \"She is capable of solving it.\"\nCorrect: \"They prevented him from entering.\"\nCorrect: \"I prefer tea to coffee.\"\nIncorrect in standard examination usage: \"capable to solve\"; \"prevented him to enter\"; \"prefer tea than coffee\".\n\nExample (xv)\n\"The workers insisted ___ receiving their wages.\"\nA. for  B. at  C. on  D. by\nSolution: The standard construction is \"insist on\" followed by a noun or -ing form. Correct answer: C.\n\n7. WORD FORMS AND PARTS OF SPEECH\nA sentence may need a noun, verb, adjective or adverb. \"Success\" is a noun; \"succeed\" is a verb; \"successful\" is an adjective; \"successfully\" is an adverb. Read the words immediately before and after the blank to identify the required grammatical role.\n\nOne word family:\n\"Her success surprised everyone.\" (noun)\n\"She succeeded in the test.\" (verb)\n\"She was successful.\" (adjective)\n\"She completed the work successfully.\" (adverb)\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"The candidate answered the questions ___.\"\nA. confidence  B. confident  C. confidently  D. confide\nSolution: The gap describes how the candidate answered; an adverb is required. Correct answer: C.\n\n8. PRECISION AND DEGREE\nThe action described in the sentence may be temporary or permanent. \"Suspend the event\" suggests a temporary halt; \"cancel the event\" means it will not go ahead as planned; \"postpone the event\" means move it to a later time. The correct word depends on what the sentence says will happen next.\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"The match was ___ until next Saturday.\"\nA. cancelled B. postponed C. abolished D. destroyed\nSolution: \"Until next Saturday\" indicates rescheduling rather than abandoning the match. Correct answer: B.\n\n\"Repair\" means make something work again; \"replace\" means put something new in its place. \"Suspend\" means stop temporarily; \"terminate\" means end. \"Criticise\" means point out faults; \"condemn\" expresses strong disapproval. Select the exact action or degree described.\n\nExample (xviii)\n\"The club decided to ___ the meeting for one week.\"\nA. postpone  B. abolish  C. destroy  D. conclude\nSolution: A meeting moved to a later time is postponed. \"Abolish\" would mean end an institution or practice; it does not fit. Correct answer: A.\n\n9. FIGURATIVE MEANING AND PHRASAL VERBS\n\"Bring up\" may mean raise a child or mention a subject. \"Carry out\" means perform a task. \"Turn down\" can mean reject an offer or reduce volume. \"Break down\" can mean stop functioning, lose emotional control or divide something into parts. Context determines the intended meaning.\n\nExample (xix)\n\"The committee will look into the complaint.\"\nA. ignore  B. investigate  C. repeat  D. postpone\nSolution: \"Look into\" means investigate. Correct answer: B.\n\n10. HOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Read the entire sentence and identify the meaning the missing word must express.\n2. Check whether the blank needs a noun, verb, adjective, adverb or preposition.\n3. Look for a familiar collocation or fixed construction.\n4. Eliminate words that are close in meaning but wrong for the situation.\n5. Insert the chosen option and read the whole sentence again.\n\nWork through the options:\n\"The chairperson asked members to ___ a decision.\"\nA. do  B. make  C. create out  D. perform\nMeaning: decide something. Pattern: \"make a decision\". Grammar: a verb is needed. Correct answer: B.\n\n11. EXTRA WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (xx)\n\"The firefighter managed to ___ the flames.\"\nA. extinguish  B. erase  C. dissolve  D. delete\nSolution: \"Extinguish\" is the precise verb for putting out a fire. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xxi)\n\"Her words helped to ___ the tension in the room.\"\nA. defuse  B. diffuse  C. refuse  D. confuse\nSolution: \"Defuse tension\" means make a difficult situation less dangerous or intense. \"Diffuse\" means spread or scatter. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xxii)\n\"The committee decided to ___ the two conflicting reports.\"\nA. reconcile  B. separate  C. provoke  D. abandon\nSolution: To \"reconcile\" conflicting reports is to make them consistent or resolve their differences. Correct answer: A.\n\nCOMMON EXAM TRAPS\nA familiar word may have the wrong preposition. A formal-sounding word may not fit the register. Two words may look alike but mean different things. An option may fit the blank grammatically but contradict the meaning of the next clause.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) Do we make or do a decision? Make a decision.\n(ii) Is a person responsible of or for an action? Responsible for.\n(iii) Does \"defer\" mean postpone or destroy? Postpone.\n(iv) Which fits a hospital: diagnosis or verdict? Diagnosis.\n(v) Does \"adapt to\" mean adjust to or take ownership of? Adjust to.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nUse the linked JAMB lexis questions below. Practise explaining the meaning, collocation and grammatical pattern behind each answer."}},{"id":"idioms","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Idioms, proverbs and fixed expressions","body":"An idiom is a fixed phrase whose meaning is not the sum of its words. JAMB tests whether you know the meaning and the exact wording.\n\nRules:\n1. Idioms are almost never literal. \"Turn over a new leaf\" has nothing to do with books or plants: it means to change for the better.\n2. The wording is fixed. \"More power to your elbow\" is an idiom; \"more effort to your elbow\" is not. \"For days on end\", not \"at end\".\n3. For proverbs, choose the general lesson. \"Look before you leap\" means think about the consequences before acting.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Literal options (a book, a crop, someone actually leaping).\n• A near-version of the idiom with one word changed.\n• Options that add details the idiom doesn't contain.\n\nUseful ones: bite off more than you can chew (take on too much), the writing on the wall (a sign of coming trouble), give the benefit of the doubt, nip in the bud (stop early), steer a middle course (compromise), a level playing field (equal conditions), iron out (resolve).\n\nQuick check: \"The rain fell for days ___.\" On end, at end, at ending or on ending? On end."},"new":{"title":"Idioms, proverbs and fixed expressions","body":"IDIOMS, PROVERBS AND FIXED EXPRESSIONS — UNDERSTANDING MEANING BEYOND THE WORDS\n\nINTRODUCTION\nAn idiom is a group of words whose accepted meaning cannot always be understood by interpreting each word separately. \"Spill the beans\" means reveal a secret; it is not about dropping food. A proverb is a short traditional saying that expresses a general lesson or piece of advice. A fixed expression is a combination of words conventionally used in a particular form, such as \"by and large\" or \"in the long run\". JAMB may test their meaning, their correct form or the situation in which they apply.\n\n1. IDIOMS ARE USUALLY FIGURATIVE\nFigurative meaning is meaning beyond the literal picture. \"He has a heart of gold\" praises kindness; it does not mean the organ is made of metal. \"She was on cloud nine\" means she was extremely happy. Read the surrounding sentence to decide which figurative meaning is intended.\n\nLiteral picture versus intended meaning:\n\"Break the ice\": literally break frozen water; idiomatically make people feel more comfortable at the start of a meeting.\n\"Hit the nail on the head\": literally strike a nail correctly; idiomatically identify the exact point.\n\"Under the weather\": literally beneath the sky; idiomatically feeling unwell.\n\"Turn over a new leaf\": literally turn a page or leaf; idiomatically change behaviour for the better.\n\nExample (i)\n\"After years of dishonesty, he decided to TURN OVER A NEW LEAF.\"\nA. open a new book  B. change for the better  C. leave the country  D. plant a tree\nSolution: The reference to past dishonesty shows that the phrase means improve his behaviour. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\n\"Her joke BROKE THE ICE at the tense meeting.\"\nA. damaged the office  B. ended the meeting  C. reduced the awkwardness  D. angered everyone\nSolution: The meeting was tense, and the joke helped people relax. Correct answer: C.\n\n2. CONTEXT TELLS YOU WHICH MEANING FITS\nAn idiom can describe an action, attitude or outcome. \"He was in hot water\" usually means he was in trouble; the sentence might tell you whether the trouble came from a mistake, punishment or disagreement. The idiom does not automatically tell you the exact cause. Answer only what its accepted meaning supports.\n\nExample (iii)\n\"After breaking the laboratory rules, the student was IN HOT WATER.\"\nA. taking a bath B. in trouble C. feeling cold D. on holiday\nSolution: Breaking rules explains why the student was in difficulty. The idiom means in trouble. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"After winning the award, the young writer was OVER THE MOON.\"\nA. extremely happy B. very confused C. travelling in space D. extremely tired\nSolution: Winning an award is a happy event; the idiom means delighted. Correct answer: A.\n\nSome expressions can be literal in one sentence and figurative in another. \"He let the cat out of the bag\" normally means he revealed a secret, but a story about an actual cat in a bag could use the words literally. The passage determines the meaning. Also watch positive and negative tone: \"a blessing in disguise\" means something that initially seems bad but later proves beneficial.\n\nTwo sentences:\n\"After the examination, the pupil was ON CLOUD NINE.\" Figurative: extremely delighted.\n\"The pilot flew above a thick cloud.\" Literal: an actual cloud.\nThe grammar and situation help you decide.\n\nExample (v)\n\"Losing the first job was A BLESSING IN DISGUISE because it led to a better opportunity.\"\nA. a hidden benefit  B. a public punishment  C. a religious festival  D. a costly mistake\nSolution: The loss appeared unfortunate but produced a better result. Correct answer: A.\n\n3. FIXED WORDING AND PREPOSITIONS\nLearn the phrase as a unit, but also understand its function. \"At a loss\" describes uncertainty; \"in the nick of time\" means just before it would be too late; \"on the verge of\" means very close to an event. A question may remove one preposition, and a grammatically possible alternative may still be nonstandard.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"The ambulance arrived ___ the nick of time.\"\nA. on B. at C. in D. by\nSolution: The fixed expression is \"in the nick of time\". Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (vii)\n\"She was ___ a loss for words.\"\nA. on B. at C. by D. from\nSolution: The expression is \"at a loss for words\", meaning unable to think of what to say. Correct answer: B.\n\nMany idioms are conventional phrases. \"At sixes and sevens\" means confused or disorganised; \"at six and seven\" is not the usual form. \"By and large\" means generally. \"On the spur of the moment\" means without advance planning. \"For days on end\" means continuously for many days. Changing a preposition can make the expression incorrect.\n\nLearn the whole phrase:\nCorrect: \"in the long run\"; not \"on the long run\".\nCorrect: \"at a loss\"; not \"in a loss\" for being uncertain what to do.\nCorrect: \"by heart\"; not \"with heart\" for memorisation.\nCorrect: \"on the whole\"; not \"in the whole\" when meaning generally.\n\nExample (viii)\n\"The students learnt the poem ___ heart.\"\nA. on  B. by  C. with  D. for\nSolution: \"Learn by heart\" means memorise. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ix)\n\"He made the decision ON THE SPUR OF THE MOMENT.\"\nA. after long planning  B. without advance planning  C. under legal pressure  D. with a written schedule\nSolution: The idiom describes a spontaneous decision. Correct answer: B.\n\n4. IDIOMS ABOUT SUCCESS, FAILURE AND EFFORT\n\"Burn the midnight oil\" means study or work late into the night. \"Go the extra mile\" means make an additional effort. \"Throw in the towel\" means give up. \"Miss the boat\" means lose an opportunity. \"Make ends meet\" means have enough money to cover essential expenses.\n\nUse them in context:\n\"Ada burnt the midnight oil before the test.\" → She studied late.\n\"The business struggled to make ends meet.\" → Income barely covered necessary expenses.\n\"Despite two defeats, the team refused to throw in the towel.\" → The team would not surrender.\n\nExample (x)\n\"The candidate BURNT THE MIDNIGHT OIL to prepare for UTME.\"\nA. started a fire  B. studied late into the night  C. travelled overnight  D. wasted electricity\nSolution: Preparation for an examination explains the figurative meaning: studying late. Correct answer: B.\n\n5. IDIOMS ABOUT SECRETS, CONFLICT AND COMMUNICATION\n\"Spill the beans\" means disclose a secret. \"Beat about the bush\" means avoid coming directly to the point. \"Read between the lines\" means notice a meaning that is suggested rather than stated directly. \"Add fuel to the fire\" means make an already bad situation worse. \"Bury the hatchet\" means end a quarrel and make peace.\n\nExample (xi)\n\"Instead of answering directly, the witness BEAT ABOUT THE BUSH.\"\nA. spoke indirectly  B. ran into a forest  C. answered precisely  D. told a joke\nSolution: The phrase contrasts with answering directly. It means avoiding the main point. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"After months of disagreement, the neighbours BURIED THE HATCHET.\"\nA. hid a tool  B. renewed the quarrel  C. made peace  D. moved house\nSolution: The phrase means ended their disagreement. Correct answer: C.\n\n6. PROVERBS EXPRESS GENERAL LESSONS\nTo interpret a proverb, identify the situation, the comparison and the advice. \"Don't put all your eggs in one basket\" warns against risking everything on a single plan. It does not forbid commitment; it encourages spreading risk. \"Actions speak louder than words\" values what people do over what they promise.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"Instead of investing all her savings in one venture, Bisi divided the money among several safer options. Her mother said, 'DON'T PUT ALL YOUR EGGS IN ONE BASKET.'\"\nQuestion: What is the advice?\nSolution: Avoid concentrating all your resources in one risky option. The eggs stand for resources and the basket for one venture.\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"The politician promised to repair the road, but residents waited to see actual work. They believed ACTIONS SPEAK LOUDER THAN WORDS.\"\nQuestion: What does the proverb mean?\nSolution: Real deeds provide stronger evidence of commitment than promises alone.\n\nUnlike an idiom, a proverb often forms a complete sentence that offers advice or states an observation. \"Look before you leap\" means consider the consequences before acting. \"A stitch in time saves nine\" means dealing with a problem early prevents greater trouble later. \"Where there is a will, there is a way\" encourages determination. A proverb may be used to comment on a particular situation; choose the general principle it expresses.\n\nSituation → proverb → lesson:\nSituation: A small roof leak is repaired immediately, preventing major damage.\nProverb: \"A stitch in time saves nine.\"\nLesson: Early action can prevent a much bigger problem.\n\nSituation: A student checks the conditions of a scholarship before applying.\nProverb: \"Look before you leap.\"\nLesson: Think carefully before making an important decision.\n\nExample (xv)\n\"When Ngozi repaired the loose wire before it caused a fire, her father said, 'A STITCH IN TIME SAVES NINE.'\"\nA. Sewing is expensive  B. Early action prevents bigger problems  C. Nine workers are needed  D. All repairs must be done at night\nSolution: The proverb praises timely prevention of a larger problem. Correct answer: B.\n\n7. DISTINGUISH IDIOM, PROVERB AND ORDINARY EXPRESSION\nIdiom: \"He kicked the bucket\" may mean he died, depending on context. Its figurative meaning cannot be worked out from the individual words.\nProverb: \"Honesty is the best policy\" gives general advice.\nOrdinary expression: \"He kicked the football\" is literal.\nFixed expression: \"By and large, the plan succeeded\" means generally; its conventional wording is important.\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"BY AND LARGE, the programme achieved its objectives.\"\nA. secretly  B. generally  C. immediately  D. accidentally\nSolution: \"By and large\" means generally or on the whole. Correct answer: B.\n\n8. PROVERBS ARE NOT ALWAYS LITERAL RULES\nProverbs are general observations, not laws without exceptions. \"Too many cooks spoil the broth\" warns about uncoordinated interference, not about every form of teamwork. \"The early bird catches the worm\" praises acting promptly when timing matters; it does not mean the earliest action always succeeds.\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"Six people gave conflicting instructions to the same carpenter, and the work was delayed. 'TOO MANY COOKS SPOIL THE BROTH,' the supervisor observed.\"\nQuestion: What caused the problem?\nSolution: Too many people interfered without coordination. The proverb does not mean the carpenter needed more food or that teamwork is always harmful.\n\n\"A bird in the hand is worth two in the bush\" means a certain benefit may be better than a larger uncertain one. It is not advice about keeping birds. \"Too many cooks spoil the broth\" warns that too many people interfering in the same task may cause problems. It does not mean teamwork is always bad. \"The early bird catches the worm\" encourages timely action, not waking at a fixed hour.\n\nExample (xviii)\n\"Emeka rejected a guaranteed offer while waiting for a much less certain one. His sister reminded him that A BIRD IN THE HAND IS WORTH TWO IN THE BUSH.\"\nA. Birds are valuable  B. Certain opportunities may be better than uncertain promises  C. Never accept any offer  D. Hunting is difficult\nSolution: The guaranteed offer is the \"bird in the hand\"; the uncertain future offer is the \"two in the bush\". Correct answer: B.\n\n9. HOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Read the full sentence to see what happened before and after the expression.\n2. Decide whether the expression is literal, idiomatic or a proverb giving advice.\n3. Replace it with a simple explanation of its meaning.\n4. For proverbs, identify the general lesson, not just the characters or objects mentioned.\n5. If the question tests the correct wording, check the exact preposition and fixed word order.\n\nStep by step:\n\"After three unsuccessful attempts, the player THREW IN THE TOWEL.\"\nContext: repeated failure. Meaning: stopped trying. Eliminate options about literal towels, celebration or extra effort. Select \"gave up\".\n\n10. EXTRA WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (xix)\n\"His excuse DID NOT HOLD WATER.\"\nA. was unconvincing  B. was waterproof  C. was extremely long  D. was approved\nSolution: \"Does not hold water\" means an argument or explanation is not convincing. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xx)\n\"The principal told the pupils to KEEP THEIR EYES PEELED.\"\nA. close their eyes  B. remain alert  C. sleep early  D. avoid reading\nSolution: The idiom means watch carefully or stay alert. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxi)\n\"The politician was ON THE FENCE about the proposal.\"\nA. fully committed  B. undecided  C. physically climbing  D. completely opposed\nSolution: \"On the fence\" means unwilling or unable to choose a side. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxii)\n\"Her uncle told her NOT TO COUNT HER CHICKENS BEFORE THEY HATCHED.\"\nA. avoid planning entirely  B. not assume success before it is certain  C. never keep poultry  D. spend expected money quickly\nSolution: The proverb warns against treating a hoped-for result as guaranteed. Correct answer: B.\n\n11. COMMON EXAM TRAPS\nAn option may describe the literal picture but miss the idiomatic meaning. Another may express a related emotion without giving the real message. Some expressions have different meanings depending on context; do not force the same explanation into every sentence. For proverbs, avoid a narrow interpretation of a single word.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) \"Spill the beans\": reveal a secret.\n(ii) \"Go the extra mile\": make more effort than expected.\n(iii) \"Look before you leap\": consider consequences before acting.\n(iv) \"At sixes and sevens\": confused or disorganised.\n(v) \"In the long run\": over an extended period or eventually.\nTry to create one original sentence for each expression.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nPractise the linked JAMB questions below. For each answer, state the figurative meaning or the proverb's lesson and explain how the sentence supports it."}},{"id":"interpretation","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Interpreting sentences (what the sentence means)","body":"\"Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence\" tests careful reading of small words.\n\nHow to answer:\n1. Find the key word that controls the meaning: at least, either, allegedly, hardly ever, must not, could not have… more.\n2. Restate the sentence in plain words, then find the option that says exactly that, no more and no less.\n\nKey words to know:\n• at least 30 = 30 or more (never less)\n• either end = both ends\n• allegedly = claimed but not proven\n• hardly ever = almost never\n• must not = forbidden; need not = not necessary\n• could not have rewarded more handsomely = rewarded as generously as possible\n• the first free elections for five years = the last free ones were five years ago\n• at once = immediately (not \"once and for all\")\n\nCommon traps:\n• Literal readings of proverbs or idioms.\n• Options that add information (\"without feeling guilt\", \"he was handsome\").\n• Options that reverse the meaning.\n\nQuick check: \"The official had allegedly been taking bribes.\" Proved, or accused? Accused. \"Allegedly\" means not proven."},"new":{"title":"Interpreting sentences (what the sentence means)","body":"SENTENCE INTERPRETATION — UNDERSTANDING EXACTLY WHAT A STATEMENT CONVEYS\n\nINTRODUCTION\nIn UTME Use of English, questions such as \"Choose the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence\" test careful reading, not merely vocabulary. A sentence may contain a small word—only, hardly, unless, at least, allegedly, even, must, could or would—that changes the whole meaning. Your task is to restate the sentence accurately, without adding facts, reversing its meaning or making it stronger than the original.\n\n1. MEANING VERSUS WHAT WE ASSUME\nA sentence tells us certain things and leaves others unknown. \"Ada was the only student who passed\" means Ada passed and no other student passed. \"Ada was among the students who passed\" means Ada passed, but others also may have passed. The second statement does not tell us how many passed. Examiners exploit this distinction by offering answers that sound plausible but go beyond the sentence.\n\nExample (i)\n\"Only two candidates completed the task.\"\nA. At least two completed it. B. Exactly two completed it. C. More than two completed it. D. None completed it.\nSolution: \"Only two\" restricts the number to two. \"At least two\" allows more, so A is too broad. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\n\"Not all the workers supported the proposal.\"\nA. No worker supported it. B. Every worker opposed it. C. At least one worker did not support it. D. Every worker supported it.\nSolution: \"Not all\" denies universal support; it does not mean nobody supported it. C is the necessary conclusion. Correct answer: C.\n\n2. QUANTITY WORDS: ALL, SOME, FEW, ONLY, AT LEAST AND AT MOST\n\"At least 30\" means 30 or more. \"At most 30\" means 30 or fewer. \"Exactly 30\" means neither more nor fewer. \"Few\" suggests a small number; \"a few\" means some. \"Hardly any\" means almost none. \"Most\" means more than half, not necessarily everyone. Never turn \"some\" into \"all\" or \"most\" into \"every\".\n\nExample (iii)\n\"The school admitted at least fifty pupils.\"\nA. Fewer than fifty were admitted. B. Exactly fifty were admitted. C. Fifty or more were admitted. D. More than one hundred were admitted.\nSolution: \"At least\" sets a minimum, not an exact figure. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"No more than three representatives may attend.\"\nA. At least three must attend. B. Up to three may attend. C. Exactly three must attend. D. Four may attend.\nSolution: \"No more than three\" sets a maximum; zero, one, two or three are permitted by that limit. Correct answer: B.\n\n3. NEGATION: NOT, NEVER, HARDLY, SCARCELY AND DOUBLE NEGATIVES\nNegation can reverse a statement or limit its force. \"He does not always arrive early\" means there are occasions when he is not early; it does not mean he is never early. \"Hardly ever\" means very rarely. \"Not impossible\" means possible, though it does not promise success. Two negative words may cancel or create a nuanced positive meaning depending on the sentence.\n\nExample (v)\n\"The manager is not always punctual.\"\nA. He is never punctual. B. He is punctual on every occasion. C. He is sometimes not punctual. D. He is always late.\nSolution: \"Not always\" allows some punctual occasions and some exceptions. C is the accurate interpretation. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"The plan is not without merit.\"\nA. It has no merit. B. It has some merit. C. It must succeed. D. It is the best plan.\nSolution: \"Not without\" denies the absence of merit; the plan has at least some value. It does not guarantee success. Correct answer: B.\n\n4. MODAL VERBS: MUST, MAY, MIGHT, SHOULD, CAN AND NEED\nModal verbs express permission, possibility, obligation, advice and deduction. \"Must submit\" expresses an obligation; \"may submit\" can express permission. \"Might be true\" expresses possibility, not certainty. \"Should apologise\" gives advice or expectation, not necessarily a command. \"Need not attend\" means attendance is unnecessary; \"must not attend\" means attendance is prohibited.\n\nExample (vii)\n\"Candidates need not bring calculators.\"\nA. Calculators are forbidden. B. Candidates are not required to bring calculators. C. Every candidate must bring one. D. Candidates cannot use any tools.\nSolution: \"Need not\" removes an obligation; it does not automatically ban calculators. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (viii)\n\"You must not enter the laboratory without permission.\"\nA. Entry is compulsory. B. Entry is prohibited unless permission is given. C. Entry is optional for everyone. D. Permission is unnecessary.\nSolution: \"Must not\" states a prohibition. The condition specifies when entry is allowed. Correct answer: B.\n\n5. UNCERTAINTY, REPORTING AND EVIDENCE\n\"Allegedly\" means something is claimed but not established as fact. \"Apparently\" means it seems so from available information, but the speaker may not be certain. \"Reportedly\" attributes the information to reports. \"Presumably\" signals an assumption. Do not convert a report into a proven event.\n\nExample (ix)\n\"The contractor allegedly diverted the funds.\"\nA. The contractor has been proved guilty. B. Someone claims the contractor diverted the funds. C. The funds were definitely recovered. D. No allegation was made.\nSolution: \"Allegedly\" marks an unproven accusation. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (x)\n\"Apparently, the examination has been postponed.\"\nA. The speaker presents postponement as something that appears to be the case. B. The examination can never be held. C. Everyone has confirmed the date. D. The speaker arranged the postponement.\nSolution: \"Apparently\" signals how the information appears; it does not establish who made the decision. Correct answer: A.\n\n6. CONDITIONS: IF, UNLESS, PROVIDED THAT AND ONLY IF\n\"If it rains, the match will be postponed\" tells us what happens if rain occurs. It does not necessarily tell us what happens if it does not rain. \"Unless you study, you may fail\" means failure is a possibility if you do not study; studying is presented as a way to avoid that risk. \"Only if\" introduces a necessary condition: \"You may enter only if you have a pass\" means having a pass is required for entry, not that a pass guarantees entry under every circumstance.\n\nExample (xi)\n\"You will be admitted only if you present your ticket.\"\nA. A ticket is unnecessary. B. A ticket is required for admission. C. Everyone with a ticket must be admitted regardless of other rules. D. Admission is impossible.\nSolution: \"Only if\" identifies a necessary condition, not a universal guarantee. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"Unless the rain stops, the match will not begin.\"\nA. The match will begin during heavy rain. B. The match cannot begin while the rain continues. C. The match has been cancelled permanently. D. The match began yesterday.\nSolution: The statement makes stopping of rain necessary for the match to begin. Correct answer: B.\n\n7. COMPARISONS AND DEGREE\n\"Not as tall as\" means shorter than. \"No less difficult than\" means at least as difficult as. \"The least expensive\" identifies the lowest price within the comparison group; it does not mean the item is cheap in absolute terms. \"Could not have been more helpful\" often means extremely helpful, not unhelpful.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"Bisi is not as old as her brother.\"\nA. Bisi is older. B. They are the same age. C. Her brother is older. D. Their ages are unknown.\nSolution: \"Not as old as\" indicates Bisi is younger. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"The teacher could not have explained the topic more clearly.\"\nA. The explanation was very clear. B. The teacher refused to explain. C. The topic was impossible to explain. D. The explanation was confusing.\nSolution: This construction expresses the highest possible degree of clarity in the speaker's judgement. Correct answer: A.\n\n8. TIME, SEQUENCE AND FREQUENCY\n\"By Friday\" means no later than Friday; \"on Friday\" specifies that day. \"Since Monday\" points from Monday to a later reference point; \"for three days\" states a duration. \"As soon as\" indicates immediate succession. \"Not until\" means an event did not occur before a stated time. \"At once\" means immediately, not permanently.\n\nExample (xv)\n\"The results will not be released until Monday.\"\nA. They must be released on Sunday. B. They will not be released before Monday. C. They were released last week. D. They can never be released.\nSolution: Monday is the earliest stated release time; the sentence does not guarantee the results will appear exactly on Monday. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"Submit the form by noon.\"\nA. Submit after noon. B. Submit no later than noon. C. Submit at midnight. D. Submit only tomorrow.\nSolution: \"By\" gives a deadline. Correct answer: B.\n\n9. EMPHASIS, FOCUS AND CONTRAST\nWord order and expressions such as \"even\", \"rather than\", \"not only\" and \"in spite of\" control focus. \"Even the principal attended\" suggests the principal's attendance was surprising or notable. \"He chose tea rather than coffee\" tells us his preference in that instance, not that he dislikes coffee in general. \"Although\" contrasts two facts without denying either.\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"Even the youngest pupil solved the problem.\"\nA. The youngest pupil failed. B. The youngest pupil solved it, which was noteworthy. C. Only the youngest pupil solved it. D. Nobody solved it.\nSolution: \"Even\" highlights a surprising or notable case. It does not mean \"only\". Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xviii)\n\"Although the road was rough, the driver arrived on time.\"\nA. The road was smooth. B. The driver arrived late. C. The driver arrived on time despite the poor road. D. The driver avoided the road.\nSolution: \"Although\" acknowledges a difficulty that did not prevent the result. Correct answer: C.\n\n10. IDIOMATIC AND NON-LITERAL SENTENCES\nSentence interpretation may involve expressions whose meaning is not literal. \"He left no stone unturned\" means he made every reasonable effort to search or investigate. \"The news came as a bolt from the blue\" means it was unexpected. You must interpret the whole expression rather than each word separately.\n\nExample (xix)\n\"The investigators left no stone unturned.\"\nA. They searched thoroughly. B. They collected stones. C. They refused to investigate. D. They abandoned the case.\nSolution: The idiom means they made a thorough effort. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xx)\n\"The announcement took everyone by surprise.\"\nA. Everyone expected it. B. Nobody was surprised. C. It was unexpected. D. It was made in secret.\nSolution: \"Took everyone by surprise\" means the announcement was unexpected. It does not necessarily mean it was secret. Correct answer: C.\n\n11. HOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Underline the controlling words: only, at least, not all, unless, might, allegedly, until and so on.\n2. Restate the sentence in simple English before reading the options.\n3. Test whether each option adds a claim, removes a qualification or reverses the relationship.\n4. Prefer the option that says exactly what the sentence supports—no more and no less.\n5. Read the chosen option back against the original sentence.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n\"Some\" does not mean \"all\"; \"not all\" does not mean \"none\"; \"need not\" does not mean \"must not\"; \"allegedly\" does not mean \"proven\"; \"by Friday\" does not necessarily mean \"on Friday\". An option may repeat most of the original words while changing one crucial word.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) \"At most ten\" permits which numbers? Ten or fewer.\n(ii) \"She hardly ever travels\" means what? She travels very rarely.\n(iii) \"You need not attend\" means what? Attendance is not required.\n(iv) \"The claim is allegedly true\" proves what? Nothing; it reports an allegation.\n(v) \"He was not entirely satisfied\" means what? His satisfaction was incomplete, not necessarily absent.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nUse the linked JAMB sentence-interpretation questions. Explain the controlling word in each sentence before selecting the option."}},{"id":"concord","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Subject–verb agreement (concord)","body":"The verb must agree with its true subject: singular subject, singular verb; plural subject, plural verb.\n\nRules JAMB tests:\n1. Find the real subject. Ignore phrases like \"with\", \"along with\", \"as well as\": \"The chairman, with his members, WAS present.\"\n2. Neither…nor / either…or: the verb agrees with the nearer subject. \"Neither Agbo nor his parents ATTEND.\"\n3. Fractions and \"of\" phrases: agree with the noun after \"of\". \"Three quarters of the hostel HAS been painted; three quarters of the students HAVE moved in.\"\n4. \"A range/number of + plural noun\" usually takes a plural verb. \"A wide range of options WERE made available.\"\n5. Collective nouns (government, jury, team): as one body, use a singular verb and \"its\". As individuals, use a plural verb and \"their\". Never mix them (\"was… their\" is wrong).\n6. Keep the tense consistent with time words (now, yesterday).\n\nCommon traps:\n• Agreeing with the nearest noun when it isn't the subject.\n• Mixing singular and plural in one sentence.\n\nQuick check: \"The government which ___ recruiting ___ workers stopped.\" Was/its or is/their? Was/its: one body, past time."},"new":{"title":"Subject–verb agreement (concord)","body":"CONCORD — SUBJECT–VERB AGREEMENT AND OTHER AGREEMENT RULES\n\nINTRODUCTION\nConcord means agreement between parts of a sentence. In JAMB Use of English, the most common form is subject–verb agreement: a singular subject usually takes a singular present-tense verb, while a plural subject takes a plural present-tense verb. But concord also concerns pronouns, number, collective nouns and special expressions. Do not choose a verb simply because the nearest noun is singular or plural; first identify the actual subject.\n\n1. THE BASIC RULE AND THE REAL SUBJECT\nIn the present simple tense, \"he/she/it works\" but \"I/you/we/they work\". \"The boy plays\"; \"the boys play\". Forms of \"be\" differ: \"I am\", \"he is\", \"they are\"; past forms include \"was\" and \"were\". Some verbs, such as modals, do not change for a singular subject: \"She can swim\", not \"she cans swim\".\n\nExample (i)\n\"The students ___ ready for the test.\"\nA. is B. are C. was D. has\nSolution: \"Students\" is plural, and the sentence is in present time. Use \"are\". Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\n\"Every pupil ___ a textbook.\"\nA. have B. has C. are D. were\nSolution: \"Every\" modifies singular \"pupil\", so the verb is singular \"has\". Correct answer: B.\n\n2. PHRASES BETWEEN SUBJECT AND VERB\nA phrase beginning with \"of\", \"with\", \"along with\", \"together with\", \"as well as\" or \"in addition to\" does not normally change the number of the main subject. Remove the phrase to test the agreement. \"The teacher, with the pupils, is here\" becomes \"The teacher is here\". But \"The teacher and the pupils are here\" has a genuinely plural compound subject.\n\nExample (iii)\n\"The box of old books ___ missing.\"\nA. are B. were C. is D. have\nSolution: \"Box\" is the head noun, not \"books\". Remove \"of old books\": \"The box is missing\". Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"The captain, together with his teammates, ___ arrived.\"\nA. have B. has C. are D. were\nSolution: The head subject is \"captain\", which is singular. \"Together with his teammates\" is additional information. Correct answer: B.\n\n3. TWO SUBJECTS JOINED BY AND\nTwo different people or things joined by \"and\" normally take a plural verb: \"Rice and beans are available.\" But two nouns may refer to one person or one idea: \"The founder and director of the school is speaking\" can describe one person who holds both roles. \"Bread and butter is my usual breakfast\" treats the combination as one dish. Use meaning and articles to decide.\n\nExample (v)\n\"The secretary and the treasurer ___ present.\"\nA. is B. are C. has D. was\nSolution: The repeated article \"the\" signals two officeholders, so the subject is plural. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"The author and illustrator of this book ___ receiving an award.\"\nA. are B. have C. is D. were\nSolution: With one article and a context indicating one person performing both roles, the singular verb \"is\" fits. If two people were intended, \"the author and the illustrator are\" would be clearer. Correct answer: C.\n\n4. EITHER ... OR AND NEITHER ... NOR\nWith two singular subjects, use a singular verb: \"Neither Ada nor Bisi is absent.\" When one subject is singular and the other plural, formal examination English commonly follows the nearer subject: \"Neither the teacher nor the pupils are ready\"; \"Neither the pupils nor the teacher is ready\". Restructure awkward combinations in ordinary writing when possible.\n\nExample (vii)\n\"Neither the principal nor the teachers ___ available.\"\nA. is B. are C. has D. was\nSolution: The nearer subject, \"teachers\", is plural. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (viii)\n\"Either the boys or their mother ___ responsible.\"\nA. are B. were C. is D. have\nSolution: The nearer subject, \"mother\", is singular. Correct answer: C.\n\n5. EACH, EVERY, EVERYONE, SOMEBODY, NOBODY AND ONE\n\"Each\", \"every\", \"everyone\", \"someone\", \"anybody\", \"nobody\", \"either\" and \"neither\" are usually singular when they function as the subject. \"Everyone is here\"; \"Each of the books has a cover\". The plural noun after \"of\" does not change the singular head. However, \"both\", \"several\", \"many\" and \"few\" are plural.\n\nExample (ix)\n\"Each of the contestants ___ given a number.\"\nA. were B. are C. was D. have\nSolution: The head is singular \"each\"; the past passive construction requires \"was given\". Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (x)\n\"Several of the candidates ___ absent.\"\nA. was B. is C. has D. were\nSolution: \"Several\" is plural; the past-time verb is \"were\". Correct answer: D.\n\n6. A NUMBER OF VERSUS THE NUMBER OF\n\"A number of students are waiting\" means several students are waiting; the verb is plural. \"The number of students is increasing\" refers to a single total or figure; the verb is singular. This distinction is frequently tested.\n\nExample (xi)\n\"A number of workers ___ complained.\"\nA. has B. have C. is D. was\nSolution: \"A number of\" means several, so \"have\" agrees with the plural workers. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"The number of workers ___ increasing.\"\nA. are B. have C. is D. were\nSolution: \"The number\" is a singular figure. Correct answer: C.\n\n7. FRACTIONS, PERCENTAGES, SOME, ALL AND MOST\nWith fractions and percentages followed by \"of\", agreement often follows the noun after \"of\". \"Half of the water is gone\" because water is uncountable. \"Half of the pupils are absent\" because pupils is plural. \"Most of the rice is cooked\"; \"Most of the books are new\". Context matters when a percentage is considered a single statistic.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"Three quarters of the land ___ cultivated.\"\nA. are B. is C. have D. were\nSolution: \"Land\" is uncountable and treated as singular here. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"Three quarters of the players ___ arrived.\"\nA. has B. is C. have D. was\nSolution: \"Players\" is plural; use \"have\". Correct answer: C.\n\n8. COLLECTIVE NOUNS\nCollective nouns include team, committee, jury, family, audience and government. When the group is viewed as one unit, use a singular verb: \"The committee has issued its report.\" In British English, a plural verb may be used when members are viewed individually: \"The committee are divided in their opinions.\" Keep the intended meaning and pronouns consistent. Examination questions often expect singular agreement for a group acting as one body.\n\nExample (xv)\n\"The team ___ won the championship.\"\nA. have B. has C. are D. were\nSolution: The team is treated as a single unit that has won. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"The jury ___ divided in their opinions.\"\nA. is B. has C. are D. was\nSolution: \"Divided in their opinions\" focuses on individual members; plural agreement is natural in British English. Correct answer: C. Note: usage differs across varieties of English, so always read the entire sentence.\n\n9. NOUNS THAT LOOK PLURAL OR SINGULAR\nSome nouns ending in -s are singular in meaning: \"Mathematics is useful\", \"The news is encouraging\", \"Measles is contagious\". Some nouns are normally plural: \"The scissors are sharp\", \"My trousers are new\", \"The police are investigating\". \"A pair of scissors is on the table\" has singular \"pair\" as its head.\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"The news ___ encouraging.\"\nA. are B. were C. is D. have\nSolution: \"News\" is grammatically singular despite ending in -s. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xviii)\n\"A pair of trousers ___ missing.\"\nA. are B. is C. have D. were\nSolution: \"Pair\" is the singular head noun. Correct answer: B.\n\n10. THERE IS, THERE ARE AND INVERTED WORD ORDER\nIn \"There is a book on the desk\", the real subject follows the verb: \"a book\". In \"There are three books\", the subject is plural. Questions may also invert the verb and subject: \"Where are the pupils?\" The first word you see may not be the subject.\n\nExample (xix)\n\"There ___ several reasons for the delay.\"\nA. is B. has C. are D. was\nSolution: \"Several reasons\" is plural, so use \"are\". Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xx)\n\"On the wall ___ two photographs.\"\nA. hangs B. is C. are D. has\nSolution: The subject \"two photographs\" follows the verb in this inverted sentence. Correct answer: C.\n\n11. RELATIVE CLAUSES AND AGREEMENT\nIn \"She is one of the students who work hard\", \"who\" refers to \"students\" and takes \"work\". In \"She is the only one of the students who works hard\", the focus is the singular \"only one\", so \"works\" is natural. Read the antecedent carefully before choosing.\n\nExample (xxi)\n\"She is one of the athletes who ___ every morning.\"\nA. trains B. train C. is training D. has trained\nSolution: \"Who\" refers to the plural \"athletes\", so \"train\" is required. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxii)\n\"He is the only one of the players who ___ selected.\"\nA. were B. have C. was D. are\nSolution: \"The only one\" is singular; \"was selected\" fits. Correct answer: C.\n\n12. PRONOUN AGREEMENT AND CONSISTENCY\nPronouns should refer clearly to their antecedents. \"The school has changed its timetable\" uses \"its\" for the school as an institution. \"The pupils have collected their books\" uses \"their\" for plural pupils. Singular \"they\" is also standard English for an unspecified or gender-neutral person, but a JAMB question may focus on consistency with a specific grammatical construction. Avoid ambiguous references.\n\nExample (xxiii)\n\"The companies have submitted ___ reports.\"\nA. its B. their C. his D. her\nSolution: \"Companies\" is plural, so \"their\" agrees. Correct answer: B.\n\n13. HOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Find the verb and ask: who or what performs the action?\n2. Identify the head of the subject; temporarily remove descriptive phrases.\n3. Check special expressions such as each, neither ... nor, a number of and fractions of.\n4. Decide whether a collective noun is treated as a unit or as individuals.\n5. Read the complete sentence to confirm tense and pronoun consistency.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\nThe noun nearest the blank is often not the true subject. \"The quality of the oranges is\" does not become plural because \"oranges\" is plural. \"A number of\" and \"the number of\" behave differently. \"News\" is singular; \"scissors\" is plural. With either ... or, check the nearer subject.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) \"Each of the girls ___ ready.\" Answer: is.\n(ii) \"The number of pupils ___ increasing.\" Answer: is.\n(iii) \"A number of pupils ___ waiting.\" Answer: are.\n(iv) \"Half of the rice ___ cooked.\" Answer: is.\n(v) \"Neither the boys nor their mother ___ here.\" Answer: is.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nPractise the mapped JAMB concord questions below. Explain the true subject and the applicable rule before selecting an answer."}},{"id":"tenses","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Tenses and verb forms","body":"JAMB tests whether the verb form fits the time and the grammar around it.\n\nRules:\n1. Finished past time (yesterday, last night, in 2001) takes the simple past: \"The president SIGNED it yesterday.\" Never \"has signed yesterday\".\n2. Earlier past (before another past action) takes the past perfect: \"He HAD STUDIED for five years before he got the certificate.\"\n3. Present perfect continuous (has/have been + -ing) is for something started earlier and still going: \"She has been working since morning.\"\n4. Used to: \"used to + base verb\" is a past habit (they used to grow rice). \"Be used to + -ing\" means accustomed to.\n5. After see/hear/watch + object, use the base verb: \"saw the culprit APPROACH\". Also \"sees crowds GATHER\".\n6. \"Look forward to\", \"object to\" and \"be used to\" take -ing: \"look forward to SEEING you\".\n7. Irregular verbs: cost-cost-cost, lay-laid-laid, lie-lay-lain. \"Costed\" only means \"worked out the cost of\".\n8. \"Having been told…\" is correct; \"having being\" is never correct.\n\nQuick check: \"This edifice ___ a fortune to build.\" Costed, cost, had costed or have cost? Cost."},"new":{"title":"Tenses and verb forms","body":"TENSES AND VERB FORMS — EXPRESSING TIME, COMPLETION AND CONTINUITY\n\nINTRODUCTION\nTense and aspect help us show when an action happens and how it unfolds. Tense places an event in time; aspect shows whether it is continuing, completed or related to another time. JAMB questions test time expressions, correct verb forms, sequence of events, reported speech, conditionals and constructions such as \"used to\" and \"look forward to\". Memorising a tense name is not enough: you must understand what each form communicates.\n\n1. THE SIMPLE PRESENT — HABITS, FACTS AND TIMETABLES\nUse the simple present for routines, general truths and scheduled events. \"She reads every evening\"; \"Water boils at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure\"; \"The train leaves at six.\" With he/she/it, the main verb often takes -s or -es. With \"does\" in a question or negative, use the base form: \"Does she read?\", not \"Does she reads?\"\n\nExample (i)\n\"Every morning, Musa ___ to school.\"\nA. walk B. walks C. walking D. walked\nSolution: \"Every morning\" signals a routine, and \"Musa\" is singular. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\n\"Does your sister ___ French?\"\nA. speaks B. speaking C. speak D. spoke\nSolution: \"Does\" already carries the present-tense marking; the main verb stays in the base form. Correct answer: C.\n\n2. THE PRESENT CONTINUOUS — ACTIONS IN PROGRESS\nForm: am/is/are + verb-ing. \"She is reading now\"; \"They are repairing the road this week.\" It often describes a temporary action or a planned near-future arrangement. Some stative verbs, such as \"know\", \"believe\" and \"own\", are not normally used in the continuous when expressing their usual states: \"I know the answer\", not \"I am knowing the answer\".\n\nExample (iii)\n\"Listen! Someone ___ at the door.\"\nA. knocks B. is knocking C. knocked D. has knock\nSolution: \"Listen!\" draws attention to an action happening now. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"She ___ the answer to the question.\"\nA. is knowing B. know C. knows D. has knowing\nSolution: \"Know\" expresses a state in this context, so the simple present is appropriate. Correct answer: C.\n\n3. THE SIMPLE PAST — COMPLETED ACTIONS AT A FINISHED TIME\nUse the simple past for completed actions at a definite past time: yesterday, last week, in 2018, two hours ago. Regular verbs usually add -ed; irregular verbs change form: go/went, write/wrote, see/saw, buy/bought. With \"did\" in a question or negative, use the base verb: \"Did she go?\", not \"Did she went?\"\n\nExample (v)\n\"The president ___ the document yesterday.\"\nA. has signed B. signs C. signed D. signing\nSolution: \"Yesterday\" is a finished past-time expression. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"Did you ___ the letter?\"\nA. wrote B. write C. written D. writing\nSolution: \"Did\" marks past time; the main verb takes its base form. Correct answer: B.\n\n4. THE PAST CONTINUOUS — AN ACTION IN PROGRESS IN THE PAST\nForm: was/were + verb-ing. It describes an action ongoing at a particular past moment. \"I was reading when the lights went out.\" The longer background action is often past continuous, while the interrupting event is simple past. Two past actions may also overlap: \"While I was cooking, my sister was studying.\"\n\nExample (vii)\n\"The children ___ when the teacher entered.\"\nA. were playing B. have played C. play D. will play\nSolution: Their activity was in progress at the moment the teacher entered. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (viii)\n\"While Ada ___, the phone rang.\"\nA. cooks B. was cooking C. has cooked D. will cook\nSolution: \"Was cooking\" describes the ongoing background action. Correct answer: B.\n\n5. THE PRESENT PERFECT — PAST ACTIONS CONNECTED TO NOW\nForm: has/have + past participle. Use it for experiences without a specified finished time (\"I have visited Kano\"), recent results relevant now (\"She has lost her keys\") and situations continuing up to now (\"He has lived here since 2020\"). Do not normally combine it with a definite finished past time such as \"yesterday\": \"She went yesterday\", not \"She has gone yesterday\".\n\nExample (ix)\n\"I ___ my homework already.\"\nA. finish B. have finished C. had finish D. finishing\nSolution: \"Already\" highlights completion relevant to the present. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (x)\n\"She ___ in Lagos since 2021.\"\nA. lives B. lived C. has lived D. is live\nSolution: The situation began in 2021 and continues to the present. Present perfect is appropriate. Correct answer: C.\n\n6. THE PRESENT PERFECT CONTINUOUS — DURATION UP TO NOW\nForm: has/have been + verb-ing. Use it to emphasise the duration or ongoing nature of an activity that began earlier: \"They have been waiting for two hours.\" \"Since\" gives the starting point (\"since Monday\"); \"for\" gives the duration (\"for three days\"). Sometimes both present perfect simple and continuous are possible but emphasise different things.\n\nExample (xi)\n\"The workers ___ since morning.\"\nA. work B. have been working C. had working D. were work\nSolution: The activity started earlier and continues now. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"She has been studying ___ three hours.\"\nA. since B. for C. by D. at\nSolution: \"Three hours\" is a duration, so use \"for\". Correct answer: B.\n\n7. THE PAST PERFECT — AN EARLIER PAST ACTION\nForm: had + past participle. Use it when one action was completed before another past action: \"The bus had left before we arrived.\" The order matters more than the order of clauses. Past perfect is not needed for every past event; use it when showing earlier completion is useful.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"By the time we arrived, the film ___.\"\nA. starts B. has started C. had started D. starting\nSolution: The film began before our arrival. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"After she ___ the work, she went home.\"\nA. had completed B. has complete C. completes D. was complete\nSolution: The work was completed before she went home. Past perfect makes the sequence explicit. Correct answer: A.\n\n8. THE PAST PERFECT CONTINUOUS — DURATION BEFORE A PAST POINT\nForm: had been + verb-ing. It emphasises how long an activity had continued before another past event: \"He had been studying for three hours when the power failed.\" Compare \"He had studied the chapter\" (completion) with \"He had been studying\" (ongoing duration).\n\nExample (xv)\n\"They ___ for an hour before the bus arrived.\"\nA. have waited B. had been waiting C. are waiting D. will wait\nSolution: The waiting continued for an hour before a later past event. Correct answer: B.\n\n9. THE FUTURE — WILL, GOING TO AND ARRANGEMENTS\n\"Will\" often expresses a decision, promise or prediction: \"I will call you.\" \"Be going to\" often describes a plan or prediction based on present evidence: \"Look at those clouds; it is going to rain.\" Present continuous can express an arranged future event: \"We are meeting the principal tomorrow.\" A simple present form may describe a timetable: \"The train leaves at noon.\"\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"Look at those dark clouds! It ___ rain.\"\nA. has B. is going to C. had D. did\nSolution: Visible clouds provide present evidence for the prediction. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"I promise I ___ return your book tomorrow.\"\nA. will B. have C. had D. was\nSolution: \"Will\" is natural for a promise. Correct answer: A.\n\n10. FUTURE PERFECT AND FUTURE CONTINUOUS\nFuture perfect: will have + past participle. It shows an action completed by a future deadline: \"By June, she will have finished the course.\" Future continuous: will be + verb-ing. It shows an activity in progress at a future time: \"At eight tomorrow, I will be writing the test.\" The key difference is completion versus activity in progress.\n\nExample (xviii)\n\"By next Friday, the builders ___ the roof.\"\nA. will have completed B. will completing C. have complete D. were complete\nSolution: \"By next Friday\" sets a future deadline; future perfect marks completion by then. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xix)\n\"At this time tomorrow, I ___ to Abuja.\"\nA. will be travelling B. have travelled C. travelled D. travel yesterday\nSolution: The journey will be in progress at a future moment. Correct answer: A.\n\n11. IRREGULAR VERBS AND PARTICIPLES\nThree forms often matter: base, simple past and past participle. Examples: go/went/gone; write/wrote/written; eat/ate/eaten; see/saw/seen; begin/began/begun; choose/chose/chosen; break/broke/broken. Use the past participle after have/has/had: \"She has written\", not \"has wrote\". Passive constructions also use the past participle: \"The letter was written.\"\n\nExample (xx)\n\"The letter has been ___.\"\nA. wrote B. writing C. written D. write\nSolution: \"Has been\" in this passive construction requires the past participle \"written\". Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xxi)\n\"She has ___ the correct answer.\"\nA. chose B. chosen C. choose D. choosing\nSolution: The past participle of \"choose\" is \"chosen\". Correct answer: B.\n\n12. USED TO, BE USED TO AND GET USED TO\n\"Used to + base verb\" expresses a past habit or state that no longer holds: \"They used to farm here.\" \"Be used to + noun/-ing\" means be accustomed to: \"She is used to waking early.\" \"Get used to + noun/-ing\" means become accustomed to: \"He is getting used to the new timetable.\" The \"to\" in the latter two is a preposition, so an -ing form follows.\n\nExample (xxii)\n\"She is used to ___ early.\"\nA. wake B. waking C. woke D. woken\nSolution: \"Be used to\" means accustomed to, and takes an -ing form. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxiii)\n\"My grandfather used to ___ long distances.\"\nA. walked B. walking C. walk D. walks\nSolution: Past habit \"used to\" takes the base verb \"walk\". Correct answer: C.\n\n13. VERB PATTERNS AFTER OTHER VERBS\nSome verbs take an infinitive: \"decide to leave\", \"hope to pass\", \"promise to return\". Others take an -ing form: \"enjoy reading\", \"avoid wasting\", \"suggest meeting\". Some accept both with a meaning difference: \"stop smoking\" means end the habit; \"stop to smoke\" means pause another activity in order to smoke. \"Look forward to\" and \"object to\" take -ing because \"to\" is a preposition.\n\nExample (xxiv)\n\"I look forward to ___ from you.\"\nA. hear B. hearing C. heard D. have heard\nSolution: \"Look forward to\" is followed by a noun or -ing form. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxv)\n\"The driver stopped ___ fuel.\"\nA. to buy B. buying C. bought D. buyed\nSolution: \"Stopped to buy\" means the driver paused the journey for the purpose of buying fuel. \"Stopped buying\" would mean discontinued the activity of buying. Correct answer: A.\n\n14. PERCEPTION VERBS AND CAUSATIVE STRUCTURES\nAfter \"see/hear/watch + object\", the base verb can describe a complete event: \"I saw her cross the road.\" The -ing form can emphasise an event in progress: \"I saw her crossing the road.\" After \"make + object\", use the base verb in the active voice: \"They made him apologise.\" In the passive, \"to\" appears: \"He was made to apologise.\"\n\nExample (xxvi)\n\"We saw the players ___ the field.\"\nA. entered B. enter C. enters D. to entered\nSolution: \"Saw + object + base verb\" is a standard pattern when the whole event is observed. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxvii)\n\"The coach made the players ___ again.\"\nA. trained B. to train C. train D. training\nSolution: Active causative \"make + object\" takes the base verb. Correct answer: C.\n\n15. TENSE CONSISTENCY, REPORTED SPEECH AND CONDITIONALS\nA narrative about completed past events generally keeps a past time frame unless the meaning shifts. In reported speech, present may change to past when the reporting verb is past: \"She said, 'I am tired'\" → \"She said that she was tired.\" But universal truths may remain present: \"The teacher said that water boils at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure.\" Conditional forms also matter: \"If it rains, we will stay home\" (real future possibility); \"If I had more time, I would study\" (hypothetical present); \"If I had studied, I would have passed\" (unreal past).\n\nExample (xxviii)\n\"If she studies hard, she ___ the examination.\"\nA. passed B. will pass C. had passed D. passing\nSolution: A real future conditional commonly uses present simple in the if-clause and \"will\" in the main clause. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxix)\n\"If I had known, I ___ earlier.\"\nA. will come B. would have come C. come D. have come\nSolution: \"If I had known\" refers to an unreal past condition; the result uses \"would have + past participle\". Correct answer: B.\n\n16. HOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Identify the time marker: yesterday, now, since, for, by the time, tomorrow.\n2. Decide whether the action is habitual, ongoing, completed or earlier than another event.\n3. Check the subject and auxiliary verb: has/have/had, am/is/are, was/were, will.\n4. Check whether the verb must be base, -ing or past participle.\n5. Read the completed sentence and check that its time relationships make sense.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n\"Has wrote\" is wrong; use \"has written\". \"Did went\" is wrong; use \"did go\". \"Since three hours\" is wrong when giving a duration; use \"for three hours\". \"Used to waking\" is wrong for past habit but correct in \"is used to waking\". \"Look forward to hear\" is wrong; use \"look forward to hearing\". A present perfect form usually does not combine with \"yesterday\" for a finished past event.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) \"She ___ yesterday.\" Answer: went (not has gone).\n(ii) \"They have been waiting ___ two hours.\" Answer: for.\n(iii) \"He has ___ a letter.\" Answer: written.\n(iv) \"I am used to ___ early.\" Answer: waking.\n(v) \"If I had known, I ___ helped.\" Answer: would have.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nUse the mapped JAMB tense questions below. Before choosing, identify the time marker, the relationship between events and the verb form required."}},{"id":"conditionals","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Conditionals and inversion","body":"Conditional sentences follow fixed tense patterns.\n\nThe patterns:\n1. Real or likely: If + present, will + verb. \"If he arrives late, everyone WILL blame him.\"\n2. Imaginary present: If + past, would + verb. \"If I WERE you, I would…\" (use \"were\" for every person).\n3. Imaginary past: If + had + past participle, would have + past participle. \"If he had considered it, he WOULD HAVE stepped aside.\"\n\nInversion: drop \"if\" and flip the subject and auxiliary.\n• \"Had he considered his image…, he would have…\"\n• \"Should you come early…\" = \"If you come early…\"\n• After \"Hardly\", \"Scarcely\" and \"No sooner\" at the start: \"Hardly HAD HE arrived when…\", \"No sooner had he arrived THAN…\"\n\nCommon traps:\n• Mixing patterns (\"If he arrives, he would…\").\n• \"Should in case\" (doubled; use one or the other).\n• \"If I was you\" (exam English wants \"were\").\n\nQuick check: \"Hardly ___ when the storm began.\" Had he arrived, he arrived, did he arrive or he had arrived? Had he arrived."},"new":{"title":"Conditionals and inversion","body":"CONDITIONALS AND INVERSION — HOW CONDITIONS CHANGE MEANING\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA conditional sentence describes a situation and its possible result. It usually has an if-clause (the condition) and a main clause (the result): \"If it rains, we will stay indoors.\" Understanding conditionals requires more than memorising tenses. Ask: Is the situation generally true, a real future possibility, imaginary now, or an unreal event in the past? Inversion changes normal word order, often to create a formal conditional or to emphasise a negative expression. JAMB tests both meaning and grammatical pattern.\n\n1. THE TWO PARTS OF A CONDITIONAL SENTENCE\n\"If the road is flooded, the bus will turn back.\" The condition is \"if the road is flooded\"; the result is \"the bus will turn back\". The clauses may exchange positions: \"The bus will turn back if the road is flooded.\" A comma is normally used when the if-clause comes first. The condition does not claim that the road is already flooded.\n\nExample (i)\n\"If the generator fails, the examination will be delayed.\"\nQuestion: What is certain from this statement?\nA. The generator has failed. B. The examination has been cancelled. C. A failure would cause a delay. D. The examination has already begun.\nSolution: The speaker describes a possible condition and its result, not an event that has occurred. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (ii)\n\"The match will continue if the rain stops.\"\nQuestion: What condition is mentioned?\nSolution: The rain must stop for the stated continuation to occur. The sentence does not tell us whether the rain has stopped.\n\n2. ZERO CONDITIONAL — GENERAL RULES AND REGULAR RESULTS\nPattern: If + present simple, present simple. Use this when a condition regularly produces a result: \"If water reaches its boiling point under the stated conditions, it boils.\" \"If pupils arrive late, the gatekeeper records their names.\" The pattern can describe scientific facts, procedures and school rules; it does not necessarily refer to a single future event.\n\nExample (iii)\n\"If you heat ice, it ___.\"\nA. melted B. melts C. would melt D. had melted\nSolution: The sentence states a general result, so both clauses take the simple present. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"If the alarm rings, the guards ___ the building.\"\nA. inspect B. inspected C. would inspect D. had inspected\nSolution: This describes a regular security procedure. Use the present simple \"inspect\". Correct answer: A.\n\n3. FIRST CONDITIONAL — A REAL FUTURE POSSIBILITY\nPattern: If + present simple, will + base verb. \"If Ada studies, she will improve.\" The event is possible, not guaranteed. \"If\" introduces a condition; \"when\" often suggests an event expected to occur. In a future time or condition clause, use present simple rather than \"will\" in ordinary exam English: \"If she arrives tomorrow, we will begin\", not \"If she will arrive tomorrow...\" when simply stating a condition. The result may also use can, may or an imperative: \"If you finish early, you may leave.\"\n\nExample (v)\n\"If the rain ___, the farmers will plant.\"\nA. stops B. will stop C. stopped D. had stopped\nSolution: The future result \"will plant\" pairs with present simple in the if-clause. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"If you see the principal, ___ her this letter.\"\nA. gave B. give C. had given D. would have given\nSolution: A real condition can have an imperative result: \"give her this letter\". Correct answer: B.\n\n4. SECOND CONDITIONAL — IMAGINARY OR UNLIKELY PRESENT/FUTURE\nPattern: If + past simple, would + base verb. \"If I had a car, I would drive to school.\" This does not say I had a car yesterday; the past form expresses distance from present reality. \"If I were you, I would apologise\" is the traditional formal examination form for advice. \"Could\" may express ability; \"might\" expresses possibility: \"If I had time, I could help.\"\n\nExample (vii)\n\"If I ___ the governor, I would improve the roads.\"\nA. am B. were C. have been D. will be\nSolution: The speaker imagines a position not held now. Formal hypothetical \"were\" fits. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (viii)\n\"If he knew the answer, he ___ us.\"\nA. tells B. will tell C. would tell D. had told\nSolution: \"If he knew\" is an imagined present condition. The matching result is \"would tell\". Correct answer: C.\n\n5. THIRD CONDITIONAL — AN UNREAL PAST\nPattern: If + had + past participle, would have + past participle. This imagines changing something that did not happen. \"If she had left earlier, she would have caught the bus\" implies she did not leave early enough and did not catch it. The result may also use \"could have\" or \"might have\" to show ability or uncertainty. Do not use \"would have\" in the if-clause in standard third-conditionals.\n\nExample (ix)\n\"If the goalkeeper had moved faster, he ___ the ball.\"\nA. will catch B. would catch C. would have caught D. catches\nSolution: The imagined condition is in the past (\"had moved\"). The unreal past result is \"would have caught\". Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (x)\n\"If I had known about the meeting, I ___ attended.\"\nA. will have B. would have C. would D. had\nSolution: The speaker refers to an unreal past opportunity. \"Would have attended\" completes the third conditional. Correct answer: B.\n\n6. MIXED CONDITIONALS — PAST CAUSE, PRESENT RESULT\nA mixed conditional links different times. \"If I had studied medicine, I would be a doctor now.\" The condition concerns an unreal past choice; the result concerns the present. Compare: \"If I had studied medicine, I would have become a doctor\" (past result). Use the time markers and meaning to decide which pattern is needed.\n\nExample (xi)\n\"If she had saved regularly, she ___ financially secure now.\"\nA. would be B. would have been C. will be D. is being\nSolution: \"Had saved\" imagines a different past; \"now\" makes the result present. \"Would be\" fits. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"If he were more careful, he ___ that mistake yesterday.\"\nA. will avoid B. would have avoided C. avoids D. had avoided\nSolution: A present general characteristic is imagined as different, with an unreal past result. \"Would have avoided\" fits. Correct answer: B.\n\n7. UNLESS, PROVIDED THAT, AS LONG AS AND IN CASE\n\"Unless\" usually means \"if not\": \"Unless you revise, you may struggle\" means if you do not revise, you may struggle. \"Provided that\" and \"as long as\" introduce requirements: \"You may enter provided that you show your pass.\" \"In case\" often expresses precaution: \"Take an umbrella in case it rains.\" This differs from \"if it rains, take an umbrella\": the first suggests taking it before rain begins. Avoid \"unless ... not\" when it creates an unintended double negative.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"Unless the fee is paid, registration will not be completed.\"\nA. Registration is completed without payment. B. Payment is required for completion. C. The fee has been refunded. D. Registration has been cancelled forever.\nSolution: \"Unless\" means \"if not\"; the fee must be paid for the stated completion. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"Take your phone charger ___ the battery runs low.\"\nA. in case B. unless C. although D. despite\nSolution: The charger is taken as a precaution before a possible problem. \"In case\" fits. Correct answer: A.\n\n8. CONDITIONAL INVERSION — FORMAL ALTERNATIVES TO IF\nIn formal English, \"if\" can sometimes be omitted and the auxiliary placed before the subject. \"If I had known\" becomes \"Had I known\"; \"If you should need help\" becomes \"Should you need help\"; \"If I were the leader\" becomes \"Were I the leader\". The meaning stays conditional. These are not ordinary questions even though the word order resembles a question.\n\nExample (xv)\n\"___ I known the truth, I would have acted differently.\"\nA. Have B. Had C. Did D. Would\nSolution: This is inversion of \"If I had known the truth\". Begin with \"Had\". Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"___ you require assistance, contact the office.\"\nA. Should B. Had C. Would D. Were\nSolution: \"Should you require\" is a formal way to say \"If you should require\". Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"___ I in your position, I would decline.\"\nA. Was B. Had C. Were D. Should\nSolution: \"Were I in your position\" is inverted hypothetical \"If I were in your position\". Correct answer: C.\n\n9. NEGATIVE-ADVERB INVERSION — HARDLY, SCARCELY AND NO SOONER\nWhen certain negative or limiting expressions begin a sentence, the auxiliary normally comes before the subject: \"Hardly had she arrived when the storm began.\" \"No sooner had she arrived than the storm began.\" Learn the pairs: hardly/scarcely ... when; no sooner ... than. They express two events happening in quick succession. The first event uses past perfect in these common examination constructions.\n\nExample (xviii)\n\"Hardly ___ the station when the train departed.\"\nA. we reached B. had we reached C. we had reached D. did we reaching\nSolution: Initial \"Hardly\" triggers inversion: \"had we reached\". The full sentence is \"Hardly had we reached the station when the train departed.\" Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xix)\n\"No sooner had the bell rung ___ the pupils rushed out.\"\nA. when B. then C. than D. that\nSolution: The fixed construction is \"No sooner ... than\". Correct answer: C.\n\n10. OTHER EMPHATIC INVERSION\n\"Never have I seen such a crowd.\" \"Rarely does she complain.\" \"Not only did he apologise, but he also corrected the mistake.\" \"Only after the rain stopped did the match begin.\" In each, fronting a negative or limiting expression changes the normal order. If there is no auxiliary in the ordinary sentence, add do/does/did as appropriate: \"She rarely complains\" → \"Rarely does she complain.\"\n\nExample (xx)\n\"Never ___ such a beautiful performance.\"\nA. I have seen B. have I seen C. I saw D. I seeing\nSolution: Initial \"Never\" requires auxiliary–subject inversion: \"have I seen\". Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxi)\n\"Not only ___ the test, but she also won a prize.\"\nA. she passed B. did she pass C. she did passed D. passed she\nSolution: \"Not only\" at the front triggers \"did she pass\". After \"did\", use the base verb \"pass\". Correct answer: B.\n\n11. COMMON ERRORS AND WHY THEY ARE WRONG\n\"If he will arrive, we will start\" is generally wrong for a straightforward future condition; use \"If he arrives\". \"If I would have known\" mixes the third conditional; use \"If I had known\". \"Had he knew\" is wrong because \"had\" takes a past participle: \"Had he known\". \"Hardly he had arrived\" lacks required inversion. \"No sooner ... when\" mismatches the standard pair; use \"than\". Avoid treating every inverted conditional as a question.\n\nExample (xxii)\nChoose the correct sentence:\nA. If she had studied, she will pass. B. If she studies, she would pass. C. If she had studied, she would have passed. D. Had she studied, she will passed.\nSolution: C correctly matches an unreal past condition with an unreal past result. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xxiii)\n\"Scarcely had the visitors sat down ___ the lights went out.\"\nA. than B. when C. that D. until\nSolution: The standard pair is \"scarcely ... when\". Correct answer: B.\n\n12. HOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Identify the time and reality: general fact, real future, imagined present, unreal past or mixed time.\n2. Locate the condition and result; check the verb form in each.\n3. For an initial \"Had\", \"Were\" or \"Should\", ask whether \"if\" has been omitted.\n4. For \"Hardly\", \"Scarcely\", \"No sooner\", \"Never\" and \"Not only\", check auxiliary–subject inversion.\n5. Check fixed pairs (\"hardly ... when\"; \"no sooner ... than\") and read the whole sentence.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) \"If it rains, we ___ indoors.\" Answer: will stay.\n(ii) \"If I ___ you, I would apologise.\" Answer: were.\n(iii) \"If she had studied, she ___ passed.\" Answer: would have.\n(iv) \"No sooner had he arrived ___ it rained.\" Answer: than.\n(v) \"Hardly ___ she entered when the lights failed.\" Answer: had.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nUse the linked JAMB questions to practise choosing the correct conditional or inverted structure. For every answer, state which time and meaning the construction expresses."}},{"id":"reported","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Reported (indirect) speech","body":"When you report what someone said, the tense usually moves back one step if the reporting verb is in the past.\n\nThe back-shift (after said, told, asked):\n• present becomes past (\"I am tired\" → he said he was tired)\n• present perfect becomes past perfect (\"I have mislaid my watch\" → he said he HAD MISLAID his watch)\n• will becomes would; can becomes could\n\nNo back-shift when the reporting verb is present (says, thinks, expects):\n• \"My mother says she EXPECTS he WILL arrive this Friday.\"\n• \"He says he IS NOT ABLE to find me a job.\"\n\nCommon traps:\n• Keeping the present perfect after \"said\" (\"said that he has misplaced\").\n• Back-shifting after a present verb (\"says she expected he would\").\n• Ungrammatical forms such as \"had forgot\" or \"he have lost\".\n• Doubled modals (\"cannot be able to\"); use one.\n\nQuick check: \"Yesterday, Ola said that ___ his watch.\" He has misplaced, or he had mislaid? He had mislaid: \"said\" is past, so shift back."},"new":{"title":"Reported (indirect) speech","body":"REPORTED SPEECH — TURNING SOMEONE'S WORDS INTO AN ACCURATE REPORT\n\nINTRODUCTION\nDirect speech repeats someone's exact words: Ada said, \"I am tired.\" Reported (indirect) speech tells us what Ada said without necessarily quoting the exact words: Ada said that she was tired. To report accurately, check the reporting verb, speaker, listener, time, place, sentence type and whether the original statement is still true. JAMB tests changes in tense, pronouns, time expressions, questions, commands and modal verbs.\n\n1. DIRECT SPEECH AND INDIRECT SPEECH\nDirect speech commonly uses quotation marks. Indirect speech usually removes them and adjusts the sentence structure. \"He said, 'I need help'\" becomes \"He said that he needed help\" when reporting from a later past viewpoint. \"That\" may be omitted in many statements: \"He said he needed help.\" The meaning should remain the same; reporting is not permission to invent information.\n\nExample (i)\n\"Bola said, 'I am hungry.'\"\nA. Bola said that she is hungry yesterday. B. Bola said that she was hungry. C. Bola said that I was hungry. D. Bola said that she hungry.\nSolution: With past \"said\", \"am\" normally becomes \"was\"; \"I\" becomes \"she\" when the reporter is not Bola. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\n\"He said, 'We have finished the work.'\"\nSolution: \"He said that they had finished the work.\" \"We\" changes according to who is included in the original group, and present perfect commonly shifts to past perfect after past \"said\".\n\n2. REPORTING VERBS: SAY, TELL, ASK AND EXPLAIN\n\"Say\" can introduce words without naming a listener: \"She said that she was ready.\" \"Tell\" normally takes an object: \"She told me that she was ready\", not \"She told that she was ready\". \"Ask\" introduces questions or requests: \"He asked whether I was ready.\" \"Explain\" can take \"that\" or \"to someone\": \"She explained to us that the road was closed.\"\n\nExample (iii)\n\"The teacher ___ the pupils that the test would begin soon.\"\nA. said B. told C. spoke D. talked\nSolution: \"Told\" correctly takes the object \"the pupils\" and a that-clause. \"Said the pupils\" is not the intended pattern. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"He ___ that he had misplaced his phone.\"\nA. told B. said C. asked me whether D. ordered\nSolution: Without a listener object, \"said that\" is the appropriate reporting structure. Correct answer: B.\n\n3. TENSE BACKSHIFT AFTER A PAST REPORTING VERB\nWhen the reporting verb is past and the report is made from a later viewpoint, tense often moves one step back:\nPresent simple → past simple: \"I work\" → she said she worked.\nPresent continuous → past continuous: \"I am working\" → she said she was working.\nPresent perfect → past perfect: \"I have worked\" → she said she had worked.\nPast simple → past perfect: \"I worked\" → she said she had worked.\nWill → would; can → could; may → might.\nBackshift is a common pattern, not an absolute law: context, current truth and time of reporting matter.\n\nExample (v)\n\"Ada said, 'I have lost my key.'\"\nA. Ada said she has lost her key. B. Ada said she had lost her key. C. Ada said she have lost her key. D. Ada said she loses her key.\nSolution: The standard backshift after past \"said\" is present perfect → past perfect. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (vi)\n\"Emeka said, 'I will travel tomorrow.'\"\nSolution: \"Emeka said that he would travel the following day.\" \"Will\" becomes \"would\"; \"tomorrow\" changes when the reporting viewpoint moves to a later day.\n\n4. WHEN BACKSHIFT IS NOT NEEDED\nIf the reporting verb is present, the original tense commonly remains: \"She says, 'I am ready'\" → \"She says that she is ready.\" A general truth may also remain present after a past reporting verb: \"The teacher said that the earth revolves around the sun.\" When a reported statement is still true, present tense may be possible, depending on context. In exam questions, look for the clearest time relationship rather than mechanically shifting every verb.\n\nExample (vii)\n\"My mother says, 'I expect the guests tomorrow.'\"\nSolution: \"My mother says that she expects the guests tomorrow.\" Present reporting \"says\" does not automatically require past \"expected\".\n\nExample (viii)\n\"The teacher said, 'Water freezes at 0°C under standard conditions.'\"\nSolution: \"The teacher said that water freezes at 0°C under standard conditions.\" The scientific general truth may remain in the present.\n\n5. PRONOUNS AND POSSESSIVES: WHO IS SPEAKING?\nPronouns must match the original speaker and listener. \"I\" may become \"he\", \"she\" or \"they\"; \"my\" may become \"his\", \"her\" or \"their\"; \"you\" may become \"I\", \"we\", \"he\", \"she\" or \"they\", depending on the listener. Never change a pronoun without identifying its referent.\n\nExample (ix)\n\"Amaka said to Chidi, 'I will help you with your homework.'\"\nSolution: \"Amaka told Chidi that she would help him with his homework.\" \"I\" refers to Amaka; \"you\" refers to Chidi.\n\nExample (x)\n\"The boys said, 'Our coach has praised us.'\"\nSolution: \"The boys said that their coach had praised them.\" \"Our\" becomes \"their\"; \"us\" becomes \"them\"; \"has praised\" backshifts to \"had praised\".\n\n6. TIME AND PLACE WORDS\nWords such as now, today, yesterday, tomorrow, here and this depend on the time and place of speaking. When the reporting viewpoint changes, common adjustments include now → then, today → that day, yesterday → the previous day, tomorrow → the following day, here → there, this → that. These are not compulsory if the original time or place is still the reporter's present time or place.\n\nExample (xi)\n\"On Monday, he said, 'I will return tomorrow.'\"\nQuestion: Report this on Wednesday.\nSolution: \"On Monday, he said that he would return the following day\" (Tuesday). Keeping \"tomorrow\" on Wednesday would wrongly suggest Thursday.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"She said, 'I saw him here yesterday.'\"\nSolution: From another place and a later day: \"She said that she had seen him there the previous day.\" Both place and time are adjusted to the reporting viewpoint.\n\n7. YES/NO QUESTIONS — IF OR WHETHER\nA direct question such as \"Are you ready?\" becomes an indirect question with \"if\" or \"whether\": \"She asked whether I was ready.\" Indirect questions use statement word order, not question word order. Do not write \"She asked whether was I ready.\" Do not keep the question mark when the full reported sentence is a statement.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"The teacher asked, 'Have you finished?'\"\nA. The teacher asked had I finished. B. The teacher asked whether I had finished. C. The teacher asked whether had I finished. D. The teacher asked I finished?\nSolution: \"Whether\" introduces the yes/no question, and \"I had finished\" uses statement order. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"He asked me, 'Can you swim?'\"\nSolution: \"He asked me if I could swim.\" \"Can\" commonly becomes \"could\" after past \"asked\"; the subject precedes the verb.\n\n8. WH-QUESTIONS — KEEP THE QUESTION WORD, CHANGE THE ORDER\nQuestions beginning who, what, where, when, why or how usually keep that question word: \"Where do you live?\" → \"She asked where I lived.\" Remove auxiliary \"do/does/did\" when it merely marks the direct question: \"What did you buy?\" → \"He asked what I had bought.\" The reported clause follows statement order.\n\nExample (xv)\n\"She asked, 'Where does Tunde work?'\"\nA. She asked where did Tunde work. B. She asked where Tunde worked. C. She asked where does Tunde work. D. She asked where worked Tunde.\nSolution: Remove question-form \"does\" and use subject + verb: \"Tunde worked\". Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"The officer asked, 'Why are you late?'\"\nSolution: \"The officer asked why I was late.\" The indirect clause has no inversion.\n\n9. COMMANDS, REQUESTS, ADVICE AND WARNINGS\nImperatives are often reported with tell/ask/order/advise/warn + object + to-infinitive. \"Sit down!\" → \"He ordered us to sit down.\" \"Please wait\" → \"She asked me to wait.\" A negative command uses \"not to\": \"Don't touch it\" → \"He warned me not to touch it.\" Choose a reporting verb that reflects the speaker's tone; \"ordered\" is stronger than \"requested\".\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"The teacher said to the pupils, 'Do not make noise.'\"\nA. The teacher told the pupils not to make noise. B. The teacher told the pupils to not making noise. C. The teacher said the pupils not make noise. D. The teacher asked the pupils do not noise.\nSolution: A negative instruction becomes \"told + object + not to + base verb\". Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xviii)\n\"Mother said, 'Please close the window.'\"\nSolution: \"Mother asked me to close the window.\" \"Asked\" preserves the polite request; the reporter must identify whom Mother addressed.\n\n10. SUGGESTIONS, OFFERS AND EXCLAMATIONS\n\"Let's leave early\" may be reported as \"He suggested leaving early\" or \"He suggested that we leave early\". Do not write \"suggested us to leave\" in standard formal English. \"Shall I carry your bag?\" may become \"He offered to carry my bag\". Exclamations can be reported with \"exclaimed\", but the meaning should be retained: \"What a wonderful result!\" → \"She exclaimed that the result was wonderful.\"\n\nExample (xix)\n\"He said, 'Let's meet at noon.'\"\nA. He suggested meeting at noon. B. He suggested us to meet at noon. C. He told meeting at noon. D. He asked that meeting noon.\nSolution: \"Suggest + -ing\" is a correct reporting pattern. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xx)\n\"She said, 'What a beautiful painting!'\"\nSolution: \"She exclaimed that it was a beautiful painting.\" The exclamation becomes a statement reporting admiration.\n\n11. MODAL VERBS AND SPECIAL CASES\nWill often becomes would; can becomes could; may becomes might. \"Must\" may stay \"must\" when obligation still applies, or become \"had to\" when reporting an earlier obligation: \"I must leave now\" → \"He said that he had to leave then.\" \"Could\", \"would\", \"should\" and \"might\" often remain unchanged. Avoid doubled modals such as \"will can\" or \"cannot be able to\".\n\nExample (xxi)\n\"He said, 'I can solve the problem.'\"\nSolution: \"He said that he could solve the problem.\" The modal backshifts naturally.\n\nExample (xxii)\n\"She said, 'I must finish this work today.'\"\nSolution: Reporting later: \"She said that she had to finish that work that day.\" \"Had to\" reports the obligation from a past viewpoint.\n\n12. HOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Identify whether the original is a statement, question, command, request, suggestion or exclamation.\n2. Check whether the reporting verb is present or past.\n3. Identify the original speaker and listener before changing pronouns.\n4. Adjust tense, time and place only as the reporting context requires.\n5. For questions, use statement word order; for commands, check the to-infinitive pattern.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n\"Told that\" without a listener is often wrong; use \"said that\" or \"told someone that\". \"Asked where did he go\" keeps direct-question order; use \"asked where he had gone\". \"Suggested us to\" is nonstandard; use \"suggested that we...\" or \"suggested going\". Never assume \"yesterday\" must remain unchanged when the reporting date has moved.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) \"She said, 'I am tired.'\" → She said that she was tired.\n(ii) \"He asked, 'Are you ready?'\" → He asked whether I was ready.\n(iii) \"Mother said, 'Don't run.'\" → Mother told me not to run.\n(iv) \"She says, 'I know him.'\" → She says that she knows him.\n(v) \"He said, 'I will return tomorrow.'\" → He said he would return the following day (if reported later).\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nPractise the linked JAMB reported-speech questions. Check pronouns and the reporter's time and place before deciding whether an option is correct."}},{"id":"prepositions","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Prepositions, phrasal verbs and collocations","body":"Many questions test which small word goes with a verb, noun or adjective. These have to be learned, not reasoned out.\n\nFixed combinations JAMB likes:\n• at variance WITH, sure OF, in respect OF, commiserate WITH, condole WITH\n• zero IN ON (aim at), hemmed IN (restricted), give UP (quit)\n• resort TO / fall back ON (turn to a backup)\n• borrow money FOR a purpose, dance TO the music\n• on the throne, on end (for days on end)\n\nHow to answer:\n1. Say each option in the sentence. The idiomatic one usually sounds familiar.\n2. Remember that a phrasal verb's meaning is not the sum of its parts: \"give up\" is not about giving.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A verb without its preposition (\"condoled the family\"; it needs \"with\").\n• A near-miss preposition (\"in variance to\", \"sure on\").\n\nQuick check: \"Our soldiers are starting to zero ___ the enemy.\" At, on at, unto or in on? In on."},"new":{"title":"Prepositions, phrasal verbs and collocations","body":"PREPOSITIONS, PHRASAL VERBS AND COLLOCATIONS — HOW WORDS WORK TOGETHER\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA preposition is a word such as in, on, at, by, for, from, of, to, with or through that shows relationships of place, time, direction, cause and other meanings. A phrasal verb combines a verb with one or more particles and may have a meaning different from the separate words: \"give up\" means stop trying. A collocation is a natural partnership between words, such as \"make a decision\" or \"heavy rain\". JAMB often offers options that sound similar but are not idiomatic in the sentence.\n\n1. PREPOSITIONS OF PLACE: IN, ON AND AT\n\"In\" often indicates enclosure or a larger area: \"in the classroom\", \"in Lagos\". \"On\" often indicates a surface: \"on the table\", \"on the wall\". \"At\" often indicates a point or specific location: \"at the gate\", \"at the bus stop\". These are general patterns, not universal rules: we say \"on a bus\" and \"in a car\" in ordinary English.\n\nExample (i)\n\"The notice was displayed ___ the wall.\"\nA. in B. on C. at D. to\nSolution: The notice is attached to a surface, so \"on the wall\" fits. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\n\"The pupils waited ___ the school gate.\"\nA. at B. in C. onto D. of\nSolution: \"At\" identifies the gate as a specific meeting point. Correct answer: A.\n\n2. PREPOSITIONS OF TIME: AT, ON, IN, BY, UNTIL, SINCE AND FOR\n\"At\" is common for clock times: \"at 7 a.m.\" \"On\" is common for days and dates: \"on Monday\", \"on 13 November\". \"In\" is common for months, years and longer periods: \"in April\", \"in 2026\". \"By Friday\" means no later than Friday; \"until Friday\" means continuing up to Friday. \"Since\" gives a starting point; \"for\" gives a duration.\n\nExample (iii)\n\"She has lived here ___ 2020.\"\nA. for B. since C. at D. during\nSolution: \"2020\" is the starting point of a period continuing to now. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (iv)\n\"The assignment must be submitted ___ Friday.\"\nA. by B. since C. during D. from\nSolution: \"By Friday\" gives a deadline. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (v)\n\"The meeting begins ___ noon.\"\nA. on B. in C. at D. for\nSolution: \"At\" introduces a specific clock time. Correct answer: C.\n\n3. MOVEMENT AND DIRECTION: TO, INTO, ONTO, THROUGH, ACROSS AND ALONG\n\"To\" indicates a destination; \"into\" indicates movement from outside to inside; \"onto\" indicates movement onto a surface. \"Through\" suggests movement within or from one end of an enclosed space to another; \"across\" suggests crossing an area or surface; \"along\" suggests following the length of a route. Contrast \"The boy jumped into the pool\" (entered the water) with \"The boy jumped over the pool\" (passed above it).\n\nExample (vi)\n\"The thief ran ___ the tunnel and escaped at the other end.\"\nA. through B. across C. onto D. above\nSolution: \"Through\" describes movement inside a passage from one end towards the other. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (vii)\n\"The children walked ___ the narrow bridge.\"\nA. across B. into C. among D. below\nSolution: \"Across\" describes movement from one side of the bridge to the other. Correct answer: A.\n\n4. ADJECTIVE + PREPOSITION COMBINATIONS\nSome adjectives require particular prepositions: afraid of, aware of, capable of, interested in, proud of, responsible for, similar to, different from, dependent on, accustomed to, satisfied with. Learn each as a complete phrase and sentence. \"She is interested in science\" differs from \"She is interesting\": the former describes her interest, while the latter describes how others perceive her.\n\nExample (viii)\n\"The manager is responsible ___ the accounts.\"\nA. of B. to C. for D. at\nSolution: \"Responsible for\" identifies the duty or area of responsibility. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (ix)\n\"The twins are similar ___ appearance.\"\nA. to B. in C. on D. at\nSolution: The idiomatic phrase is \"similar in appearance\" when naming the respect in which they resemble one another. \"Similar to\" would introduce the person or thing compared. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (x)\n\"She is accustomed ___ waking early.\"\nA. for B. to C. with D. at\nSolution: \"Accustomed to\" takes a noun or -ing form. Correct answer: B.\n\n5. VERB + PREPOSITION COMBINATIONS\nCommon combinations include depend on, insist on, participate in, object to, apologise for, congratulate someone on, accuse someone of, comply with, resort to, listen to and belong to. Different prepositions can change the meaning: \"agree with a person\", \"agree to a proposal\", \"agree on a date\".\n\nExample (xi)\n\"The official was accused ___ misconduct.\"\nA. for B. of C. on D. to\nSolution: The construction is \"accuse someone of something\". Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xii)\n\"The workers insisted ___ better conditions.\"\nA. on B. at C. of D. to\nSolution: \"Insist on\" introduces what is demanded. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xiii)\n\"The two parties agreed ___ a date for the hearing.\"\nA. to B. with C. on D. for\nSolution: \"Agree on\" means reach a shared decision about a matter. Correct answer: C.\n\n6. NOUN + PREPOSITION COMBINATIONS\nNouns also have usual partners: access to, demand for, reason for, solution to, increase in, interest in, preference for, objection to, advantage of/over, respect for. A common trap is choosing a preposition that follows a related verb but not the noun in the sentence: \"solve the problem\" but \"a solution to the problem\".\n\nExample (xiv)\n\"The engineers found a solution ___ the problem.\"\nA. for B. of C. to D. by\nSolution: The fixed noun phrase is \"solution to\". Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xv)\n\"There has been an increase ___ the price of fuel.\"\nA. of B. in C. at D. to\nSolution: \"An increase in\" names what has risen. \"An increase of ten percent\" would name the amount. Correct answer: B.\n\n7. PHRASAL VERBS — ONE VERB, DIFFERENT PARTICLES\nA phrasal verb may be literal or figurative. \"Look after\" = care for; \"look into\" = investigate; \"look up\" = search for information; \"look down on\" = regard as inferior. \"Take off\" can mean remove clothing or leave the ground in an aircraft; \"take after\" means resemble a family member. The sentence decides the meaning.\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"The police promised to look ___ the complaint.\"\nA. after B. into C. down D. over\nSolution: The police are expected to investigate the complaint. \"Look into\" fits. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"The plane took ___ at dawn.\"\nA. after B. down C. off D. over\nSolution: \"Take off\" means leave the ground and begin flying. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xviii)\n\"She takes ___ her mother in appearance.\"\nA. after B. into C. up D. out\nSolution: \"Take after\" means resemble a relative. Correct answer: A.\n\n8. SEPARABLE AND INSEPARABLE PHRASAL VERBS\nSome phrasal verbs allow an object between the verb and particle: \"Turn off the light\" / \"Turn the light off\". When the object is a pronoun, it normally goes between: \"Turn it off\", not \"Turn off it\". Other phrasal verbs are inseparable: \"Look after the baby\" / \"Look after her\", not \"Look her after\". Multiword verbs may also have two particles: \"Put up with\" means tolerate.\n\nExample (xix)\n\"Please turn ___ before you leave.\"\nA. the lights off B. off them C. them off D. up with them\nSolution: If the missing words refer to previously mentioned lights using a pronoun, \"turn them off\" is the standard separable pattern. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xx)\n\"The nurse will look ___ the patient.\"\nA. after B. up C. out D. over\nSolution: \"Look after\" means care for. Correct answer: A.\n\n9. COLLOCATIONS — NATURAL WORD PARTNERS\nWe say \"make a mistake\", \"do homework\", \"take a risk\", \"pay attention\", \"draw a conclusion\", \"lodge a complaint\", \"keep a promise\", \"meet a deadline\" and \"deliver a speech\". For adjectives, common pairs include heavy rain, strong evidence, deep concern and bitter disappointment. The meaning may be understandable with a different word, but JAMB tests conventional usage.\n\nExample (xxi)\n\"The witness was asked to ___ an account of the incident.\"\nA. do B. give C. perform D. construct\nSolution: \"Give an account\" is the standard collocation. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxii)\n\"The company must ___ the deadline.\"\nA. meet B. catch C. win D. hold\nSolution: \"Meet a deadline\" means finish by the required time. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xxiii)\n\"The manager decided to ___ a complaint.\"\nA. lodge B. build C. manufacture D. draw\nSolution: A complaint is formally \"lodged\". Correct answer: A.\n\n10. MEANING CHANGES WITH A SMALL WORD\n\"Fall back on savings\" means rely on savings when another resource is unavailable. \"Resort to\" means turn to an option, often after other options fail. \"Zero in on\" means focus attention closely on a target. \"At variance with\" means in disagreement with. \"Commiserate with\" and \"condole with\" express sympathy; their appropriate use depends on context. A wrong preposition may completely change or spoil the expression.\n\nExample (xxiv)\n\"When the business slowed down, the family fell back ___ its savings.\"\nA. in B. on C. to D. at\nSolution: \"Fall back on\" means rely on a reserve or alternative. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxv)\n\"The findings are at variance ___ the earlier report.\"\nA. to B. with C. from D. on\nSolution: \"At variance with\" means inconsistent with. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxvi)\n\"The investigators began to zero ___ the main suspect.\"\nA. in on B. at C. into D. unto\nSolution: \"Zero in on\" means focus closely on. Correct answer: A.\n\n11. PREPOSITION VERSUS CONJUNCTION\n\"Despite\" is a preposition and normally takes a noun phrase or -ing form: \"Despite the rain, we went out.\" \"Although\" is a conjunction and introduces a clause: \"Although it rained, we went out.\" \"Because of\" takes a noun phrase; \"because\" introduces a clause. A blank may require the correct word not just by meaning but by the structure following it.\n\nExample (xxvii)\n\"___ the heavy rain, the match continued.\"\nA. Although B. Despite C. Because D. Unless\nSolution: \"The heavy rain\" is a noun phrase; \"despite\" fits. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxviii)\n\"___ it rained heavily, the match continued.\"\nA. Despite B. In spite of C. Although D. Because of\nSolution: \"It rained heavily\" is a clause; \"although\" introduces a contrast clause. Correct answer: C.\n\n12. HOW TO ANSWER — QUICK EXAM GUIDE\n1. Read the entire sentence, not just the blank.\n2. Decide whether the missing word shows time, place, direction, cause, relationship or a fixed partnership.\n3. If a phrasal verb is involved, identify its meaning in that sentence before choosing the particle.\n4. Check the grammar of what follows: a noun phrase, a full clause, a pronoun or an -ing form.\n5. Read the completed sentence aloud in your mind and check whether the combination is conventional and meaningful.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n\"Different to\" may occur in some varieties, but \"different from\" is the safest standard examination choice. \"Interested on\" is wrong for \"interested in\". \"Look into\" (investigate) is not \"look after\" (care for). \"Despite he was ill\" is wrong; use \"although he was ill\" or \"despite his illness\". \"Turn off it\" is wrong; use \"turn it off\". Do not guess a particle only because it is familiar.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) \"Responsible ___ the funds.\" Answer: for.\n(ii) \"She has lived here ___ 2020.\" Answer: since.\n(iii) \"Look ___ the complaint\" meaning investigate. Answer: into.\n(iv) \"Despite ___ ill, she attended.\" Answer: being.\n(v) \"A solution ___ the problem.\" Answer: to.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nUse the mapped JAMB questions to practise identifying the fixed partnership and explaining why the other prepositions or particles do not fit."}},{"id":"pronouns","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Pronouns and question tags","body":"Pronouns: use the subject form for the doer and the object form after verbs and prepositions.\n• Subject: I, he, she, we, they. \"Amadi and I went to the stadium.\"\n• Object: me, him, her, us, them. \"They invited Amadi and me.\"\n• Trick: remove the other person. \"I went\", not \"Me went\".\n• \"Myself\" is only for emphasis or a reflexive (\"I did it myself\"), never as a plain subject.\n\nNegative questions use anyone/anybody: \"Didn't ANYONE draw your attention…?\" (\"Didn't nobody\" is a double negative.)\n\nQuestion tags:\n1. A positive statement takes a negative tag: \"He knows, doesn't he?\"\n2. A negative statement takes a positive tag: \"He does not seem to know, DOES HE?\"\n3. The tag uses the same auxiliary as the statement (does/did/is/has).\n\nCommon traps: \"me and Amadi\" as a subject; a negative tag after a negative statement.\n\nQuick check: \"He does not seem to know how to solve it, ___?\" Does he, or doesn't he? Does he."},"new":{"title":"Pronouns and question tags","body":"PRONOUNS AND QUESTION TAGS — CLEAR REFERENCE AND CORRECT AGREEMENT\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA pronoun replaces or refers to a noun or noun phrase so that we do not repeat names unnecessarily. In \"Ngozi misplaced her pen, but she found it\", \"she\" refers to Ngozi and \"it\" refers to the pen. JAMB tests the form of a pronoun, its reference, agreement with the noun, and the auxiliary and polarity of a question tag. Learn the reason behind each choice, not just a list of pronouns.\n\n1. SUBJECT AND OBJECT PRONOUNS\nSubject forms are I, you, he, she, it, we and they; object forms include me, you, him, her, it, us and them. A subject normally performs the verb's action: \"She called.\" An object receives the action or follows a preposition: \"The teacher called her\"; \"The gift is for her.\" A useful test with coordinated pronouns is to remove the other person: \"Amaka and I went\" becomes \"I went\"; \"They invited Amaka and me\" becomes \"They invited me\". Do not automatically choose \"I\" because it sounds formal.\n\nExample (i)\n___ went to the library with Amaka.\nA. Me B. I C. Myself D. Mine\nSolution: The pronoun is the subject of 'went'; use 'I', not the object form 'me'. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\nThe teacher congratulated Musa and ___.\nA. I B. myself C. me D. mine\nSolution: The verb 'congratulated' takes an object. Remove 'Musa and': 'congratulated me'. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (iii)\nBetween you and ___, the plan needs revision.\nA. I B. me C. myself D. mine\nSolution: 'Between' is a preposition and takes the object form 'me'. Correct answer: B.\n\n2. POSSESSIVE ADJECTIVES AND POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS\nMy, your, his, her, its, our and their come before a noun: \"their books\". Mine, yours, his, hers, ours and theirs stand independently: \"The books are theirs.\" Never add an apostrophe to possessive \"its\" or \"yours\". \"It's\" means \"it is\" or \"it has\", whereas \"its\" expresses possession. \"Whose\" asks about possession; \"who's\" means \"who is\" or \"who has\".\n\nExample (iv)\nThe red bicycle is ___.\nA. her B. hers C. she's D. she\nSolution: The blank follows 'is' without a noun, so the independent possessive 'hers' is needed. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (v)\nThe dog wagged ___ tail.\nA. it's B. its C. it D. its'\nSolution: 'Its' is possessive. 'It's' would mean 'it is', which does not fit before 'tail'. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (vi)\n___ bag is lying outside?\nA. Who's B. Whose C. Who D. Whom\nSolution: 'Whose' asks about ownership. 'Who's' means 'who is' or 'who has'. Correct answer: B.\n\n3. REFLEXIVE AND EMPHATIC PRONOUNS\nReflexive pronouns include myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, yourselves and themselves. Use them when the object refers back to the subject: \"Ada blamed herself.\" The same forms can add emphasis: \"The principal herself presented the award.\" Do not use \"myself\" as a substitute for ordinary \"I\" or \"me\": \"Myself and John attended\" is not the expected formal construction.\n\nExample (vii)\nThe boy injured ___ while playing.\nA. him B. himself C. his D. he\nSolution: The subject and object refer to the same boy, so the reflexive 'himself' is required. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (viii)\nThe headmistress ___ welcomed the visitors.\nA. herself B. her C. she D. hers\nSolution: 'Herself' adds emphasis: the headmistress personally welcomed them. Correct answer: A.\n\n4. DEMONSTRATIVE AND INDEFINITE PRONOUNS\nThis and these usually refer to things nearer in place or discourse; that and those refer to things farther away or previously mentioned. \"This book\" is singular; \"these books\" is plural. Indefinite pronouns include someone, anyone, everyone, nobody, each, several and both. In standard concord, \"everyone\", \"each\" and \"nobody\" normally take singular verbs: \"Everyone is ready\"; \"Several are ready\". \"Anyone\" is natural in many questions and negatives.\n\nExample (ix)\n___ of the candidates has received a number.\nA. Each B. Several C. Both D. Many\nSolution: 'Each' is singular and matches 'has'. The others are plural. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (x)\nI cannot find ___ of my keys.\nA. someone B. any C. every D. each\nSolution: 'Any' is natural in a negative sentence referring to an unspecified quantity. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xi)\n___ books on that distant shelf belong to us.\nA. This B. That C. Those D. It\nSolution: 'Books' is plural and the shelf is distant, so 'those' fits. Correct answer: C.\n\n5. RELATIVE PRONOUNS AND THEIR ANTECEDENTS\nWho commonly refers to people as subject; whom is an object form in formal English; whose expresses possession; which commonly refers to things; that can introduce restrictive clauses about people or things. \"The pupil who won\" identifies the pupil. \"The pupil whose bag was stolen\" links the bag to its owner. In a nonrestrictive clause set off by commas, \"which\" or \"who\" is normally used, not \"that\": \"My brother, who lives in Kano, is visiting.\"\n\nExample (xii)\nThe woman ___ won the prize is my aunt.\nA. which B. who C. whose D. whom\nSolution: The relative pronoun is the subject of 'won' and refers to a person. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xiii)\nThe pupil ___ father is a pilot came first.\nA. who B. whom C. whose D. which\nSolution: 'Whose' expresses the relationship between the pupil and the father. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xiv)\nThe bicycle, ___ was repaired yesterday, is working.\nA. that B. who C. which D. whom\nSolution: The comma-marked nonrestrictive clause refers to a thing; 'which' is appropriate. Correct answer: C.\n\n6. PRONOUN REFERENCE AND AGREEMENT\nA pronoun should point clearly to its antecedent. \"When Mary met Jane, she was delighted\" is ambiguous: which woman was delighted? Rewrite to name the intended person. Number also matters: \"The students brought their books\"; \"The school changed its rules\". Singular \"they\" is widely accepted for an unspecified or gender-neutral individual: \"Every candidate should check their details.\" In examination items, prefer the answer that is both clear and grammatical in the given context.\n\nExample (xv)\nThe players collected ___ medals.\nA. its B. his C. their D. her\nSolution: The plural antecedent 'players' requires plural possessive 'their'. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xvi)\nThe company announced ___ new policy.\nA. their B. its C. his D. them\nSolution: The company is treated as one institution; 'its' agrees with that singular sense. Correct answer: B.\n\n7. THE BASIC QUESTION-TAG RULE\nA question tag is a short question added to a statement to request confirmation: \"She is here, isn't she?\" A positive statement usually takes a negative tag; a negative statement usually takes a positive tag. Match the auxiliary and the subject: \"They have arrived, haven't they?\" \"She can swim, can't she?\" If there is no auxiliary, use do/does/did: \"He works here, doesn't he?\" A tag does not merely repeat the last verb.\n\nExample (xvii)\nHe speaks French, ___?\nA. isn't he B. doesn't he C. didn't he D. does he\nSolution: The positive present-simple statement has no auxiliary, so use negative 'doesn't he'. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xviii)\nThey haven't finished, ___?\nA. haven't they B. do they C. have they D. did they\nSolution: The main clause is negative present perfect; the positive tag repeats 'have'. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xix)\nShe can drive, ___?\nA. can she B. doesn't she C. can't she D. isn't she\nSolution: A positive clause with modal 'can' takes the negative tag 'can't she'. Correct answer: C.\n\n8. SPECIAL TAGS: I AM, LET'S, IMPERATIVES AND THERE\nSome tags do not follow the simplest pattern. \"I am late, aren't I?\" is standard; \"amn't I\" is not standard in most exam contexts. \"Let's go, shall we?\" invites agreement. A command may take \"will you?\" or \"would you?\" depending on tone: \"Close the door, will you?\" \"There is a problem, isn't there?\" repeats \"there\" in the tag. \"Nothing happened, did it?\" uses a positive tag because \"nothing\" is negative in meaning.\n\nExample (xx)\nI am your classmate, ___?\nA. amn't I B. aren't I C. isn't I D. don't I\nSolution: The conventional tag for positive 'I am' is 'aren't I?'. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxi)\nLet's begin the lesson, ___?\nA. will you B. shall we C. do we D. aren't we\nSolution: 'Let's' proposing joint action commonly takes 'shall we?'. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxii)\nThere is a problem, ___?\nA. isn't there B. isn't it C. doesn't there D. is there\nSolution: An existential 'there is' clause uses 'there' again in the tag. Correct answer: A.\n\n9. NEGATIVE WORDS AND TAG POLARITY\nWords such as never, hardly, scarcely, seldom, rarely, nobody, nothing and few may make a clause negative in meaning even without \"not\". Such clauses usually take a positive tag: \"She seldom complains, does she?\" \"Nobody came, did they?\" \"A few\" differs from \"few\": \"A few people came\" is positive in meaning; \"Few people came\" suggests almost none and takes a positive tag.\n\nExample (xxiii)\nShe hardly ever travels, ___?\nA. doesn't she B. does she C. isn't she D. did she\nSolution: 'Hardly ever' makes the present-simple statement negative in meaning, so use a positive tag. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxiv)\nNobody complained, ___?\nA. didn't they B. did they C. doesn't he D. was it\nSolution: 'Nobody' is negative in meaning; the past-simple tag is positive 'did they'. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxv)\nHe does not seem to know, ___?\nA. doesn't he B. does he C. is he D. did he\nSolution: The statement is negative with auxiliary 'does'; the tag is positive 'does he'. Correct answer: B.\n\n10. HOW TO ANSWER AND COMMON EXAM TRAPS\nFor pronouns, first identify the noun being replaced and the grammatical job of the blank. Remove other coordinated nouns to test I/me. For tags, find the auxiliary, identify whether the whole statement is positive or negative, and repeat the correct subject as a pronoun. Beware \"myself\" used as a subject, \"it's\" mistaken for \"its\", and tags after \"never\" or \"hardly\".\n\nExample (xxvi)\nChoose the correct sentence.\nA. Myself and Kemi went. B. Kemi and me went. C. Kemi and I went. D. Me and Kemi went.\nSolution: Removing 'Kemi and' leaves 'I went', the correct subject form. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xxvii)\nThe teachers were absent, ___?\nA. were they B. weren't they C. didn't they D. aren't they\nSolution: Positive past 'were' takes the negative past tag 'weren't they'. Correct answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) \"John and ___ attended.\" Answer: I.\n(ii) \"The teacher spoke to Ada and ___.\" Answer: me.\n(iii) \"She is ready, ___?\" Answer: isn't she?\n(iv) \"Nobody arrived, ___?\" Answer: did they?\n(v) \"Let's leave, ___?\" Answer: shall we.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nUse the mapped JAMB questions below. For pronouns, identify the antecedent and grammatical function. For tags, identify the auxiliary, polarity and matching subject."}},{"id":"modifiers","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Modifiers, adjective order and comparison","body":"A modifier must sit next to the word it describes.\n\nDangling and misplaced modifiers:\n• \"Running fast, the bus was caught by Mary\" is wrong: the bus isn't running. Put the doer right after the comma: \"Running fast, Mary caught the bus.\"\n• Put almost, only and just next to the word they limit: \"drove them to school almost every day\", not \"almost drove\".\n\nAdjective order: opinion, then size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, and finally purpose or type. The word that says what kind of thing the noun is goes right next to it: \"a new major social trend\".\n\nComparison:\n• \"no less appealing than\", \"as appealing as\" (\"as much appealing as\" is wrong; \"as much\" goes with nouns)\n• \"hardly any\" = almost none (\"no any\" is wrong)\n• \"the rest of the thieves\" (keep \"the… of the\")\n\nQuick check: \"The weather is ___ appealing for Jos residents ___ for foreigners.\" No less / than, or as much / as? No less / than."},"new":{"title":"Modifiers, adjective order and comparison","body":"MODIFIERS, ADJECTIVE ORDER AND COMPARISON — MAKING SENTENCES PRECISE\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA modifier adds information about another word or group of words. Adjectives modify nouns: \"a careful driver\". Adverbs often modify verbs, adjectives or other adverbs: \"She drove carefully\"; \"very careful\". Modifiers help us describe precisely, but their position matters. A misplaced modifier can accidentally describe the wrong person or action. JAMB also tests the natural order of multiple adjectives and accurate comparisons.\n\n1. ADJECTIVES, ADVERBS AND WHAT THEY MODIFY\nAn adjective describes a noun or pronoun: \"The examination was difficult.\" An adverb may describe how, when, where or to what degree something happens: \"She answered confidently\"; \"They arrived yesterday\"; \"The answer is extremely clear.\" Do not confuse adjective and adverb forms. After linking verbs such as seem, appear, become and feel, an adjective often describes the subject: \"The soup tastes delicious\", not \"tastes deliciously\" when describing the soup.\n\nExample (i)\nThe candidate spoke ___.\nA. confident B. confidently C. confidence D. confide\nSolution: 'Spoke' is an action verb; the adverb 'confidently' tells us how. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\nThe flowers smell ___.\nA. sweet B. sweetly C. sweetness D. sweeterly\nSolution: 'Smell' is a linking verb here, and 'sweet' describes the flowers. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (iii)\nThe instructions were ___ clear.\nA. extreme B. extremely C. extremity D. extremes\nSolution: The adverb 'extremely' modifies the adjective 'clear'. Correct answer: B.\n\n2. MISPLACED MODIFIERS — PUT DESCRIPTIONS NEAR THEIR TARGETS\nA modifier should sit close to what it describes. \"She served rice to the children on paper plates\" might suggest the children stood on plates. Better: \"She served the children rice on paper plates.\" Another example: \"He saw a dog walking to school\" may suggest the dog was walking to school; rewrite \"While walking to school, he saw a dog.\" In exam questions, look for a sentence whose meaning changes because the description is attached to the wrong noun.\n\nExample (iv)\nChoose the clearest sentence.\nA. Walking to school, a snake was seen by Mary. B. Walking to school, Mary saw a snake. C. A snake saw Mary walking to school. D. Walking, a snake to school was seen.\nSolution: The opening phrase describes Mary, so Mary must be the subject immediately after it. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (v)\nChoose the clearest sentence.\nA. The teacher gave books to the pupils in brown covers. B. The teacher gave the pupils books in brown covers. C. In brown covers the teacher gave pupils. D. Books gave the pupils in brown covers.\nSolution: The brown covers belong to the books, not the pupils; place the phrase beside 'books'. Correct answer: B.\n\n3. DANGLING MODIFIERS — WHEN THE DOER IS MISSING\nA dangling modifier names an action or condition without attaching it to the correct doer. \"Having finished the homework, the television was switched on\" suggests the television finished the homework. Correct: \"Having finished the homework, Musa switched on the television.\" To repair such sentences, ask \"Who performed the opening action?\" and make that person or thing the subject of the main clause.\n\nExample (vi)\nChoose the correct sentence.\nA. Having completed the test, the bell rang. B. Having completed the test, Ada submitted her paper. C. Completed the test, Ada's paper was submitted. D. The bell having completed the test rang.\nSolution: Ada is the person who completed the test and the subject of the main clause. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (vii)\nChoose the clearest sentence.\nA. While crossing the road, the bag fell. B. While crossing the road, I dropped my bag. C. Crossing the road, the bag was heavy. D. The bag crossed the road and fell.\nSolution: 'I' is the person crossing; the opening modifier now has a clear subject. Correct answer: B.\n\n4. LIMITING MODIFIERS: ONLY, JUST, EVEN, ALMOST AND NEARLY\nThese small words can alter the meaning dramatically. \"Only Tunde borrowed the book\" means nobody else borrowed it. \"Tunde borrowed only the book\" means he borrowed no other item. \"Tunde almost won\" means he did not quite win; \"Tunde won almost every race\" means he won many but not all. Position \"only\", \"almost\" and \"even\" beside the element they limit.\n\nExample (viii)\nWhich sentence means Tunde borrowed no item except the book?\nA. Only Tunde borrowed the book. B. Tunde only borrowed the book. C. Tunde borrowed only the book. D. Tunde almost borrowed the book.\nSolution: 'Only' directly limits 'the book', so other borrowed items are excluded. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (ix)\n'She almost passed the examination' means\nA. she passed easily B. she came close but did not pass C. she passed every paper D. she did not sit the exam\nSolution: 'Almost passed' means the passing point was nearly reached but not achieved. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (x)\nWhich sentence means nobody except Ada won?\nA. Ada only won a medal. B. Only Ada won. C. Ada won only a medal. D. Ada almost won.\nSolution: 'Only' limits the person Ada rather than the prize or action. Correct answer: B.\n\n5. ADJECTIVE ORDER — HOW ENGLISH NATURALLY STACKS DESCRIPTIONS\nWhen several adjectives appear before one noun, English commonly follows an order such as opinion → size → age → shape → colour → origin → material → purpose/type → noun. For example: \"a beautiful small old round brown Italian wooden table\". Real sentences rarely need so many adjectives; excessive stacking is awkward. The pattern is a guide, not an absolute mathematical rule. A noun used as a classifier usually stays close to the head noun: \"a new school bus\", \"a large metal water tank\".\n\nExample (xi)\nChoose the natural order.\nA. a red beautiful dress B. a beautiful red dress C. a dress red beautiful D. a beautiful dress red\nSolution: Opinion ('beautiful') normally precedes colour ('red'). Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xii)\nChoose the natural phrase.\nA. a wooden old small box B. a small old wooden box C. an old wooden small box D. a box wooden old small\nSolution: Size, age and material appear in their usual relative order. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xiii)\nChoose the natural phrase.\nA. a Nigerian new sports car B. a sports new Nigerian car C. a new Nigerian sports car D. a car sports Nigerian new\nSolution: Age/newness precedes origin, and the type 'sports' stays near 'car'. Correct answer: C.\n\n6. COMPARATIVE AND SUPERLATIVE FORMS\nComparatives compare two people or things: tall/taller, useful/more useful, good/better, bad/worse. Superlatives identify an extreme within a group: tallest, most useful, best, worst. Do not combine \"more\" with an -er comparative (\"more taller\") or \"most\" with an -est superlative (\"most tallest\"). Use \"than\" after a comparative and \"the\" before many superlatives: \"Ada is taller than Bola\"; \"Ada is the tallest in the class\".\n\nExample (xiv)\nThis route is ___ than the old one.\nA. more shorter B. shortest C. shorter D. most short\nSolution: 'Shorter' is the correct comparative; 'more shorter' doubles the marking. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xv)\nOf the three solutions, this is the ___.\nA. better B. best C. more best D. gooder\nSolution: A superlative is appropriate for identifying the best among three. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xvi)\nHer second essay is ___ than her first.\nA. good B. best C. better D. more better\nSolution: 'Better' is the irregular comparative of 'good'. Correct answer: C.\n\n7. AS ... AS, NOT AS ... AS AND NO LESS ... THAN\nFor equality, use \"as + adjective/adverb + as\": \"The road is as wide as the bridge.\" For inequality, \"not as wide as\" means narrower. \"No less appealing than\" means at least as appealing as; it does not mean less appealing. \"As much\" commonly modifies uncountable nouns (\"as much water as\") or verb phrases (\"as much as she can\"), not a plain adjective: say \"as appealing as\", not \"as much appealing as\".\n\nExample (xvii)\nThe weather is ___ pleasant for residents ___ for visitors.\nA. as much / as B. as / as C. more / as D. most / than\nSolution: The equality pattern is 'as + adjective + as'. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xviii)\nThe new design is no less attractive ___ the old one.\nA. as B. to C. than D. from\nSolution: The fixed comparison is 'no less ... than'. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xix)\nThis box is not as heavy as that one. This means\nA. this box is heavier B. the boxes weigh the same C. this box is lighter D. neither box has weight\nSolution: 'Not as heavy as' indicates the first box is lighter. Correct answer: C.\n\n8. FEWER, LESS, MANY, MUCH AND DEGREE\nUse \"fewer\" with countable plural nouns (\"fewer chairs\") and \"less\" with uncountable quantities (\"less water\"). Use \"many\" with countable nouns and \"much\" with uncountable nouns. In everyday English, \"less\" also occurs with measurements and amounts, but exam questions commonly test the countable/uncountable distinction. \"Very\" intensifies adjectives; \"too\" often suggests an excessive degree: \"too hot to touch\".\n\nExample (xx)\nThere are ___ pupils today than yesterday.\nA. less B. fewer C. little D. much\nSolution: Pupils can be counted, so 'fewer' is the standard comparative. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxi)\nWe need ___ sugar in this recipe.\nA. fewer B. many C. less D. several\nSolution: Sugar is uncountable in this use, so 'less' fits. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xxii)\nThe soup is ___ hot to drink.\nA. too B. very much C. many D. fewer\nSolution: 'Too ... to' expresses an excessive degree preventing the action. Correct answer: A.\n\n9. PARALLEL COMPARISONS AND LOGICAL COMPLETENESS\nCompare like with like. \"The population of Kano is larger than that of the village\" compares two populations. \"The population of Kano is larger than the village\" incorrectly compares a population with a place. Also distinguish \"She likes reading more than writing\" (two activities) from \"She likes reading more than her sister\" (ambiguous: more than her sister likes reading, or more than she likes her sister).\n\nExample (xxiii)\nChoose the logically balanced comparison.\nA. The salary of Ada is higher than Bisi. B. Ada's salary is higher than Bisi's. C. Ada's salary is higher than Bisi. D. Salary Ada is higher than Bisi.\nSolution: The comparison must be between two salaries, not a salary and a person. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxiv)\nChoose the correct sentence.\nA. This book is more useful than that one. B. This book is more useful as that one. C. This book is most useful than that one. D. This book is useful than that one.\nSolution: A comparative with 'more' takes 'than'. Correct answer: A.\n\n10. HOW TO ANSWER AND COMMON EXAM TRAPS\nIdentify the word being described before choosing a modifier. For a participial phrase at the beginning, make sure the subject immediately after the comma is the doer. For adjective order, start with the noun and place classification/material nearest to it. For comparisons, count how many things are compared and check whether they are of the same kind. Avoid \"more better\", \"as much beautiful as\", and an \"only\" placed where it changes the intended meaning.\n\nExample (xxv)\nChoose the correct sentence.\nA. Running fast, the bus was caught by Mary. B. Running fast, Mary caught the bus. C. The bus running fast was caught by Mary running. D. Running fast, the bus caught Mary.\nSolution: Mary is the runner; the participial phrase correctly modifies her. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxvi)\nThe answer was ___ clear for anyone to misunderstand.\nA. too B. enough C. so D. fewer\nSolution: 'Too clear ... to misunderstand' expresses an excessive degree of clarity for misunderstanding, although 'so clear that no one could misunderstand' would be more natural in polished prose. Correct answer: A.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) \"She answered ___.\" Answer: carefully.\n(ii) \"Walking home, ___ saw a bird.\" Answer: I / a person who was walking.\n(iii) \"A ___ dress\" with beautiful and blue. Answer: beautiful blue dress.\n(iv) \"This road is ___ than that one.\" Answer: wider.\n(v) \"There are ___ chairs today.\" Answer: fewer (when comparing countable chairs).\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nUse the linked JAMB questions to identify the modifier, the noun it describes and the comparison pattern being tested."}},{"id":"spelling","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Spelling, plurals and punctuation","body":"Spelling:\n• accommodate (double c, double m), occasion, necessary, embarrass\n• Greek and Latin plurals: criterion → criteria, phenomenon → phenomena, datum → data\n\nApostrophes:\n• one owner: dog's bone\n• plural owners ending in s: dogs' bones, teachers' training\n• no apostrophe for a simple plural: teachers\n\nSemicolons:\n• join two complete sentences: \"She passed; she was not happy.\"\n• before \"however\" or \"therefore\", with a comma after: \"She passed the exam; however, she was not happy.\"\n• never put a semicolon before \"and\", \"but\" or \"because\"\n\nCommon traps: \"criterias\" (already plural), \"dogs bone\" (missing apostrophe), \"; but\".\n\nQuick check: Which is right: \"I love music; because it soothes me\" or \"I love music because it soothes me\"? The second. No semicolon before \"because\"."},"new":{"title":"Spelling, plurals and punctuation","body":"SPELLING, PLURALS AND PUNCTUATION — WRITING WORDS AND SENTENCES ACCURATELY\n\nINTRODUCTION\nCorrect spelling makes a word recognisable; correct plural formation shows number; punctuation helps readers see where ideas begin, end and connect. JAMB may test all three through missing words, sentence correction and selecting the correctly written form. Memorise difficult patterns, but also learn the rule and why a tempting alternative is wrong.\n\n1. COMMON SPELLING DIFFICULTIES\nSome English words have repeated consonants or letters that are easy to omit. \"Accommodate\" has double c and double m; \"embarrass\" has double r and double s; \"necessary\" has one c and double s; \"occasion\" has double c and one s. Study words in meaningful sentences, then practise writing them without looking. Correct pronunciation does not always reveal spelling.\n\nExample (i)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. accomodate B. accommodate C. acommodate D. accommoddate\nSolution: 'Accommodate' contains two c's and two m's. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ii)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. neccessary B. necesary C. necessary D. necessery\nSolution: 'Necessary' has one c and two s's. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (iii)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. embarass B. embarrass C. embarras D. embaress\nSolution: 'Embarrass' has double r and double s. Correct answer: B.\n\n2. SILENT LETTERS AND CONFUSABLE WORDS\nEnglish spelling does not always follow sound. The k in \"know\" and \"knife\" is silent; the b in \"debt\" and \"doubt\" is silent; the h in \"honest\" is silent. Other words sound alike but differ in spelling and meaning: \"their\" (belonging to them), \"there\" (place), \"they're\" (they are); \"your\" (belonging to you) and \"you're\" (you are); \"principal\" (main/head) and \"principle\" (rule). The sentence determines the choice.\n\nExample (iv)\n___ going to submit the forms tomorrow.\nA. Their B. There C. They're D. Theirs\nSolution: 'They're' means 'they are', which fits before 'going'. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (v)\nThe school ___ addressed the pupils.\nA. principle B. principal C. principel D. principall\nSolution: 'Principal' means the head of a school; 'principle' means a rule or belief. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (vi)\nPlease put the books over ___.\nA. their B. they're C. there D. theirs\nSolution: 'There' refers to a place. Correct answer: C.\n\n3. SPELLING RULES — WHEN TO KEEP, DROP OR CHANGE LETTERS\nEnglish spelling has patterns that make unfamiliar words easier to spell. A spelling rule is a useful guide, not a promise that every word follows it. Learn the common pattern, practise examples, then remember the exceptions. In a JAMB question, first identify the base word and the ending being added. This helps you choose between similar-looking options.\n\n3A. DROP FINAL SILENT -E BEFORE A VOWEL-STARTING SUFFIX\nWhen a word ends in a silent e, we often drop that e before adding a suffix beginning with a vowel, especially -ing, -able or -ous: make → making; write → writing; hope → hoping; fame → famous. We usually keep the e before a consonant-starting suffix: hope → hopeful; care → careless; safe → safely. Some words keep e to preserve pronunciation or avoid confusion: change → changeable; notice → noticeable; dye → dyeing (to distinguish it from dying).\n\nExample (vii)\nChoose the correct spelling for \"write\" + \"-ing\".\nA. writeing B. writing C. writting D. writeng\nSolution: \"Write\" ends in silent e. Drop e before -ing: writing. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (viii)\nChoose the correct spelling for \"hope\" + \"-ful\".\nA. hopful B. hopeful C. hopefull D. hopeeful\nSolution: -ful begins with a consonant, so keep the final e: hopeful. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (ix)\nWhich is correctly spelt?\nA. changable B. changeable C. changeible D. changgeable\nSolution: \"Changeable\" keeps the e after g so the soft /dʒ/ sound is preserved. This is an important exception to the usual dropping rule. Correct answer: B.\n\n3B. DOUBLE THE FINAL CONSONANT WHEN THE PATTERN REQUIRES IT\nIn many one-syllable words ending consonant–vowel–consonant (CVC), double the final consonant before a vowel-starting suffix: stop → stopping; plan → planned; big → bigger; run → running. Do not double final w, x or y: snow → snowing; fix → fixing; play → playing. Do not double when two vowels come before the final consonant: rain → raining; need → needed. In longer words, stress matters: begin → beginning and prefer → preferred (last syllable stressed), but visit → visiting and open → opening (last syllable unstressed). British English often doubles final l in travelling and cancelled, even when the last syllable is unstressed; American English often writes traveling and canceled.\n\nExample (x)\nThe workers are ___ the road.\nA. repairring B. repairing C. repaireing D. repairringg\nSolution: \"Repair\" ends in two vowel letters before r and does not follow the short CVC doubling pattern here. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xi)\nChoose the correct form of \"stop\" + \"-ed\".\nA. stoped B. stopped C. stoppped D. stopeed\nSolution: Stop is a one-syllable CVC word. Double p before -ed: stopped. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xii)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. begining B. beginning C. beggining D. begininng\nSolution: In \"begin\", the final syllable is stressed and ends with a single consonant after a short vowel. Double n: beginning. Correct answer: B.\n\n3C. CHANGE CONSONANT + Y TO I BEFORE MANY SUFFIXES\nWhen a consonant comes immediately before final y, change y to i before many suffixes: happy → happier/happiest; carry → carried; easy → easily; beauty → beautiful. Keep y when a vowel comes before it: play → played; enjoy → enjoyment. Usually keep y before -ing: carry → carrying; study → studying. Also remember special spellings such as pay → paid and say → said.\n\nExample (xiii)\nChoose the correct form of \"carry\" + \"-ed\".\nA. carryed B. carried C. carriied D. carred\nSolution: A consonant (r) comes before y, so change y to i before -ed: carried. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xiv)\nChoose the correct form of \"study\" + \"-ing\".\nA. studing B. studiing C. studying D. studyying\nSolution: Keep the y before -ing: studying. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xv)\nWhich is correctly spelt?\nA. beautifull B. beautiful C. beautyful D. beautifol\nSolution: Beauty changes its final y to i before -ful: beautiful. The suffix -ful has only one l. Correct answer: B.\n\n3D. FORMING ADVERBS WITH -LY\nMany adverbs are formed by adding -ly: quick → quickly; slow → slowly. When an adjective ends in consonant + y, change y to i: happy → happily; easy → easily. When it ends in -le, many words drop e and add -y: gentle → gently; simple → simply. When it ends in -ic, many take -ally: basic → basically; dramatic → dramatically, but public → publicly. Some adverbs do not end in -ly: fast, hard, late, well. \"Hardly\" means almost not, so \"work hard\" and \"hardly work\" have very different meanings.\n\nExample (xvi)\n\"She answered the question ___.\"\nA. happyly B. happily C. happilly D. happy\nSolution: \"Happy\" changes consonant + y to i before -ly: happily. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xvii)\n\"Please speak ___.\"\nA. gentlely B. gentley C. gently D. gentilly\nSolution: \"Gentle\" changes to \"gently\": drop the final e and add y. Correct answer: C.\n\n3E. ADDING PREFIXES WITHOUT CHANGING THE BASE WORD\nA prefix is added to the beginning of a word. Common prefixes include un- (unhappy), dis- (disagree), re- (rewrite), mis- (misunderstand), im- (impossible) and in- (incomplete). The base spelling is often preserved: necessary → unnecessary; appear → disappear; write → rewrite. The negative prefix changes form according to the following sound: illegal, irregular, impossible, immature. These are conventional spellings, not forms invented freely.\n\nExample (xviii)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. unecessary B. unnecessary C. unnecessery D. unneccessary\nSolution: Add un- to the correctly spelt \"necessary\" (one c, double s): unnecessary. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xix)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. inpossible B. impossible C. ilpossible D. irpossible\nSolution: The conventional negative form before p is im-: impossible. Correct answer: B.\n\n3F. -IE- AND -EI-: USE THE PATTERN, BUT CHECK EXCEPTIONS\nA common memory aid says \"i before e except after c\", as in believe, field, receive and ceiling. However, it is not a dependable rule for all English words. Common exceptions include weird, seize, either, neither, science, ancient and efficient. Instead of trusting the rhyme blindly, learn word families and practise words that JAMB may test. For example, receive/receipt/reception are related but their spelling patterns differ.\n\nExample (xx)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. recieve B. receive C. receeve D. recive\nSolution: \"Receive\" has ei after c: r-e-c-e-i-v-e. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxi)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. wierd B. weird C. weerd D. wiard\nSolution: \"Weird\" is a common exception to the memory aid; it is spelt with ei. Correct answer: B.\n\n3G. -ABLE AND -IBLE ENDINGS\nBoth -able and -ible often form adjectives meaning \"can be\" or \"fit to be\". Many words with recognisable base verbs take -able: wash → washable; read → readable; accept → acceptable. Other words use -ible: possible, visible, responsible, flexible. Do not assume every word can be formed by adding -able to its apparent base. Learn common exceptions such as accessible and sensible. For some words, spelling changes before -able: rely → reliable; change → changeable.\n\nExample (xxii)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. readible B. readable C. readeable D. readabble\nSolution: The verb \"read\" takes -able: readable. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxiii)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. responsable B. responsible C. responseable D. responsibble\nSolution: The standard adjective is \"responsible\", ending in -ible. Correct answer: B.\n\n3H. HOW TO APPLY SPELLING RULES IN JAMB QUESTIONS\nFirst, identify the root word and any prefix or suffix. Next, ask whether the word ends in silent e, consonant + y, or a short CVC pattern. Then check stress if the word has several syllables. Finally, check for known exceptions and read the word in its sentence. Do not rely on pronunciation alone: \"necessary\", \"separate\" and \"definitely\" contain letters that learners often miss. A useful study habit is to keep a small list of words you personally misspell, write each in a sentence, and revisit it after a day and again after a week.\n\nExample (xxiv)\nChoose the correct spelling of the adverb from \"definite\".\nA. definately B. definitely C. definitly D. definatly\nSolution: \"Definitely\" comes from \"definite\" + -ly. The spelling retains the i in the middle: d-e-f-i-n-i-t-e-l-y. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxv)\nChoose the correct spelling.\nA. seperate B. separate C. separete D. seperrate\nSolution: The correct word is \"separate\" with a after the first r. Its related word \"separation\" can help you remember the spelling. Correct answer: B.\n\n4. REGULAR PLURALS: -S, -ES AND -IES\nMost countable nouns add -s: book/books. Words ending in s, sh, ch, x and often z add -es: bus/buses, brush/brushes, box/boxes. When a consonant comes before final y, change y to -ies: city/cities, baby/babies. When a vowel comes before y, simply add -s: boy/boys, key/keys. Do not turn \"boy\" into \"boies\".\n\nExample (xxvi)\nThe plural of 'city' is\nA. citys B. cities C. citis D. cityes\nSolution: Consonant + y changes to -ies. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxvii)\nThe plural of 'boy' is\nA. boies B. boyes C. boys D. boiis\nSolution: Vowel + y keeps the y and adds -s. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xxviii)\nThe plural of 'box' is\nA. boxs B. boxies C. boxes D. boxen\nSolution: Final x commonly takes -es. Correct answer: C.\n\n5. IRREGULAR PLURALS AND WORDS ENDING IN -F OR -O\nSome plurals change the internal vowel: man/men, woman/women, foot/feet, tooth/teeth, mouse/mice. Some remain unchanged: sheep/sheep, deer/deer. Some words ending in -f or -fe change to -ves: leaf/leaves, knife/knives; others simply add -s: roof/roofs, chief/chiefs. Words ending in -o vary: potato/potatoes, tomato/tomatoes, photo/photos, piano/pianos. Learn exceptions individually.\n\nExample (xxix)\nThe plural of 'knife' is\nA. knifes B. knives C. knivies D. knifees\nSolution: 'Knife' changes -fe to -ves. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxx)\nThe plural of 'roof' is\nA. rooves B. roofes C. roofs D. rofs\nSolution: 'Roof' normally adds -s; it is an exception to the common -ves pattern. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xxxi)\nThe plural of 'mouse' is\nA. mouses B. mice C. mices D. mousees\nSolution: 'Mouse' has the irregular plural 'mice'. Correct answer: B.\n\n6. CLASSICAL PLURALS AND UNCOUNTABLE NOUNS\nSome words of Greek or Latin origin retain classical plurals in formal English: criterion/criteria, phenomenon/phenomena, analysis/analyses, basis/bases, thesis/theses, nucleus/nuclei. \"Data\" historically is the plural of \"datum\"; modern usage sometimes treats \"data\" as a mass noun, but many formal examination items test \"datum/data\". Uncountable nouns such as information, advice, equipment and furniture do not normally take ordinary plural -s. Say \"pieces of advice\", not \"advices\".\n\nExample (xxxii)\nSeveral ___ were used to judge the entries.\nA. criterias B. criterion C. criteria D. criterions\nSolution: 'Criteria' is the standard plural of 'criterion'. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xxxiii)\nThe scientists observed two unusual ___.\nA. phenomenons B. phenomena C. phenomenon D. phenomenas\nSolution: 'Phenomena' is the plural of 'phenomenon'. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxxiv)\nThe teacher gave us useful ___.\nA. advices B. advice C. an advice D. many advice\nSolution: 'Advice' is uncountable; use 'advice' or 'pieces of advice'. Correct answer: B.\n\n7. COMPOUND NOUNS AND POSSESSIVE PLURALS\nA compound noun may pluralise its main noun: mother-in-law/mothers-in-law, passer-by/passers-by. Some compounds pluralise the last element: toothbrush/toothbrushes. In \"attorneys general\", \"attorneys\" is pluralised because it is the head noun. Distinguish ordinary plural forms from possession: \"teachers\" means more than one teacher; \"teachers' books\" means books belonging to multiple teachers.\n\nExample (xxxv)\nThe plural of 'mother-in-law' is\nA. mother-in-laws B. mothers-in-law C. mothers-in-laws D. mother-ins-law\nSolution: The main noun 'mother' becomes plural. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xxxvi)\nThe plural of 'passer-by' is\nA. passers-by B. passer-bys C. passers-bys D. passer-by's\nSolution: The head noun 'passer' becomes plural; an apostrophe is not used. Correct answer: A.\n\n8. APOSTROPHES: POSSESSION VERSUS CONTRACTION\nFor one owner, add 's: \"the girl's bag\". For regular plurals already ending in s, add only the apostrophe: \"the girls' bags\". For irregular plurals not ending in s, add 's: \"the children's books\", \"the men's room\". Apostrophes also mark omitted letters in contractions: \"don't\" = do not; \"it's\" = it is/it has. Do not put an apostrophe into an ordinary plural: \"three apple's\" is wrong.\n\nExample (xxxvii)\nThe books belong to several teachers. Choose the correct phrase.\nA. teacher's books B. teachers books C. teachers' books D. teachers's books\nSolution: Plural 'teachers' ends in s; the possessive apostrophe follows the s. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xxxviii)\nThe toys belong to the children. Choose the correct phrase.\nA. childrens toys B. childrens' toys C. children's toys D. children toys'\nSolution: 'Children' is an irregular plural without final s; add 's for possession. Correct answer: C.\n\nExample (xxxix)\n___ raining heavily.\nA. Its B. It's C. Its' D. It\nSolution: 'It's' is the contraction of 'it is'. Correct answer: B.\n\n9. COMMAS: LISTS, OPENINGS AND EXTRA INFORMATION\nCommas separate items in a list: \"We bought rice, beans and oil.\" They often follow an introductory phrase: \"After the lesson, the pupils revised.\" They can set off nonessential information: \"My uncle, who lives in Abuja, is visiting.\" Removing that clause leaves the main statement intact. Do not separate a subject from its verb with an unnecessary comma: \"The students, are ready\" is incorrect.\n\nExample (xl)\nChoose the correctly punctuated sentence.\nA. After the test the pupils, left. B. After the test, the pupils left. C. After, the test the pupils left. D. After the test the, pupils left.\nSolution: The introductory phrase is followed by a comma; the subject and verb stay together. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xli)\nChoose the correct sentence.\nA. My sister who lives in Kano, is visiting. B. My sister, who lives in Kano, is visiting. C. My sister who, lives in Kano is visiting. D. My sister, who lives in Kano is, visiting.\nSolution: The extra relative clause is enclosed by commas. Correct answer: B.\n\n10. SEMICOLONS AND COLONS\nA semicolon can join two closely related independent clauses: \"The road was flooded; the buses stopped.\" Each side could stand alone as a sentence. With a linking adverb such as \"however\" or \"therefore\", use a semicolon before it and a comma after it when joining two independent clauses: \"She studied hard; however, she was nervous.\" A colon can introduce a list or explanation after a complete clause: \"Bring these items: a pen, a pencil and an eraser.\" Do not place a semicolon between a dependent clause and the main clause: \"Because it rained; we stayed home\" is wrong.\n\nExample (xlii)\nChoose the correct punctuation.\nA. The road was flooded; the buses stopped. B. The road was flooded; because the buses stopped. C. The road; was flooded the buses stopped. D. The road was flooded, the buses stopped.\nSolution: Two complete closely related clauses can be joined with a semicolon. Correct answer: A.\n\nExample (xliii)\nChoose the correct sentence.\nA. She passed; however she was unhappy. B. She passed; however, she was unhappy. C. She passed however; she was unhappy. D. She passed; but she was unhappy.\nSolution: The linking adverb 'however' is introduced by a semicolon and followed by a comma. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xliv)\nChoose the correct sentence.\nA. Bring: a pen and a ruler. B. Bring these items: a pen and a ruler. C. Bring these: items a pen and a ruler. D. Bring these items; a pen and a ruler.\nSolution: A complete introductory clause precedes the colon introducing the list. Correct answer: B.\n\n11. FULL STOPS, QUESTION MARKS, EXCLAMATION MARKS AND QUOTATION MARKS\nA full stop ends a statement. A question mark ends a direct question: \"Where is the library?\" An indirect question inside a statement normally ends with a full stop: \"She asked where the library was.\" An exclamation mark conveys strong emotion or a forceful command; do not use it after every ordinary statement. Quotation marks enclose direct speech: \"Ada said, 'I am ready.'\" The exact placement of commas and full stops with quotation marks varies between British and American conventions, so follow the consistent style of the examination sentence.\n\nExample (xlv)\nChoose the correctly punctuated sentence.\nA. She asked where was the office? B. She asked where the office was. C. She asked, where the office was? D. She asked where, the office was.\nSolution: An indirect question uses statement order and ends as a statement. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xlvi)\nWhich sentence is a direct question?\nA. He asked where I lived. B. I wonder where she lives. C. Where does she live? D. She knows where I live.\nSolution: A direct question has question form and a question mark. Correct answer: C.\n\n12. HYPHENS, CAPITAL LETTERS AND ABBREVIATIONS\nA hyphen may join words functioning together before a noun: \"a well-known author\", \"a five-year plan\". Compare \"The author is well known\" where a hyphen is often unnecessary. Capital letters begin sentences and proper nouns: Nigeria, Friday, November, River Niger. Common nouns such as \"teacher\" and \"school\" are not automatically capitalised. Abbreviations follow conventional forms; avoid inserting apostrophes into ordinary acronym plurals (\"two NGOs\", not \"two NGO's\").\n\nExample (xlvii)\nChoose the natural phrase.\nA. a five years plan B. a five-year plan C. a five-years plan D. a five year plan's\nSolution: A number + singular noun used as a compound adjective before 'plan' is commonly hyphenated. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (xlviii)\nChoose the correctly capitalised sentence.\nA. We visited lagos on monday. B. We visited Lagos on Monday. C. We visited Lagos on monday. D. We visited lagos on Monday.\nSolution: Proper place names and days of the week take capitals. Correct answer: B.\n\n13. HOW TO ANSWER AND COMMON EXAM TRAPS\nFor spelling, look at repeated consonants and known word families. For plurals, identify the head noun, then decide whether the word is regular, irregular, classical or uncountable. For punctuation, first locate complete clauses: a semicolon joins independent clauses, while a comma alone may create a comma splice. Check whether an apostrophe marks possession or omitted letters. Never choose punctuation solely because it looks formal.\n\nExample (xlix)\nChoose the correct sentence.\nA. I enjoy music; because it relaxes me. B. I enjoy music because it relaxes me. C. I enjoy music,; because it relaxes me. D. I enjoy; music because it relaxes me.\nSolution: 'Because it relaxes me' is a dependent reason clause and should not be separated by a semicolon. Correct answer: B.\n\nExample (l)\nWhich is correctly written?\nA. three criterias B. three criteria C. three criterion D. three criterions's\nSolution: 'Criteria' is already plural; do not add -s or an apostrophe. Correct answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) Spell the word with double c and double m meaning 'provide room for'. Answer: accommodate.\n(ii) Give the plural of 'criterion'. Answer: criteria.\n(iii) Write the possessive for books belonging to several pupils. Answer: pupils' books.\n(iv) Join two related complete clauses with appropriate punctuation. Answer: a semicolon may be used.\n(v) Is \"I love music; because it helps me\" correct? No; remove the semicolon.\n\nEXAM CONNECTION\nPractise the linked JAMB questions below. For every choice, explain the spelling, plural or punctuation rule rather than guessing from appearance."}},{"id":"clauses","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Sentences, clauses and parts of speech","body":"Sentence types:\n• Simple: one main clause.\n• Compound: two main clauses joined by and, but or or.\n• Complex: one main clause plus at least one dependent clause (\"The woman WHO LIVES NEXT DOOR has a dog\").\n\nRelative clauses:\n• Defining: no commas, and it identifies the noun (\"the bridge that the Romans built\").\n• Non-defining: between commas, and it adds extra information (\"The bridge, which was built by the Romans, is still standing\").\n\nParts of speech depend on the job a word does in the sentence:\n• \"wounded soldier\": an adjective (describes a noun)\n• \"The bullet wounded him\": a verb\n• REFuse (rubbish) is a noun; reFUSE (say no) is a verb\n• In \"It was he who stole it\", \"it\" is a dummy (expletive) subject that adds emphasis.\n\nQuick check: In \"The wounded soldier was carried away\", what is \"wounded\"? An adjective, because it describes \"soldier\"."},"new":{"title":"Sentences, clauses and parts of speech","body":"SENTENCES, CLAUSES AND PARTS OF SPEECH\n\nINTRODUCTION\nGrammar builds from words to phrases, clauses and complete sentences. Identify the function of each word in context.\n\n\n1. FOUNDATIONS: PARTS OF SPEECH\nA noun names a person, place, thing or idea. A pronoun replaces a noun. A verb expresses action or state. An adjective modifies a noun; an adverb modifies a verb, adjective or adverb. Prepositions express relationships, conjunctions connect elements, and interjections express feeling. A word's role depends on its sentence: 'wounded' describes a soldier but is a verb in 'The bullet wounded him'.\n\nExample (i)\nQuestion: The wounded soldier rested.\nSolution: adjective. It describes soldier.\n\nExample (ii)\nQuestion: The bullet wounded him.\nSolution: verb. It expresses the bullet's action.\n\nExample (iii)\nQuestion: She spoke very softly: very.\nSolution: adverb. It modifies softly.\n\n\n2. PHRASES AND THEIR FUNCTIONS\nA phrase is a group of words functioning as a unit without forming a complete independent clause. A noun phrase can act as subject or object ('the old school building'); a prepositional phrase can locate or describe ('under the bridge'); a verb phrase contains auxiliaries and the main verb ('has been studying'). Do not confuse a phrase with a dependent clause containing a subject and finite verb.\n\nExample (iv)\nQuestion: The boy with a red bag smiled: with a red bag.\nSolution: prepositional phrase. It begins with with and describes boy.\n\nExample (v)\nQuestion: They have been waiting: have been waiting.\nSolution: verb phrase. Auxiliaries combine with the main verb.\n\nExample (vi)\nQuestion: The new library opened: the new library.\nSolution: noun phrase. It functions as subject.\n\n\n3. MAIN AND SUBORDINATE CLAUSES\nA clause normally contains a subject and a verb. A main clause can stand alone: 'The pupils left.' A subordinate clause depends on a main clause: 'because the bell rang'. Join them to express a complete idea: 'The pupils left because the bell rang.' A subordinate clause may come first, followed by a comma. Identify the finite verb and the subject in each clause.\n\nExample (vii)\nQuestion: We left because the rain began: dependent clause.\nSolution: because the rain began. It gives the reason and cannot stand alone.\n\nExample (viii)\nQuestion: Which is a complete clause: although it rained / the match ended?\nSolution: the match ended. It stands alone as a complete statement.\n\nExample (ix)\nQuestion: Although he was tired, he continued: main clause.\nSolution: he continued. It can stand independently.\n\n\n4. SIMPLE, COMPOUND, COMPLEX AND COMPOUND-COMPLEX SENTENCES\nA simple sentence has one independent clause, even with a compound subject or predicate: 'Ada and Musa sang and danced.' A compound sentence joins two independent clauses: 'Ada sang, and Musa danced.' A complex sentence joins an independent and dependent clause: 'Ada sang because Musa asked.' A compound-complex sentence has at least two independent clauses and one dependent clause. Count clauses, not commas or total words.\n\nExample (x)\nQuestion: Ada and Musa sang and danced: sentence type.\nSolution: simple. There is only one independent clause.\n\nExample (xi)\nQuestion: Ada sang but Musa danced: sentence type.\nSolution: compound. Two independent clauses are joined by but.\n\nExample (xii)\nQuestion: When it rained, we stayed home: sentence type.\nSolution: complex. A dependent time clause joins a main clause.\n\nExample (xiii)\nQuestion: When it rained, we stayed home, and the pupils revised: sentence type.\nSolution: compound-complex. Two main clauses and one dependent clause.\n\n\n5. NOUN, ADJECTIVE AND ADVERB CLAUSES\nA noun clause performs a noun's role: 'What he said surprised us' (subject). An adjective or relative clause describes a noun: 'The girl who won is here.' An adverb clause adds time, cause, condition, contrast or purpose: 'We waited until the rain stopped.' Determine the role of the whole clause, not just its opening word.\n\nExample (xiv)\nQuestion: What he said surprised us: What he said.\nSolution: noun clause. It is the subject of surprised.\n\nExample (xv)\nQuestion: The woman who called is my aunt: who called.\nSolution: adjective clause. It identifies the woman.\n\nExample (xvi)\nQuestion: We left when the bell rang: when the bell rang.\nSolution: adverb clause. It tells when we left.\n\n\n6. DEFINING AND NON-DEFINING RELATIVE CLAUSES\nA defining relative clause identifies the noun: 'The book that you borrowed is mine.' It normally has no commas. A non-defining clause supplies extra information: 'My brother, who lives in Kano, is visiting.' Commas mark the extra information; who or which is usual, not that. Removing a non-defining clause leaves the noun's identity intact.\n\nExample (xvii)\nQuestion: The book that I bought is new: clause type.\nSolution: defining. It identifies which book.\n\nExample (xviii)\nQuestion: My aunt, who is a doctor, arrived: clause type.\nSolution: non-defining. The clause adds information about an identified aunt.\n\nExample (xix)\nQuestion: My bicycle, which is blue, is outside: which/that?\nSolution: which. A non-defining clause normally uses which.\n\n\n7. RELATIVE PRONOUNS AND ANTECEDENTS\nWho is commonly used for people as subjects, whom for people as objects in formal English, whose for possession, and which for things. That often introduces defining clauses. An object relative pronoun may be omitted: 'The book I borrowed'; a subject relative pronoun cannot ordinarily be omitted without changing the structure: 'The man who called'.\n\nExample (xx)\nQuestion: The girl ___ won the prize.\nSolution: who. The pronoun is the subject of won.\n\nExample (xxi)\nQuestion: The pupil ___ bag was lost.\nSolution: whose. It expresses possession.\n\nExample (xxii)\nQuestion: The book I borrowed: omitted relative.\nSolution: that. The object relative can be omitted.\n\n\n8. ACTIVE AND PASSIVE VOICE\nIn active voice, the subject acts: 'The guard opened the gate.' In passive voice, the subject receives the action: 'The gate was opened by the guard.' Use an appropriate form of be plus a past participle, and preserve tense: 'has repaired' becomes 'has been repaired'. An intransitive verb such as sleep in 'The child slept' has no direct object for a normal passive.\n\nExample (xxiii)\nQuestion: The boy broke the window: passive.\nSolution: The window was broken by the boy. Was plus broken preserves simple past.\n\nExample (xxiv)\nQuestion: The letter has been written: voice.\nSolution: passive. Has been plus participle marks present perfect passive.\n\nExample (xxv)\nQuestion: The baby slept: ordinary passive possible?\nSolution: no. The verb has no direct object here.\n\n\n9. FINITE VERBS, INFINITIVES, GERUNDS AND PARTICIPLES\nFinite verbs carry tense or subject agreement: 'She walks'; 'They walked'. Non-finite forms include infinitives ('to walk'), -ing participles ('walking') and past participles ('written'). A gerund is an -ing form functioning like a noun: 'Swimming is healthy.' A participle can modify a noun: 'The crying child'. Do not assume every -ing word is the main verb.\n\nExample (xxvi)\nQuestion: Swimming is enjoyable: swimming.\nSolution: gerund. It functions as a noun subject.\n\nExample (xxvii)\nQuestion: The crying child slept: crying.\nSolution: participle used adjectivally. It describes child.\n\nExample (xxviii)\nQuestion: She wants to travel: to travel.\nSolution: infinitive. It is a non-finite complement.\n\n\n10. FRAGMENTS, RUN-ONS AND PARALLELISM\nA fragment lacks a complete main clause: 'Because the road flooded.' A run-on joins complete clauses without correct punctuation: 'The bell rang the pupils left.' Repair with a full stop, semicolon or coordinating conjunction. Parallelism keeps equal elements in matching forms: 'reading, swimming and dancing', not 'reading, to swim and dancing'.\n\nExample (xxix)\nQuestion: Because the school closed: sentence status.\nSolution: fragment. It has no main clause.\n\nExample (xxx)\nQuestion: The bell rang, and the pupils left: structure.\nSolution: correct compound sentence. The conjunction joins two independent clauses.\n\nExample (xxxi)\nQuestion: She enjoys reading, singing and dancing: principle.\nSolution: parallelism. The three activities have matching -ing forms.\n\n\n11. CLEFT SENTENCES AND DUMMY SUBJECTS\nA cleft sentence emphasises a particular element: 'It was Ada who solved the problem' emphasises Ada; 'It was yesterday that she arrived' emphasises time. The introductory it is a dummy or expletive subject, not a reference to an object. In 'There is a problem', there introduces existence rather than naming a place.\n\nExample (xxxii)\nQuestion: It was Ada who spoke: it.\nSolution: dummy subject. The cleft structure emphasises Ada.\n\nExample (xxxiii)\nQuestion: It was yesterday that he arrived: emphasis.\nSolution: yesterday. The time is placed in focus.\n\nExample (xxxiv)\nQuestion: There are two books: there.\nSolution: existential subject marker. It introduces the existence of books.\n\nHOW TO ANSWER AND COMMON EXAM TRAPS\nIdentify subjects and finite verbs first; then decide whether each clause stands alone. A long sentence can still be simple. A participle can act like an adjective, a gerund like a noun, and a relative clause can be defining or non-defining. Practise the linked JAMB questions by explaining the grammatical function.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) The wounded soldier rested. Answer: adjective.\n(ii) The boy with a red bag smiled: with a red bag. Answer: prepositional phrase.\n(iii) We left because the rain began: dependent clause. Answer: because the rain began.\n(iv) Ada and Musa sang and danced: sentence type. Answer: simple.\n(v) What he said surprised us: What he said. Answer: noun clause.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the mapped past questions and explain the rule before choosing."}},{"id":"figures","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Figures of speech and literary terms","body":"• Simile: a comparison using like or as (\"as brave as a lion\").\n• Metaphor: says one thing is another (\"Time is a thief\").\n• Mixed metaphor: two images that clash (\"lit the fire that would drown her plans\").\n• Personification: human qualities given to things (\"the wind whispered\").\n• Onomatopoeia: words that imitate sounds (babble, buzz, sizzle).\n• Hyperbole: deliberate exaggeration (\"I've told you a million times\").\n• Irony: the opposite of what is expected or said.\n• Paradox: seems contradictory but holds a truth (\"The more you learn, the less you know\").\n• Oxymoron: two opposite words together (\"bitter-sweet\").\n• Chiasmus: reversed word order, A-B then B-A (\"Ask not what your country can do for you; ask what you can do for your country\").\n• Anaphora: repeating the first words of lines.\n• Alliteration: repeating first sounds (\"Peter Piper picked\").\n• Tone: the writer's attitude to the subject (mocking, sad, admiring).\n\nCommon traps: confusing simile with metaphor, and paradox with oxymoron.\n\nQuick check: \"The brook babbled over the stones\": which figure? Onomatopoeia, because \"babbled\" imitates the sound."},"new":{"title":"Figures of speech and literary terms","body":"FIGURES OF SPEECH AND LITERARY TERMS\n\nINTRODUCTION\nFigures of speech make expression vivid through comparison, contrast, exaggeration and sound.\n\n\n1. LITERAL AND FIGURATIVE MEANING\nLiteral words express their ordinary meaning; figurative words suggest an additional comparison or interpretation. 'The room was cold' may be literal, while 'Her cold reply' suggests lack of sympathy. Always use the context to avoid labelling an ordinary description as a figure of speech.\n\nExample (i)\nQuestion: The room was cold: likely meaning.\nSolution: literal. It describes temperature.\n\nExample (ii)\nQuestion: Her cold reply: cold.\nSolution: unfriendly. The word figuratively describes attitude.\n\n\n2. SIMILE AND METAPHOR\nA simile explicitly compares unlike things, often using like or as: 'He ran like the wind.' A metaphor directly identifies one thing with another: 'Time is a thief.' Not every like signals a simile: 'I like rice' expresses preference. A sustained metaphor develops the same comparison through several sentences.\n\nExample (iii)\nQuestion: As brave as a lion.\nSolution: simile. As introduces an explicit comparison.\n\nExample (iv)\nQuestion: Time is a thief.\nSolution: metaphor. Time is figuratively equated with a thief.\n\nExample (v)\nQuestion: Her voice was music.\nSolution: metaphor. One thing is presented as another.\n\n\n3. PERSONIFICATION AND APOSTROPHE\nPersonification gives human qualities to non-human things: 'The wind whispered.' Apostrophe directly addresses an absent person, abstract idea or object: 'O Hope, do not leave me!' The literary term apostrophe differs from the punctuation mark used in 'Ada's book'.\n\nExample (vi)\nQuestion: The trees danced in the storm.\nSolution: personification. Trees are described as dancing.\n\nExample (vii)\nQuestion: O Death, where is your power?\nSolution: apostrophe. An abstract idea is directly addressed.\n\nExample (viii)\nQuestion: The sun smiled at us.\nSolution: personification. The sun receives a human action.\n\n\n4. HYPERBOLE, UNDERSTATEMENT AND LITOTES\nHyperbole deliberately exaggerates: 'I have told you a million times.' Understatement minimises an event: calling a severe flood 'a little rain'. Litotes expresses a positive idea through negating its opposite: 'not bad' suggests fairly good. Do not confuse every negative statement with litotes.\n\nExample (ix)\nQuestion: I waited a thousand years.\nSolution: hyperbole. Time is deliberately exaggerated.\n\nExample (x)\nQuestion: A devastating storm was a little inconvenience.\nSolution: understatement. The severity is minimised.\n\nExample (xi)\nQuestion: Not unreasonable.\nSolution: litotes. Negating the opposite conveys cautious approval.\n\n\n5. IRONY AND SARCASM\nVerbal irony contrasts intended meaning with literal words: saying 'How punctual!' to someone two hours late. Sarcasm is a sharper, often mocking form of verbal irony. Situational irony occurs when an outcome contrasts with expectations, such as a safety officer ignoring safety rules. A surprising event is not automatically ironic without an expectation contrast.\n\nExample (xii)\nQuestion: How punctual you are! said to a latecomer.\nSolution: verbal irony. The words mean the opposite of the criticism.\n\nExample (xiii)\nQuestion: A safety officer breaks safety rules.\nSolution: situational irony. The event contradicts the expected role.\n\nExample (xiv)\nQuestion: Mocking praise meant to hurt.\nSolution: sarcasm. The irony is biting.\n\n\n6. PARADOX AND OXYMORON\nA paradox is a statement that seems contradictory but may reveal a truth: 'The more I learn, the more I realise I do not know.' An oxymoron places opposing terms close together: 'deafening silence', 'bittersweet memory'. An oxymoron is often a short phrase; a paradox can be a whole proposition.\n\nExample (xv)\nQuestion: Deafening silence.\nSolution: oxymoron. Contradictory words appear together.\n\nExample (xvi)\nQuestion: The more he owned, the poorer he felt.\nSolution: paradox. A seemingly contradictory statement suggests a truth.\n\nExample (xvii)\nQuestion: Bittersweet memory.\nSolution: oxymoron. Contrasting feelings combine.\n\n\n7. ALLITERATION, ASSONANCE AND CONSONANCE\nAlliteration repeats initial consonant sounds: 'Bright birds beat their wings.' Assonance repeats vowel sounds: 'The slow road home' repeats /əʊ/. Consonance repeats consonant sounds, often later in words. Sound rather than spelling decides these devices: phone and fish begin with the same /f/ sound.\n\nExample (xviii)\nQuestion: Bright birds beat their wings.\nSolution: alliteration. Initial /b/ repeats.\n\nExample (xix)\nQuestion: The slow road home.\nSolution: assonance. The vowel /əʊ/ repeats.\n\nExample (xx)\nQuestion: Phone and fish share which device?\nSolution: alliteration. Both begin with /f/ despite different letters.\n\n\n8. ONOMATOPOEIA\nOnomatopoeia uses words that imitate or suggest sounds: buzz, hiss, splash, bang, crackle and tick-tock. 'The bees buzzed' uses a sound-imitating verb. Some sentences combine sound imitation with personification or alliteration; identify what the question specifically highlights.\n\nExample (xxi)\nQuestion: The bees buzzed.\nSolution: onomatopoeia. Buzz imitates a sound.\n\nExample (xxii)\nQuestion: The fire crackled.\nSolution: onomatopoeia. Crackled evokes burning sounds.\n\nExample (xxiii)\nQuestion: Splash! The stone entered the pool.\nSolution: onomatopoeia. Splash imitates the sound.\n\n\n9. METONYMY AND SYNECDOCHE\nMetonymy replaces a thing with a closely associated term: 'The crown announced' for the monarch. Synecdoche uses a part for a whole or a whole for a part: 'All hands on deck' for all sailors. These devices can overlap, but the relationship between the substitute and intended referent is the key.\n\nExample (xxiv)\nQuestion: The crown approved the law.\nSolution: metonymy. Crown represents monarchy.\n\nExample (xxv)\nQuestion: We need more hands.\nSolution: synecdoche. Hands represent whole workers.\n\nExample (xxvi)\nQuestion: The White House issued a statement.\nSolution: metonymy. A building represents its administration.\n\n\n10. EUPHEMISM, PUN AND ALLUSION\nEuphemism softens an unpleasant expression: 'passed away' for died. A pun plays on multiple meanings or similar sounds, often humorously. An allusion briefly refers to a known story, event or person: 'He met his Waterloo' refers to a decisive defeat. The meaning depends on shared background knowledge.\n\nExample (xxvii)\nQuestion: He passed away.\nSolution: euphemism. A gentler phrase replaces died.\n\nExample (xxviii)\nQuestion: A joke using two meanings of sole.\nSolution: pun. It exploits ambiguity.\n\nExample (xxix)\nQuestion: He met his Waterloo.\nSolution: allusion. It refers indirectly to a historic defeat.\n\n\n11. ANAPHORA, ANTITHESIS AND CHIASMUS\nAnaphora repeats words at the start of successive clauses: 'We will work; we will learn.' Antithesis balances contrasting ideas: 'Many are called, but few are chosen.' Chiasmus reverses the order of related terms or ideas: 'We shape our habits, and our habits shape us.' Look at the arrangement of ideas.\n\nExample (xxx)\nQuestion: We will work; we will learn.\nSolution: anaphora. The opening words repeat.\n\nExample (xxxi)\nQuestion: Many are called, few are chosen.\nSolution: antithesis. Contrasting ideas are balanced.\n\nExample (xxxii)\nQuestion: We shape habits; habits shape us.\nSolution: chiasmus. The subject-object relationship reverses.\n\n\n12. TONE, MOOD, IMAGERY AND SYMBOLISM\nTone is the writer's attitude: admiring, anxious, mocking or hopeful. Mood is the feeling produced in the reader: suspense, sadness or excitement. Imagery appeals to senses: 'Bitter smoke stung our eyes.' Symbolism gives an object a wider meaning: a broken chain can symbolise freedom. Context decides interpretation.\n\nExample (xxxiii)\nQuestion: The writer praises volunteers.\nSolution: admiring tone. Praise shows approval.\n\nExample (xxxiv)\nQuestion: Footsteps echoed in the dark hall.\nSolution: suspenseful mood. The details create tension.\n\nExample (xxxv)\nQuestion: A broken chain in a liberation story.\nSolution: freedom. The object symbolises release.\n\n\n13. MIXED METAPHOR AND OVERLAPPING DEVICES\nA mixed metaphor combines images that do not fit: 'We must steer this ship before the problem boils over.' One sentence can also contain several devices: 'The wind whispered wildly' includes personification and alliteration. Answer according to the particular feature highlighted, not a memorised label.\n\nExample (xxxvi)\nQuestion: Steer the ship before the problem boils over.\nSolution: mixed metaphor. Ship steering and boiling images clash.\n\nExample (xxxvii)\nQuestion: The wind whispered.\nSolution: personification. Wind is given human speech.\n\nExample (xxxviii)\nQuestion: Her smile was sunshine.\nSolution: metaphor. The smile is directly compared to sunshine.\n\nHOW TO ANSWER AND COMMON EXAM TRAPS\nDistinguish simile from metaphor, oxymoron from paradox, and personification from apostrophe. For sound devices listen to sounds rather than letters; for irony identify the contrast between words and intention or expectation. Practise the linked JAMB questions by naming the evidence for each figure.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) The room was cold: likely meaning. Answer: literal.\n(ii) As brave as a lion. Answer: simile.\n(iii) The trees danced in the storm. Answer: personification.\n(iv) I waited a thousand years. Answer: hyperbole.\n(v) How punctual you are! said to a latecomer. Answer: verbal irony.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the mapped past questions and explain the rule before choosing."}},{"id":"stress","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Word stress (which syllable is strong)","body":"In words of more than one syllable, one syllable is said louder and longer. JAMB follows British pronunciation.\n\nUseful patterns:\n1. -tion, -sion, -ic, -ical: stress the syllable just before. associAtion, eduCAtion, phoTOgraphy (but PHOtograph).\n2. -ee, -eer, -ette, -ean are usually stressed themselves: evacuEE, cigaRETTE, EuroPEan.\n3. Two-syllable nouns usually stress the first syllable (TAXi, SAlon, CLASSroom); two-syllable verbs usually stress the second (reSIST, conFIRM, comPEL).\n4. Noun/verb pairs switch stress: REfuse/reFUSE, REcord/reCORD, OBject/obJECT.\n5. Prefixes such as re-, con-, mis-, in- are rarely stressed (reproDUCE, misREAD).\n6. Learn the exceptions: adVERtisement (British), CApitalism, AGriculture, JuLY, adMINistrative.\n\nHow to answer \"different stress\" questions: say each word aloud, tapping the strong syllable, and note its position (1st, 2nd, 3rd). The odd position out is the answer.\n\nQuick check: Which is stressed on the first syllable: resist, intact, confirm or salon? Salon."},"new":{"title":"Word stress (which syllable is strong)","body":"WORD STRESS (WHICH SYLLABLE IS STRONG)\n\nINTRODUCTION\nWord stress concerns which spoken syllable is most prominent, not which letters look longest.\n\n\n1. SYLLABLES AND PRIMARY STRESS\nA syllable is a spoken beat organised around a vowel sound. 'School' has one; 'TAble' two; 'eduCAtion' four. Stress is the most prominent syllable, often clearer, longer and accompanied by pitch movement. Capitals in these examples mark stress for teaching; they are not ordinary spelling. Count spoken beats, not written vowels.\n\nExample (i)\nQuestion: Education: number of syllables.\nSolution: four. It is commonly divided e-du-ca-tion.\n\nExample (ii)\nQuestion: TAble: stressed syllable.\nSolution: first. TA is prominent.\n\nExample (iii)\nQuestion: beGIN: stressed syllable.\nSolution: second. GIN is prominent.\n\n\n2. UNSTRESSED VOWELS AND SCHWA\nAn unstressed syllable may reduce its vowel to schwa /ə/, as in the first syllable of 'aBOUT' in common British pronunciation. Stress affects vowel quality and speech rhythm, not merely loudness. A long word may have secondary stress, but odd-one-out JAMB questions usually compare the position of primary stress.\n\nExample (iv)\nQuestion: aBOUT: stressed syllable.\nSolution: second. BOUT is prominent.\n\nExample (v)\nQuestion: Schwa /ə/ is a?\nSolution: reduced vowel. It often occurs in unstressed syllables.\n\nExample (vi)\nQuestion: TAble versus aBOUT: which begins with primary stress?\nSolution: TAble. Its first syllable is stressed.\n\n\n3. TWO-SYLLABLE NOUNS AND ADJECTIVES\nMany two-syllable nouns and adjectives have first-syllable stress: TAble, DOCtor, HAPpy, CLEVer. This is a tendency, not a universal rule. Exceptions include hoTEL, JuLY and, in common British pronunciation, saLON. Avoid teaching a blanket rule that all nouns are stressed first.\n\nExample (vii)\nQuestion: Which is stressed first: TAble or hoTEL?\nSolution: TAble. The first syllable is prominent.\n\nExample (viii)\nQuestion: Which is stressed second: DOCtor or hoTEL?\nSolution: hoTEL. TEL carries stress.\n\nExample (ix)\nQuestion: HAPpy: stress position.\nSolution: first. HAP is prominent.\n\n\n4. TWO-SYLLABLE VERBS\nMany two-syllable verbs stress the second syllable: beGIN, conFIRM, reSIST, reLAX. But ANswer, OPE(n) and FOLlow are commonly first-stressed. The pattern is a clue, not a substitute for hearing the word. Check a British dictionary when uncertain.\n\nExample (x)\nQuestion: Which is stressed second: ANswer or conFIRM?\nSolution: conFIRM. FIRM is prominent.\n\nExample (xi)\nQuestion: Which is stressed first: beGIN or ANswer?\nSolution: ANswer. AN is prominent.\n\nExample (xii)\nQuestion: reSIST: stress position.\nSolution: second. SIST is prominent.\n\n\n5. NOUN–VERB STRESS SHIFTS\nSome words shift stress with grammatical role: REcord (noun) / reCORD (verb); PREsent (noun) / preSENT (verb); OBject (noun) / obJECT (verb); CONduct (noun) / conDUCT (verb). Read the sentence first to identify the intended part of speech. Not all noun–verb pairs shift stress.\n\nExample (xiii)\nQuestion: Please record the score: record.\nSolution: reCORD. The word is a verb.\n\nExample (xiv)\nQuestion: The record was broken: record.\nSolution: REcord. The word is a noun.\n\nExample (xv)\nQuestion: They object to the proposal: object.\nSolution: obJECT. The word is a verb.\n\nExample (xvi)\nQuestion: The object is heavy: object.\nSolution: OBject. The word is a noun.\n\n\n6. STRESS-ATTRACTING SUFFIXES\nEndings -ee, -eer, -ese and -ette commonly attract stress to the final syllable: employEE, enginEER, JapanESE, cigarETTE. Learn the actual spoken form rather than assuming all final letters create stress. These suffixes are helpful in exam comparisons.\n\nExample (xvii)\nQuestion: EmployEE: stress position.\nSolution: final. The -ee ending attracts stress.\n\nExample (xviii)\nQuestion: EnginEER: stress position.\nSolution: final. The -eer ending attracts stress.\n\nExample (xix)\nQuestion: JapanESE: stress position.\nSolution: final. The -ese ending attracts stress.\n\n\n7. STRESS BEFORE -TION, -SION AND -IC\nMany words ending in -tion and -sion stress the preceding syllable: eduCAtion, inforMAtion, deCIsion. The -ic ending often brings stress to the preceding syllable: draMAtic, geoGRAPHic. Related -ical forms often follow similar patterns, but check the word's pronunciation.\n\nExample (xx)\nQuestion: EduCAtion: stress position.\nSolution: third. CA precedes -tion.\n\nExample (xxi)\nQuestion: DraMAtic: stress position.\nSolution: second. MA precedes -ic.\n\nExample (xxii)\nQuestion: DeCIsion: stress position.\nSolution: second. CI precedes -sion.\n\n\n8. STRESS SHIFTS WITHIN WORD FAMILIES\nAdding suffixes can move stress: PHOtograph, phoTOGraphy, photoGRAPHic; EConomy, ecoNOmic. A learner who always stresses the same root syllable will make mistakes. Say the entire family and mark the strongest syllable in each word.\n\nExample (xxiii)\nQuestion: Photograph: stress.\nSolution: first. PHO is stressed.\n\nExample (xxiv)\nQuestion: Photography: stress.\nSolution: second. TOG is stressed.\n\nExample (xxv)\nQuestion: Photographic: stress.\nSolution: third. GRAPH is stressed.\n\n\n9. PREFIXES AND COMPOUND NOUNS\nPrefixes such as re-, mis- and dis- are often unstressed in verbs: reWRITE, misREAD, disAGREE. Compound nouns often stress the first element: BLACKboard, CLASSroom, GREENhouse. Stress can distinguish a GREENhouse (plant-growing building) from a green HOUSE (a house painted green). Exceptions exist.\n\nExample (xxvi)\nQuestion: Rewrite: main stress.\nSolution: WRITE. The prefix is not prominent.\n\nExample (xxvii)\nQuestion: BLACKboard: main stress.\nSolution: first element. Compound nouns often stress the first part.\n\nExample (xxviii)\nQuestion: GREENhouse versus green HOUSE: distinction.\nSolution: plant building versus coloured house. Stress changes meaning.\n\n\n10. BRITISH PRONUNCIATION AND EXCEPTIONS\nJAMB usually follows British pronunciation. Learn notable exceptions: adVERtisement commonly has second-syllable stress in British English; AGriculture has first-syllable stress; JuLY has second-syllable stress; hoTEL and saLON are commonly second-stressed. Dictionaries may list variants, so avoid treating one broad tendency as absolute.\n\nExample (xxix)\nQuestion: British advertisement: stress.\nSolution: VER. Second-syllable stress is common.\n\nExample (xxx)\nQuestion: JuLY: stress.\nSolution: second. LY is prominent.\n\nExample (xxxi)\nQuestion: AGriculture: stress.\nSolution: first. AG is prominent.\n\n\n11. ODD-ONE-OUT EXAMINATION TECHNIQUE\nDivide words into spoken syllables, say them naturally, identify the primary stress and record its position. Compare the positions: TAble, DOCtor, HAPpy and reLAX gives reLAX as the odd one out. For REcord/reCORD, read the grammatical context. Dictionary pronunciation guides mark primary stress with ˈ.\n\nExample (xxxii)\nQuestion: Odd one: TAble / DOCtor / reLAX / HAPpy.\nSolution: reLAX. Only it has second-syllable stress.\n\nExample (xxxiii)\nQuestion: Odd one: beGIN / conFIRM / reSIST / ANswer.\nSolution: ANswer. Only it has first-syllable stress.\n\nExample (xxxiv)\nQuestion: Odd one: employEE / enginEER / JapanESE / PHOtograph.\nSolution: PHOtograph. Only it has first-syllable stress.\n\nHOW TO ANSWER AND COMMON EXAM TRAPS\nDo not count written vowels as syllables. Many nouns are first-stressed and many verbs second-stressed, but exceptions matter. Check noun/verb pairs and stress-shifting word families. For JAMB odd-one-out questions, mark the primary stressed syllable in each word before choosing.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n(i) Education: number of syllables. Answer: four.\n(ii) aBOUT: stressed syllable. Answer: second.\n(iii) Which is stressed first: TAble or hoTEL? Answer: TAble.\n(iv) Which is stressed second: ANswer or conFIRM? Answer: conFIRM.\n(v) Please record the score: record. Answer: reCORD.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the mapped past questions and explain the rule before choosing."}}];
